        "pageDescription": "OpenStreetMap US hosted the 14th annual State of the Map US conference on June 11–13, 2026 in Madison, Wisconsin. Thank you to everyone who participated. Talks were recorded and will be available online soon.",
        "evidenceText": "State of the Map US 2026 | OpenStreetMap US State of the Map US 2026 OpenStreetMap US hosted the 14th annual State of the Map US conference on June 11–13, 2026 in Madison, Wisconsin. Thank you to everyone who participated. Talks were recorded and will be available online soon. Sponsors Recorded Talks The Mappiest Event of the Year State of the Map US is the largest gathering of OpenStreetMap community members from across the country. It’s three days of mappers, businesses, academics, government agencies, and nonprofits all collaborating around the free and editable map of the world. Whether you’re brand new to open mapping or you’re an OpenStreetMap veteran, this conference is for you. Narrative Map Competition Results In partnership with Esri, OpenStreetMap US hosted our very first SOTMUS Narrative Map Competition! Congratulations to this year’s winners: Best Overall: “ Cyclist Survival Map ” by Atlas Guo Best example of Mapping for Your Community: “ Find Your Place ” by James Piacentini Best example of Mapping for Mobility: “ Rail Globe ” by Gareth Baldrica-Franklin Best example of Open Data for Resiliency: “ Divided by Highways ” by Paige Kiarie Best example of Open Data for Healthier Ecosystems: “ Scales in the Canals ” by Hareshram Sivaraman Talks Welcome to State of the Map US 2026 Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Maggie Cawley Kick off your State of the Map US with welcoming remarks from OSM US Executive Director Maggie Cawley. Welcome to Wisconsin Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Howard Veregin Wisconsin State Cartographer Howard Veregin welcomes State of the Map US to Madison. An Update from your Hosts, OpenStreetMap US Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Alyssa Castronuovo , Jake Low , and Maggie Cawley What’s cooking at OpenStreetMap US? Find out the latest happenings from the OSM US Staff - from program updates to tech and tools! From Sidewalk to Signal: Mapping Internet Access with Street-Level Imagery Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Felipe Valdez What if communities could map their own neighborhoods and control their digital futures? Philly Community Wireless and Temple Libraries combine Mapillary street-level imagery with OpenStreetMap to map infrastructure gaps and community assets.... Madison in 50 Maps: A Cartographic Tour through OSM Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Stephen Kennedy Take a visual tour of Madison through the lens of OpenStreetMap! Based on the #30DayMapChallenge and a forthcoming book, this session highlights several maps of our host city—discussing at least 3 or... Cartography on Autopilot: Turning GTFS and OSM into Beautiful Maps Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Kapeel Daryani MTC is creating an automated map production system that blends OSM with GTFS data and uses QGIS plugins to produce uniform, print ready transit maps. The workflow processes stops and routes from... TeenMaptivists: Open Mapping in High School Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Steven Johnson A thumbnail overview of the TeenMaptivist project: training the next generation of OpenStreetMappers. A Decade of Mapping Impact: Celebrating 10 Years of YouthMappers Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Chad Blevins In 2025, YouthMappers officially celebrates 10 years of empowering the next generation of open-source cartographers. What started as a vision to connect university students with meaningful geospatial projects has grown into a... State of the OSM cartography landscape Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Alan McConchie and Kelsey Taylor Creating a basemap using OSM data is easier than ever, but to design one from scratch, or to make substantial modifications to an existing basemap, requires a hodgepodge of different tools to... OSM US Tech Updates Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Jake Low OSM US maintains a number of useful tools in the OpenStreetMap ecosystem. Learn what’s new in MapRoulette, OSMCha, Layercake, the OSM US Tileservice, and more. Why Ground-Truthing is Important Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Rob Savoye This is a short talk about why ground-truthing is important. Incorrect or lacking tags often leads to navigation mistakes which would be better to not experience… In addition, in many small rural... PWG 1.0: Creating the Pedestrian Working Group Schema & Guide Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Jacob Hall and Amy Bordenave In this 15-minute presentation, OSM US Pedestrian Working Group participants Amy Bordenave and Jacob Hall will announce the 1.0 releases of the PWG Schema and PWG Guide, share our Working Group’s process... How Amazon Generates Map Tiles for Last-Mile Delivery Using OSM-Derived Datasets and Planetiler Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Vishal Kumar and Shaishav Maisuria Amazon Last Mile generates 22+ custom map tile layers using OSM-derived datasets like Overture Maps, Natural Earth, and lake centerlines. We re-architected our legacy PostGIS based pipeline, introducing Planetiler engine with GeoParquet... OSM-lish: OpenStreetMap's Own English Dialect Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Clay Smalley While OSM may have begun as a British project, the language we tag with is anything but. Come explore the evolution of OSM tagging since its 2004 inception, and the mishmash of... Review of American Roadways (ROAR) Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Matthew Whilden We have 10.5 million sections of roadway left to review from the original TIGER imports. Fortunately, we have more data sources available than ever before. What tools can we create to help... Dhanyasri's take on megacities climate resilience Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Dhanyasri Bolla As cities face increasing climate risks, researchers are racing to model resilience in megacities. While analyzing adaptation strategies across 43 megacities, I found that standard data often lacks the critical details on... Using OpenStreetMap data to assess the bikeability of schools Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Harald Kliems , Ben Varick , and Samuel Younkin Biking to school can have many health, environmental, and social benefits, but few students in the United States bike to school. In a community-engaged research partnership between UW-Madison, the Wisconsin Bike Fed,... Integrating OSM User Authentication into OldInsuranceMaps.net Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Adam Cox OldInsuranceMaps.net is a Django-based crowdsourcing platform with hundreds of users, but was built with basic account configuration–no social logins or external integrations. However, as interest in the project from the OSM and... MapLibre Tiles - next generation tiles and other MapLibre news Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Yuri Astrakhan Learn everything MapLibre has been up to, from the new tile format to Martin tile server, MapLibre GL, MapLibre Native, and many other projects Scoring the feasibility of micromobility with OSM data Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Julio May Springfield, IL, faces high car dependency and infrastructure gaps. This session explores using multi-criteria geospatial analysis to solve the “last-mile” problem. We’ll dive into a composite scoring model built on five key... Wikimedia Enterprise – Lessons learned from working with large commercial reusers Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Lane Becker and Michal Migurski Over the last 6 years, The Wikimedia Foundation has developed a separate infrastructure to handle large commercial reusers of Wikipedia (etc) content. And then AI came along… Having the legal, technical, and... The Walksheds Tool: Evaluating Pedestrian Accessibility Using OSM Data Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Sam Yasen The OpenSidewalks Schema is an open pedestrian transportation network data standard for describing and sharing pedestrian network and pedestrian network-adjacent data. See how The Walksheds tool (https://walksheds.tdei.us/) turns OpenStreetMap (OSM) sidewalks, crossings,... Community mapping, data sovereignty and Indigenous toponyms on OSM Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Emily Jacobi In 2015 Awana Digital (previously Digital Democracy) used OSM iD Editor to create Mapeo; an offline, open-source app co-built with Indigenous communities in the Amazon. Now with CoMapeo, people around the world... How We Prepared HIFLD for the Singularity Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Jeremy Herzog When HIFLD Open was taken offline in 2025, we launched a community-driven effort to restore its 400+ critical data layers. As automata increasingly navigate systems for users, they prioritize what is structured... Embedding Tiles: Should We Be Structuring Open Map Data for AI? Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Sean Gorman AI models are increasingly integrating open map data into their data corpus. Should the community leave AI labs to their own devices or help intermediate this interaction? To this end we are... OpenPOIs: a superset of open place data Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Nat Henry Free and global-scale datasets for points of interest (POIs) have proliferated over the past two years, and OSM users need new tools to understand and compare them. This talk introduces OpenPOIs, a... Open Wetlands Map - an accessible, community-maintained dataset of wetland observations Jun 12, 2026 &middot; EmmaLi Tsai Wetlands filter our water & protect our communities. Despite their importance, up-to-date wetland data remains fragmented, resulting in decision-making based on incomplete information. To address this, we are collaborating with OSM US... Safeguarding America's Open Infrastructure Data: OSM As An Ecosystem Enabler Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Puneet Kollipara The federal open-data portal HIFLD Open long curated geospatial data layers on critical infrastructure from across agencies. But its 2025 shutdown by DHS wiped out a common reference point for officials protecting... Can you make a legal map with OpenStreetMap? Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Michal Migurski Boundary disputes are a major but little-known challenge in mapping: how can you show disputed borders between neighboring countries that claim the same territory? Here in the U.S. we’re used to seeing... Good vibes about places Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Minh Nguyễn In the United States, we have long classified populated places based on diverse personal perspectives, resulting in a patchwork that is inadequate for data consumers and difficult for users to understand. How... OHM: In Search of the Eastern Turnpike Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Richard Welty The Eastern Turnpike operated between the areas of Albany NY and Williamstown MA from 1799 into the late 19th century. A private toll road, it was typical of the period. There were... Thematic OpenStreetMap with TheMap.is Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Quincy Morgan OSM has a LOT of data, it can be hard to sift through it all! TheMap.is lets you easily browse OSM data by theme so you can focus on just the data... Rapid Disaster Mapping for Playgrounds: Turning Geospatial Data into Recovery Priorities Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Alice Lee After the 2025 Kerrville, TX floods and Los Angeles wildfires, KABOOM! needed to quickly support communities by identifying damaged playgrounds. In this 15-minute talk, I’ll share a GIS workflow: compile playspace locations... Sanborn in Sandusky - A Search for Local Stories Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Emma Farahay After being inspired during Mapping USA by OpenHistoricalMap and OldInsuranceMaps.net, explore the history of Sanborn Fire Insurance Maps in Sandusky, Ohio. How might these maps be used by local agencies versus local... How Madison civic tech gave me a map to navigate my early career Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Abe Rubenstein “Are you a developer interested in building your own bus app?” In 2013, I saw this question on the SMSMyBus website – an ad for a real-time Madison Metro bus arrival API.... Using LANDSAT and OSM to Map Urban Heat Impact Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Luke Everhart This session would go over a class project I completed over the course of last semester. I developed a Python program that takes both LANDSAT thermal band imagery and OSM ways exported... Mergin Maps: an open source platform based on QGIS for data collection and collaboration Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Vitor Vieira Mergin Maps simplifies field data collection, offering an open-source platform built on the power and familiarity of QGIS. Capture, share and publish your geospatial data seamlessly with an intuitive mobile app and... Using Maps to Tell Stories Jun 12, 2026 &middot; Daniel Steiner Data drives analysis but curiosity drives story, and that’s available to everyone. Keynote: Engaging with the Local Landscape: The Story of Mapping Teejop Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Gareth Baldrica-Franklin Hear Gareth share the story of Mapping Teejop, a digital mapping project centering Indigenous history and presence in and around Madison, Wisconsin. Mapping Teejop is an educational project for classroom use—a series... Impact of Mapillary Camera Grant and Scaling Open Street-Level Imagery Across US Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Harald Kliems In 2025, the Mapillary Camera Grant Program distributed 40 cameras to the OpenStreetMap US community. Over 5 million fresh 360° images and comprehensive coverage of cities from Lansing, Michigan to Washington D.C.... The Social Life of Data Quality in OSM Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Gopi Malathi OSM data quality evaluations typically focus on measuring the accuracy and usability of the data. But how do contributors with diverse backgrounds, skills, and interests collectively decide what “good quality” data actually... Coordinates of Connection: Mapping the People, Places, and Purpose That Bind Us Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Lindsey Peña OpenStreetMap is more than data: it’s a community. Coordinates of Connection explores how relationships, shared purpose, and collaboration strengthen the geospatial fabric across the U.S. From first-time mappers to public, nonprofit, and... Reinventing Map-Making with Edge AI Dashcams: KartaDashCam at Scale Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Adrian Margin GrabMaps’ KartaDashCam transforms everyday ride-hailing trips in Southeast Asia into a passive mapping fleet. Using Edge AI, we detect traffic signs and POIs on-device to drastically reduce mapping costs. We’re also coming... (Re)Mapping DC's Bus Network Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Andrew Heare On June 29, 2025, the Metrobus network in the Washington, DC area was radically altered overnight. Every route number was changed, bus stops were closed, and some routes were entirely different than... The Usefulness of Road Networks in OSM Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Michal Migurski What good can you do with an OpenStreetMap road network? OSM has been the basis of several transportation network datasets like SharedStreets, OSMnx, and most recently Overture. Road network owners and transportation... Using Official Datasets for Roads and Trails Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Rob Savoye This talk covers the details of using official government datasets from the BLM, NPS, and USFS, and processing the data to update OSM with anything useful, like a trail name or road... OpenSidewalks in OpenStreetMap: Using OS-CONNECT as a Reference Layer in Rapid Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Amy Bordenave OpenSidewalks (OSW) is an open data standard for mapping connected pedestrian networks, developed by the Taskar Center for Accessible Technology (TCAT) at the UW. In Washington State, a legislative proviso funded the... How OpenStreetMap Infiltrated our Relationship (or how you can get your loved ones into OSM) Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Alyssa Castronuovo and Jacob Hall One day in 2021 Jacob opened up StreetComplete on a date, and now Alyssa works for OpenStreetMap US. Hear how we navigate our map-filled personal and professional lives, and how you can... History however you look at it Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Minh Nguyễn OpenHistoricalMap is more than a map. As an open data project, we want to help you discover and understand the wealth of information that mappers have contributed, whether or not it fits... Announcing MapRoulette 4 Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Martijn van Exel I am excited to give you a first look at the brand new MapRoulette interface! With this redesign, we have tried to give you a fresh user interface with targeted improvements, while... Motivating mappers Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Matthew Whilden If you can spend an hour doing an activity that causes another mapper to do 2 more hours of mapping, that’s an incredible increase in mapping. But what could possibly do that?... Plotting OpenStreetMap Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Ariel Kadouri There are many fun flavors of OpenStreetMap. From the classic style on openstreetmap.org, to Americana, OpenTrailMap, OpenCycleMap and more. Plenty of us create physical maps too - using QGIS or other GIS... What is real? Estimating the fidelity of OSM data using changesets Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Nat Henry OSM users want to know that the map reflects reality at their location and for their points of interest (POIs). I built a statistical model that uses changesets to estimate OSM’s data... Scaling OSM Curation: AI Agents for High-Volume Ground-Truth Validation Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Aditya Sridhar Manual curation is a bottleneck, with backlogs hitting 200k tasks. By using a distributed data network, we’ve cut data expiration from 11 to 3 months, making our map 4x fresher. This volume... State of OpenStreetMap on Internet-in-a-Box Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Daniel Krol and Jacob Chapman Internet-in-a-Box is an open source project that delivers offline access to educational content (Wikipedia, Khan Academy, OSM) for communities with limited or no Internet connectivity. You can turn your low-cost hardware into... Contributing with an Open Source Toolchain Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Devon Hanbey There are so many ways you can contribute data to the OSM project- from manual entry and street level photos to aerial photography. This talk will explore them all, along with the... Mapping with Restraint: How Lyft leverages and contributes to the OSM Community Jun 13, 2026 &middot; Parth Vyas and Brian Spencer At Lyft’s scale, responsible OSM stewardship means knowing when not to edit. We defend the critical routing layer via “golden route” simulation that validates human edits before they ship, absorb transient and... State of the Map US 2026 Sponsors Become a sponsor Diamond · $30,000 Platinum · $20,000 Gold · $15,000 Silver · $8,000 Bronze · $4,000 Flamingo · $2,500 Meet our speakers Abe Rubenstein Adam Cox Aditya Sridhar Adrian Margin Alan McConchie Alice Lee Alyssa Castronuovo Amy Bordenave Andrew Heare Ariel Kadouri Ben Varick Brian Spencer Chad Blevins Clay Smalley Daniel Krol Daniel Steiner Devon Hanbey Dhanyasri Bolla Emily Jacobi Emma Farahay EmmaLi Tsai Felipe Valdez Gareth Baldrica-Franklin Gopi Malathi Harald Kliems Howard Veregin Jacob Chapman Jacob Hall Jake Low Jeremy Herzog Julio May Kapeel Daryani Kelsey Taylor Lane Becker Lindsey Peña Luke Everhart Maggie Cawley Martijn van Exel Matthew Whilden Michal Migurski Minh Nguyễn Nat Henry Parth Vyas Puneet Kollipara Quincy Morgan Richard Welty Rob Savoye Sam Yasen Samuel Younkin Sean Gorman Shaishav Maisuria Stephen Kennedy Steven Johnson Vishal Kumar Vitor Vieira Yuri Astrakhan News Reminiscing on State of the Map US 2026 Jul 8, 2026 &middot; OpenStreetMap US Staff State of the Map US made its way back to the midwest after seven years, so you betcha we had a great time in Madison! Over three days, 200 fellow mappers and... Join the State of the Map US Narrative Map Competition! Apr 2, 2026 &middot; OpenStreetMap US Staff This year, we’re excited to bring a new twist to State of the Map US! In partnership with Esri we’re launching the State of the Map US Narrative Map Competition, a worldwide... New for 2026: State of the Map US Fellowships Jan 5, 2026 &middot; OpenStreetMap US Staff For the past ten years, OpenStreetMap US has had the pleasure of hosting scholars at State of the Map US. Each year, they bring invaluable knowledge, unique perspectives, and new opinions to... The Call for Proposals for State of the Map US 2026 is Now Open Dec 15, 2025 &middot; OpenStreetMap US Staff All members of the OpenStreetMap community are invited to propose a session for State of the Map US 2026, coming to Madison Wisconsin this summer! Each year, the conference planning committee is... Past conferences 2025 Boston, MA 2024 Salt Lake City, UT 2023 Richmond, VA 2022 Tucs",
