        "evidenceText": "Publishers pressure Meta to nix new terms that would let the company scrape their websites | Nieman Journalism Lab \n\n \n \n\n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n  \n  \n  \n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n   \n \n \n \n\t\t \r\n\t\t \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n   \n     \n   \n\n   \n     \n    \n  \n\n \n \n   \n     \n   \n\n   \n     \n       \n       \n         \n         \n           \n         \n       \n     \n    \n  \n\n \n \n   \n     \n   \n\n   \n\n     \n       Get Nieman Lab in your inbox. \n       All the latest headlines and insights from the world of digital journalism and publishing. Our daily newsletter is a must-read from newsrooms to executive suites. \n     \n\n         \n\n             \n       \n       \n\n             \n\n             \n         Leave this empty \n         \n       \n\n       \n         Email address \n         \n\n         Name  (optional)  \n         \n       \n\n       \n         \n           Subscribe \n                      \n         \n       \n\n       \n        We will never sell your address.\n         Prefer a weekly email? \n       \n\n              \n     \n\n         \n       Nearly there &mdash; check your email. \n       \n        We have sent a confirmation link to   .\n        Click it and you are on the list. If it has not turned up in a\n        minute or two, it may be in your spam folder.\n       \n       Subscribe another address \n     \n\n     \n    \n\n      \n  \n\n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n  \n   \n     \n           \n   \n \n\n \n        (Photo by  Dima Solomin  on  Unsplash )    \n\n\n \n\n\n \n      \n   \n     \n\n              \n \n   \n     \n      Publishers pressure Meta to nix new terms that would let the company scrape their websites     \n   \n\n   \n           \n         Link \n         newsmediaalliance.org ↗ \n       \n    \n           \n         Posted by \n         Hanaa' Tameez        \n    \n     \n       October 22, 2026 \n     \n   \n \n\n\n \n   The largest news publishing trade association in the United States is calling on Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg to suspend the implementation of the company’s new commercial terms of service, set to go into effect on October 30. \n In an  open letter  to Zuckerberg published Thursday, News/Media Alliance’s president and CEO  Danielle Coffey  wrote that Meta should “withdraw these terms and should hold discussions around future changes with NMA and publishers.” \n The terms,  first reported by Nieman Lab on October 15 , would allow for the mass scraping of websites “associated with” business accounts on Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, and Threads. There is currently no option to opt out. On October 16, Meta  removed  the word “train” from its terms and limited the use of future scraped content to “advertising and business products.” But  legal experts told Nieman Lab  the revised terms would still permit mass scraping. \n “News publishers will be forced into providing their valuable journalism to support the development of products that may be used to compete with them for readers and advertisers, under duress and without compensation,” Coffey said in her letter. “It is wholly unreasonable for the mere use of a Meta business account to require publishers to grant unrestricted access to their websites (and even content posted by their users), for the benefit of Meta to develop its business products.” \n NMA represents more than 2,000 news publishers in the United States. The discussions, Coffey said, should focus on four core issues: \n    \n (i) the specific Meta Company Products and uses for which Meta would like to use publishers’ scraped content; \n (ii) how publishers can opt in to permit scraping, separate from obtaining access to Meta’s business products; \n (iii) the specific crawlers, fetchers, or other bots that will be utilized; \n (iv) how Meta will ensure publishers’ robots.txt files, registration walls, or paywalls are respected. \n   \n Coffey requested a public response from Zuckerberg to the letter by October 26, and noted that if “in the event Meta adopts these revised terms over our members’ express objections, our members do not consent to them and reserve all of their legal rights.” \n The World Association of News Publishers (WAN-IFRA) sent  a similar letter  to Meta’s chief global affairs officer Joel Kaplan on Thursday, while adding its concern about Meta’s implementation of its new subscription service, Meta One, which requires users to pay a fee to post links on Meta platforms. WAN-IFRA’s membership spans 20,000 publishers in 120 countries around the world. \n The Center for Media and Digital Governance (CMDG) at the Open Markets Institute  published  its own statement on Thursday, saying that regulators in the United States should take immediate action to “stop Meta from exploiting publishers around the world.” \n “If Meta gets away with this, every dominant technology company will have a blueprint for doing the same,” said CMDG director  Courtney Radsch . “Regulators have an opportunity to draw a line now, before the next generation of AI monopolies is built on the uncompensate",
