        "finalUrl": "https://gfmd.info/fundings/fault-lines-reporting-fund/",
        "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
        "pageTitle": "Fault Lines Reporting Fund - GFMD",
        "pageDescription": "The IWMF&#039;s Fault Lines Reporting Fund supports journalists reporting on the forces driving racism, antisemitism, Islamophobia, misogyny, and other forms of intolerance promoting hate in the United States.",
        "evidenceText": "Fault Lines Reporting Fund - GFMD « Back to all Fundings Fault Lines Reporting Fund Organisation: International Women’s Media Foundation Status: Open Type: Grant Theme: Freedom of expression Funding Size: USD 2,500–USD 10,000 Deadline: Ongoing Eligibility: USA journalists writing about discrimination Target Region: USA Application Language: English The IWMF’s Fault Lines Reporting Fund supports journalists reporting on the forces driving racism, antisemitism, Islamophobia, misogyny, and other forms of intolerance promoting hate in the United States. Successful applications examine how false, divisive, and harmful narratives — such as stories that promote or normalise hate, discrimination, and the dehumanisation of individuals and communities — take hold, including what is fuelling them, where they are most pronounced, the scope of their impact, and where individuals and communities are making real progress in combating these hate-based agendas. The three-year programme provides grants to staff and freelance journalists planning well-researched, investigative, and evidence-based reporting within the United States. Grant funds may be used to cover travel, logistics, security, insurance, and other reporting-related expenses. Eligibility Applicants must report in the United States. The applicant’s primary profession must be journalism; applications will be accepted from freelance, staff, or content-based journalists. Applicants must have 3+ years of professional journalism experience outside of internships. Applicants must present a strong track record of exceptional journalism or show proof of interest from an editor. Requirements A completed application in English (reporting itself may be conducted in any language). A well-developed story pitch demonstrating investigative merit, originality, reach, potential impact, and need for funding. Supporting documentation demonstrating journalistic track record. Grantees must publish in a U.S. outlet within one year of receiving a grant and must credit the IWMF. How to Apply Submit the application through the IWMF Submittable portal on a rolling basis; there is no fixed closing date for the current programme cycle. Larger awards are considered on a case-by-case basis. Apply here . For more information, please visit this page . For the latest funding opportunities, profiles of media donors, and expert insight from GFMD’s MediaDev Fundraising Guide sign up for Bottom Line newsletter Sign Up Search Search for: This website uses cookies. By continuing to use the website you consent to the use and storage of cookies on your device. You are using an outdated browser which can not show modern web content. We suggest you download Chrome or Firefox .",
