        "pageTitle": "Building OSINT evidence databases: Lessons from the Amnesty Digital Investigations Summit | HURIDOCS",
        "pageDescription": "Reflections on organising, preserving and making open-source information usable for human rights investigations and accountability.",
        "evidenceText": "Building OSINT evidence databases: Lessons from the Amnesty Digital Investigations Summit | HURIDOCS \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\t \n\n\n \n \n \n \n\t\t \n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t \n\t\t\t \n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t \n\t\t \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n   \n \n \n \r\n \r\n                                   \n  \n \n \n \n \n     \n \n\n \n     \n          \n\n   \n\n  \n     Skip to content \n\n     \n       \n      \n\n     \n\n \n\t \n\n     \n\t\t\t \n\n\t\t\t\t    Home   >   Latest   >   News   >  Building OSINT evidence databases: Lessons from the Amnesty Digital Investigations Summit   \n\t\t\t \n\n\t\t\t\n \n   \n\n           Building OSINT evidence databases: Lessons from the Amnesty Digital Investigations Summit \n    \n           Reflections on organising, preserving and making open-source information usable for human rights investigations and accountability.\n \n    \n           \n        By\n                   \n              Lucía Gómez           \n                on  3 November 2026 \n       \n    \n   \n   \n           \n         \n       \n       \n        Lucía Gómez Vicente presented a workshop on organising and storing open-source information and how to build evidence databases      \n\n       \n\n\t\t\t\t \n\n        \t\n A video disappears from social media. An account is suspended. A photograph is shared thousands of times, losing its original context along the way. For human rights investigators working with open-source information, these are everyday challenges that can determine whether information remains useful long after an event has taken place. \n\n\n\n Videos, photographs, social media posts and satellite imagery can help document human rights violations, reconstruct events and support accountability efforts. Yet collecting information is only the beginning. The real challenge lies in preserving it, understanding what it can tell us and ensuring it remains accessible and reliable over time. \n\n\n\n   The Amnesty Digital Investigations Summit was held in Geneva from 8 to 9 October 2026.  \n\n\n\n At the  Amnesty Digital Investigations Summit  in Geneva on 8 and 9 October 2026, practitioners explored the methods, tools and challenges involved in open-source investigations. Drawing on HURIDOCS’ experience in human rights information management, we presented  Organising and Storing Open-Source Information: How to Build Evidence Databases , exploring how documentation methodologies, structured data and digital preservation can support investigations over the long term. \n\n\n\n Four themes stood out: the importance of provenance and traceability, the need to preserve digital material early, the challenges of verification in the age of AI, and the value of designing information systems around investigators’ needs. \n\n\n\n  Starting with the purpose, not the technology  \n\n\n\n When faced with large volumes of digital information, it can be tempting to start by looking for the right tool. But an effective evidence database begins with a different question: what do we need this information to help us understand? \n\n\n\n An investigation focused on criminal accountability may have different requirements from one supporting advocacy, journalism or public documentation. These differences shape how information should be collected, organised, preserved and accessed. \n\n\n\n Before selecting a platform or building a data model, teams need to establish what they are documenting, how the information will be used and which relationships they need to capture between events, people, locations and sources. \n\n\n\n This requires collaboration between investigators, documenters and technical teams. The goal is not to collect every possible data point, but to build a system that supports the investigation’s actual needs and can evolve as new findings emerge. \n\n\n\n  Connecting the dots without losing sight of the source  \n\n\n\n Imagine a video circulating online that appears to show an incident involving security forces. Where was it published? When was it collected? Can its location be verified? Does other material support or challenge the initial interpretation? These questions illustrate why organising evidence involves more than storing files. Investigators need to distinguish between the source itself, the information recorded about it and the claims it may support. \n\n\n\n The original video is one element. Its metadata and collection history provide another. The interpretation that it depicts a particular event, location or actor is a separate conclusion requiring assessment. \n\n\n\n Keeping these elements distinct allows investigators to trace conclusions back to their supporting material, connect multiple sources to an event and identify where uncertainty remains. A well-designed database makes these relationships visible without treating an investigative interpretation as an established fact. \n\n\n\n This is particularly important when information may be used in accountability processes, where investigators need to explain not only what they believe happened, but how they reached their conclusions. \n\n\n\n   Participants at the D",
