        "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt A Democratic landslide reshaped American politics. The week’s best reporting revealed how it happened and what comes next. Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION The stories that explain a historic election in America A supporter holds up a hat with an American flag for Democratic Senate candidate Texas state Rep. James Talarico on Tuesday night in Texas. (AP Photo/Eric Gay) What a week. On Tuesday, in what could be one of the most consequential elections in American history, voters turned a blue wave into a blue tsunami. The result: In what appears to be a direct repudiation of President Donald Trump, Americans overwhelmingly voted to give the Democrats power in both the House of Representatives and the U.S. Senate. Democrats not only won high-profile races in traditionally blue regions of the country, but they also flipped previously red strongholds &mdash; places such as South Carolina, Kansas, Ohio and, perhaps most notably, Texas. Not only did Democrats win in states where they have typically struggled, but they won easily. On Thursday, I recorded an episode for &ldquo;The Poynter Report Podcast&rdquo; that will come out next Monday. My guest, CNN&rsquo;s John King, told me it was an election that political experts will study for years to come. The aftershocks will last for years to come, too. Here are some thoughts, links and commentary looking back at the week that was, and ahead at what&rsquo;s possibly to come. Most interesting piece of the week Do you wonder what was going on inside the White House and the Republican Party as the world was about to crash around them? Well, be sure to check out the must-read piece of the week: The New York Times&rsquo; Shane Goldmacher, Jonathan Swan and Maggie Haberman with &ldquo;I Have Seen the Enemy, and It Is Us&rsquo;: Inside Team Trump&rsquo;s Historic Collapse.&rdquo; It&rsquo;s a fascinating look at a brewing storm that the Republicans seemed to sense was coming. The Times talked to more than 70 people &mdash; among them lawmakers, political strategists, party leaders, pollsters, White House officials and congressional aides &mdash; who were directly involved in the midterm elections. In the end, a combination of things sank the GOP&rsquo;s ship: an unpopular war in Iran, high gas and grocery prices, undesirable candidates either backed or hand-picked by an unpopular president and other factors. The Times wrote, &ldquo;What Republicans could not overcome was a president who lost the public&rsquo;s trust on the economy and so much more. And what they had not counted on was an unpopular war in the Middle East &mdash; one that would close the Strait of Hormuz and push gas prices past $4 a gallon &mdash; that would rage all the way through the election.&rdquo; The article explored flaws in the Republican strategy heading into this election cycle and how Democrats recognized the GOP&rsquo;s vulnerabilities and ran effective campaigns across the country. The Times wrote, &ldquo;They set about recruiting candidates with distinctive biographies: military veterans, doctors, farmers, a smokejumper, a Tejano music star &mdash; just about anyone who didn&rsquo;t scream professional politician.&rdquo; In the end, it was a perfect storm for the Democrats and the Times&rsquo; account of exactly what went down, based on superb sourcing and reporting, is fascinating. Good news The good news, at least so far, is that there have been no widespread or serious allegations of election fraud &mdash; especially from the MAGA supporters who have claimed elections were rigged in the past. CNN&rsquo;s Donie O&rsquo;Sullivan said, &ldquo;MAGA media personalities are not happy about the election results, but they are accepting that they lost. The focus of MAGA now is not on election denial but who to blame for these losses.&rdquo; Trump has already said he will accept the results. That, however, seemed like an unlikely scenario not all that long ago. The New York Times&rsquo; Richard Fausset and Nick Corasaniti wrote , &ldquo;Mr. Trump had started his second term intent on harnessing the power of the federal government to resurface false claims that widespread fraud had tainted his earlier defeat &mdash; and to use those claims to assert unprecedented control over the midterms this year. He failed. Voters so thoroughly rejected Republicans on Tuesday, ushering in the largest Democratic House majority in generations and flipping seats that had been red for decades, that it became impossible to blame the outcome on fraud.&rdquo; But there was one hiccup in all of this. Trump posted on Truth Social on Thursday that incumbent Republican Sen. Dan Sullivan of Alaska should be declared the winner of the still-too-close-to-call race against Democrat Mary Peltola. Another candidate named Dan Sullivan, along with a fourth candidate, also appeared on the ballot.  Trump wrote, &ldquo;The vote totals for the Fake Dan Sullivan, plus the other &lsquo;slug&rsquo; that the Dems put on the Ballot, should be immediately given to the REAL Dan Sullivan, and the Election is over - He easily wins. There is no other fair solution!&rdquo; A final call on this race might not happen for days or even weeks. Speaking of Trump So far, Trump&rsquo;s reaction to the midterms has been oddly muted. New York Times White House correspondent Maggie Haberman said in a video on the Times&rsquo; website, &ldquo;Trump&rsquo;s reaction to the midterm results seems out of character.&rdquo; For example, Trump spent the day after Election Day talking more about the ballroom construction project than the midterms. Haberman, however, said it was &ldquo;not entirely inconsistent&rdquo; with how Trump has been behaving behind the scenes. According to Haberman and her Times colleagues, Trump asked those around him what might have happened if the U.S. never went to war with Iran. Then again, Trump insists that going to war was necessary to, he claimed, keep Iran from having a nuclear weapon. The president was repeatedly told by advisers that voters were unhappy with the economy and Trump&rsquo;s focus on White House renovations. But, Haberman said, Trump was &ldquo;unmoved&rdquo; because he was not on the ballot. In fact, Trump would say it was an honor when Republican candidates did better when he was on the ballot. &ldquo;However,&rdquo; Haberman said, &ldquo;the repudiation of the president and his party in the midterms this week is so undeniable that it&rsquo;s not clear what an honor he sees it at right now.&rdquo; Haberman continued, &ldquo;He&rsquo;s in something that people who have known him for a long time would describe as a processing state. President Trump behaved on Wednesday as if nothing had happened the day before, as if people were not bothered by the fact that he spent so much time on what they saw as vanity projects.&rdquo; Don&rsquo;t mess with This election was full of big headlines. But the biggest headline of all was what happened in Texas, which elected a Democratic governor for the first time since the 1990s. And in what was probably the race of this midterm, Democrat James Talarico defeated Republican Ken Paxton for the U.S. Senate. Here are two good stories to dissect what happened in Texas: The New York Times&rsquo; J. David Goodman with &ldquo;The Machine, and the Messaging, That Won Texas for Democrats.&rdquo; The Washington Post&rsquo;s Karen Tumulty, Matthew Choi and Molly Hennessy-Fiske with &ldquo;How the Texas blue wave came crashing down on Republicans.&rdquo; A plea for journalism freedom The Committee to Protect Journalists issued a statement Thursday asking that the incoming Democratic-led Congress &ldquo;restore press freedom protections at home and abroad by immediately strengthening oversight of federal agencies.&rdquo; CPJ CEO Jodie Ginsberg said, &ldquo;The incoming Congress has a historic opportunity to reverse the United States&rsquo; precipitous decline in press freedom and hold officials accountable for attacks on journalists. Lawmakers must defend the First Amendment as a pillar of American democracy and ensure that protecting press freedom is a priority in U.S. foreign policy.&rdquo; CPJ went on to say, &ldquo;CPJ has documented escalating attacks on U.S. media during the second Trump administration, including restricted government access, issuing subpoenas, retaliatory litigation, regulatory threats against broadcasters, and arbitrary arrests of journalists. Internationally, the administration has dismantled longstanding bipartisan efforts to counter censorship and support independent journalism. Congress must prioritize immediate oversight of the Federal Communications Commission and end the use of its regulatory powers to retaliate against news outlets. Lawmakers should also reintroduce and pass the PRESS Act to protect journalists&rsquo; confidential sources and reporting materials, and hold accountable those responsible for attacks on journalists in the U.S. and abroad.&rdquo; Media news, tidbits and interesting links for your weekend review Trump continues his war against the press, specifically CNN. The network was scheduled to handle TV pool duty on Thursday, but Trump continued to ban it from the pool. That meant none of the other TV networks in the pool (ABC, CBS, NBC, Fox News and CNN) covered Trump&rsquo;s remarks at an event in the Oval Office. The New York Times&rsquo; Jodi Kantor and Michael S. Schmidt with &ldquo;Justice Dept. Steps Up Criminal Inquiry of Supreme Court Abortion Decision Leak.&rdquo; NBC announced that Carson Daly has been named co-host of the &ldquo;Saturday Today&rdquo; show. Starting this Saturday, Daly will join co-anchor Laura Jarrett, feature anchor Joe Fryer and meteorologist Angie Lassman on the Saturday edition of the iconic morning show. Daly succeeds Peter Alexander, who left earlier this year to become a daytime anchor at MS NOW. Daly will continue to appear weekdays as the features reporter on the regular &ldquo;Today&rdquo; show. Here&rsquo;s more from NBC. My Poynter colleague Angela Fu with &ldquo;USA Today Co. blames AI for its revenue woes. It also sees AI as a solution.&rdquo; Salon, the digital news site known for its political and cultural commentary, announced Thursday it was shutting down after 31 years. The site&rsquo;s closing announcement said, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s difficult to find the right words for the end of something that has meant so much to so many people. Salon was never just a website. It was an idea about what journalism could be, born at a moment when the internet was still a largely unexplored frontier.&rdquo; The statement continued, &ldquo;The business realities of independent digital journalism have changed profoundly over the years. After careful consideration, we have reached the difficult decision that our publishing model is no longer sustainable. We know this news will be deeply disappointing to many of you, and we share that sadness.&rdquo; The New York Times&rsquo; Benjamin Mullin and Lauren Hirsch with &ldquo;New York Times Company in Advanced Talks to Acquire Letterboxd.&rdquo; Also in The New York Times, John Koblin and Michael M. Grynbaum with &ldquo;Steve Kornacki and NBC Drew Crowds as Midterms Got a Ratings Bump.&rdquo; Finally, Status&rsquo; Oliver Darcy with &ldquo;The &lsquo;60&rsquo; Blame Game.&rdquo; Darcy writes, &ldquo;The Trump administration&rsquo;s attack on a &lsquo;60 Minutes&rsquo; report has prompted behind-the-scenes finger pointing at CBS News, where Status has learned irate staffers are accusing network brass of throwing its journalists under the bus.&rdquo; More resources for journalists Strengthen your editing skills and earn an industry-recognized certificate from Poynter and ACES . Choose from Introductory, Intermediate or Advanced pathways covering current editing standards, tools and best practices. You can start at the level that&rsquo;s right for you and learn at your own pace. Build stronger fact-checking skills with Fact-Checking for Everyone , a new self-directed course from Poynter&rsquo;s International Fact-Checking Network. Learn how to evaluate sources, verify digital content and use professional fact-checking techniques in your everyday work.  Don't have a researcher on staff? Poynter Research can help you uncover hard-to-find facts, verify claims and track down public records, so you can save time and report with confidence. Have feedback or a tip? Email Poynter senior media writer Tom Jones at tjones@poynter.org . The Poynter Report is your daily dive into the world of media, packed with the latest news and insights. Get it delivered to your inbox Monday through Friday by signing up here . And don&rsquo;t forget to tune into our biweekly podcast for even more. Support the journalism that keeps you informed. GIVE NOW ADVERTISE // DONATE // LEARN // JOBS Did someone forward you this email? Sign up here. © All rights reserved Poynter Institute 2026 801 Third Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701 If you don't want to receive email updates from Poynter, we understand. You can change your subscription preferences or unsubscribe from all Poynter emails .",
