        "finalUrl": "https://carolinapublicpress.org/82900/gop-gerrymander-backfires-in-nc-as-states-congressional-map-goes-blue/",
        "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
        "pageTitle": "Congressional gerrymander fails for NC GOP, as Democrats prevail",
        "pageDescription": "Not only did gerrymander not rip away Don Davis&#039; congressional seat, but NC Republicans assumed to be safe ended up losing.",
        "evidenceText": "Congressional gerrymander fails for NC GOP, as Democrats prevail Close Search for: Search Close Skip to content Signs featuring the congressional race between Don Davis and Laurie Buckhout are among those outside a Nash County polling place in late October 2026. Sarah Michels / Carolina Public Press Republican state legislators were clear in October 2025, when they redrew North Carolina’s congressional maps: they wanted Democratic US Rep. Don Davis out. Redistricting normally takes place only immediately after the decennial Census, but North Carolina was one of several states that heeded President Donald Trump’s call to redraw maps to give Republicans an edge in this year’s midterm elections. State Sen. Ralph Hise , R-Mitchell, led the effort.  “The motivation behind this redraw is simple and singular: draw a new map that will bring an additional Republican seat to the North Carolina congressional delegation,” Hise said at the time. “Republicans hold a razor-thin margin in the United States House of Representatives. If Democrats flip four seats in the upcoming midterm elections, they will take control of the House, and torpedo president Trump’s agenda.” Don Davis was first elected to Congress in 2022, but came with a much longer resume. A graduate of the Air Force Academy, he achieved the rank of Air Force Captain, taught at East Carolina; became the mayor of Snow Hill, his hometown in Greene County; and served multiple terms in the state Senate. In his 2024 reelection bid, Davis won the First Congressional District race to represent northeastern North Carolina by fewer than two percentage points. When the national mid-decade redistricting frenzy began, state Republicans saw the narrow margin as an opportunity. The much redder Third Congressional District runs along NC-1’s southern border, so they thought they could afford to exchange some of NC-1’s bluer sections with NC-3’s redder ones.  But the effort, and the transparency behind it, backfired. By the end of election night, Davis was ahead of Buckhout, with 50.07% of the vote and a more than 10,000 vote edge, leading the Republican challenger to concede. Davis’ race wasn’t the only Republican disappointment following that gerrymander, which itself was built on earlier gerrymandering.  Democratic farmer Jamie Ager defeated State Rep. Jennifer Balkcom , R-Henderson, in Western North Carolina’s 11th Congressional District.  In the Fifth Congressional District, Democrat Chuck Hubbard appeared to have unseated longtime Republican incumbent Virginia Foxx in a very narrow race. The pair are separated by 99 votes, close enough to call for a recount. Late-arriving absentee ballots and provisional ballots could potentially alter that vote total. In the Sixth Congressional District, Democrat Cyril Jefferson had a slightly larger lead, with 51.14% of the vote over US Rep. Addison McDowell . In the Ninth Congressional District, Democrat Richard Ojeda bested Richard Hudson with 51.11% of the vote as well. In the 13th Congressional District, Democrat Paul Barringer won against US Rep. Brad Knott with 50.5% of the vote. In a major upset, Democrat Lakesha Womack earned the majority of the vote in the 14th Congressional District against US Rep. Tim Moore . Moore, who trails Womack by nearly 6,000 votes, or about 2.22 percentage points, had been one of the most influential Republicans in the state, long serving as NC House Speaker before running for Congress. The only US House seats Republicans appear to have kept are NC-3, represented by US Rep. Greg Murphy , NC-7, represented by US Rep. David Rouzer , NC-8, represented by US Rep. Mark Harris , and NC-10, represented by US Rep. Pat Harrigan . And the vote count in Murphy’s race appears too close to call, with only a 233-vote edge, enough to be affected by late-arriving absentee ballots and provisional votes, and likely subject to a recount. The final vote tally — and apparent victors — could change between now and the county canvass, when the official results are tallied. Election officials still need to consider whether to count provisional ballots, and absentee and provisional voters who need to fix any issues have a few days to do so. Losing candidates also may file election protests, alleging irregularities in the process, or ask for recounts. Under state law, losing US House candidates may request a recount if the difference in votes between the first- and second-place candidates is less than 1% of the total votes cast in the race.  Some winning Democrats were optimistic about their chances weeks before the election, including Ojeda and Hubbard. They pointed to their opponents, who began running campaign ads for the first cycle in a long time.  However, most political watchers did not expect them to win, much less several of the other Democrats who flipped seats, if the vote tallies hold.  The newly elected Democrats were part of a national wave in which Republicans were swept from office. They will have the opportunity to go to Washington as part of a congressional majority. Editor’s note: This is a developing story and will be updated. Related Republish This Story This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License. You may republish our stories for free, online or in print. Simply copy and paste the article contents from the box below. Note, some images and interactive features may not be included here. Close window Republish this article This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License . This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License . You may republish our stories for free, online or in print. Simply copy and paste the article contents from the box below. Note, some images and interactive features may not be included here. Read our Republishing Guidelines for more information. GOP gerrymander backfires in NC, as state’s congressional map goes blue by Sarah Michels, Carolina Public Press November 4, 2026 <h1>GOP gerrymander backfires in NC, as state’s congressional map goes blue</h1> <p class=\"byline\">by Sarah Michels, Carolina Public Press <br />November 4, 2026</p> <p>Republican state legislators were clear in October 2025, when they redrew North Carolina’s congressional maps: they wanted Democratic US Rep. <strong>Don Davis</strong> out. </p> <p>Redistricting normally takes place only immediately after the decennial Census, but North Carolina was one of several states that heeded President <strong>Donald Trump’s</strong> call to redraw maps to give Republicans an edge in this year’s midterm elections.</p> <p>State Sen. <strong>Ralph Hise</strong>, R-Mitchell, led the effort.&nbsp;</p> <figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full is-resized\"><a href=\"https://carolinapublicpress.org/subscribe-to-carolina-public-press-newsletters/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\" noopener\"><img src=\"https://carolinapublicpress.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/CPP-SUBSCRIBE-CTA-3-2026.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-82702\" style=\"object-fit:cover;width:300px;height:250px\" /></a></figure> <p>“The motivation behind this redraw is simple and singular: draw a new map that will bring an additional Republican seat to the North Carolina congressional delegation,” Hise said at the time. “Republicans hold a razor-thin margin in the United States House of Representatives. If Democrats flip four seats in the upcoming midterm elections, they will take control of the House, and torpedo president Trump's agenda.”</p> <p>Don Davis was first elected to Congress in 2022, but came with a much longer resume. A graduate of the Air Force Academy, he achieved the rank of Air Force Captain, taught at East Carolina; became the mayor of Snow Hill, his hometown in Greene County; and served multiple terms in the state Senate.</p> <p>In his 2024 reelection bid, Davis won the First Congressional District race to represent northeastern <a href=\"https://www.carolinapublicpress.org\">North Carolina</a> by fewer than two percentage points. </p> <p>When the national mid-decade redistricting frenzy began, state Republicans saw the narrow margin as an opportunity. The much redder Third Congressional District runs along NC-1’s southern border, so they thought they could afford to exchange some of NC-1’s bluer sections with NC-3’s redder ones.&nbsp;</p> <p>But the effort, and the transparency behind it, backfired. By the end of election night, Davis was ahead of Buckhout, with 50.07% of the vote and a more than 10,000 vote edge, leading the Republican challenger to concede. </p> <p>Davis’ race wasn’t the only Republican disappointment following that gerrymander, which itself was built on earlier gerrymandering.&nbsp;</p> <p>Democratic farmer <strong>Jamie Ager</strong> <a href=\"https://carolinapublicpress.org/82886/ager-mountain-democrat-campaign-in-far-west-flips-longtime-gop-seat/\">defeated</a> State Rep. <strong>Jennifer Balkcom</strong>, R-Henderson, in Western North Carolina’s 11th Congressional District.&nbsp;</p> <p>In the Fifth Congressional District, Democrat <strong>Chuck Hubbard</strong> appeared to have unseated longtime Republican incumbent <strong>Virginia Foxx</strong> in a very narrow race. The pair are separated by 99 votes, close enough to call for a recount. Late-arriving absentee ballots and provisional ballots could potentially alter that vote total.</p> <p>In the Sixth Congressional District, Democrat <strong>Cyril Jefferson</strong> had a slightly larger lead, with 51.14% of the vote over US Rep. <strong>Addison McDowell</strong>.</p> <p>In the Ninth Congressional District, Democrat <strong>Richard Ojeda</strong> bested <strong>Richard Hudson</strong> with 51.11% of the vote as well. In the 13th Congressional District, Democrat <strong>Paul Barringer</strong> won against US Rep. <strong>Brad Knott</strong> with 50.5% of the vote.</p> <p>In a major upset, Democrat <strong>Lakesha Womack</strong> earned the majority of the vote in the 14th Congressional District against US Rep. <strong>Tim Moore</strong>. Moore, who trails Womack by nearly 6,000 votes, or about 2.22 percentage points, had been one of the most influential Republicans in the state, long serving as NC House Speaker before running for Congress.</p> <p>The only US House seats Republicans appear to have kept are NC-3, represented by US Rep. <strong>Greg Murphy</strong>, NC-7, represented by US Rep. <strong>David Rouzer</strong>, NC-8, represented by US Rep. <strong>Mark Harris</strong>, and NC-10, represented by US Rep. <strong>Pat Harrigan</strong>. And the vote count in Murphy's race appears too close to call, with only a 233-vote edge, enough to be affected by late-arriving absentee ballots and provisional votes, and likely subject to a recount.</p> <p>The final vote tally — and apparent victors — could change between now and the county canvass, when the official results are tallied. Election officials still need to consider whether to count provisional ballots, and absentee and provisional voters who need to fix any issues have a few days to do so. </p> <p>Losing candidates also may file election protests, alleging irregularities in the process, or ask for recounts.</p> <p>Under state law, losing US House candidates may request a recount if the difference in votes between the first- and second-place candidates is less than 1% of the total votes cast in the race.&nbsp;</p> <figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full is-resized\"><a href=\"https://carolinapublicpress.fundjournalism.org/donate/?amount=15&amp;campaign=701VH000014ry3GYAQ&amp;frequency=monthly\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\" noopener\"><img src=\"https://carolinapublicpress.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Web-ad-GENERAL-300x250-1.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-82914\" style=\"object-fit:cover;width:300px;height:250px\" /></a></figure> <p>Some winning Democrats were optimistic about their chances weeks before the election, including Ojeda and Hubbard. They pointed to their opponents, who began running campaign ads for the first cycle in a long time.&nbsp;</p> <p>However, most political watchers did not expect them to win, much less several of the other Democrats who flipped seats, if the vote tallies hold.&nbsp;</p> <p>The newly elected Democrats were part of a national wave in which Republicans were swept from office. They will have the opportunity to go to Washington as part of a congressional majority.</p> <p><em>Editor's note: This is a developing story and will be updated.</em></p> <p>This <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://carolinapublicpress.org/82900/gop-gerrymander-backfires-in-nc-as-states-congressional-map-goes-blue/\">article</a> first appeared on <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://carolinapublicpress.org\">Carolina Public Press</a> and is republished here under a <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nd/4.0/\">Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License</a>.&nbsp;<img src=\"https://i0.wp.com/carolinapublicpress.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/cropped-cropped-CPPlogo_icon_RGB.png?fit=150%2C150&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" style=\"width:1em;height:1em;\"></p> <img id=\"republication-tracker-tool-source\" src=\"https://carolinapublicpress.org/?republication-pixel=true&post=82900\" style=\"width:1px;height:1px;\"><script> PARSELY = { autotrack: false, onload: function() { PARSELY.beacon.trackPageView({ url: \"https://carolinapublicpress.org/82900/gop-gerrymander-backfires-in-nc-as-states-congressional-map-goes-blue/\", urlref: window.location.href }); } } </script> <script id=\"parsely-cfg\" src=\"//cdn.parsely.com/keys/carolinapublicpress.org/p.js\"></script> Copy to Clipboard Sarah Michels Sarah Michels is a staff writer for Carolina Public Press specializing in coverage of North Carolina politics and elections. She is based in Raleigh. Email her at [email protected] to contact her. More by Sarah Michels Latest News Dissolution decisions loom for two small NC towns November 10, 2026 November 10, 2026 Bringing maternal healthcare to rural NC. Grant-powered community health centers offer hope. November 9, 2026 November 9, 2026 After the deluge. Making sense of the altered political landscape in NC. November 6, 2026 November 6, 2026 Seismic ripples in NC mountain politics. Foxx trails by 99 votes. November 6, 2026 November 9, 2026 Mecklenburg’s Jail North operating again, but solution for juveniles still unclear November 5, 2026 November 5, 2026 Read More Latest News Support investigative journalism in North Carolina With the support of readers like you, we provide thoughtfully researched articles for a more informed and connected community. This is your chance to support credible, community-based, public-service journalism. Please join us! Donate Now This is not a paywall! You can keep reading Carolina Public Press, your nonprofit source for trustworthy, in-depth reporting that holds power to account in North Carolina, for free. We deliver independent, investigative news coverage of the topics that shape our state and local communities. Rather than just telling stories about the powerful, we tell stories that empower the public. Also free, our daily, weekly and special newsletters. Subscribe today to begin receiving. Subscribe now Link Copy link",
