        "pageDescription": "A five-month investigation by Investigate Europe and media partners in nine countries reveals the harsh realities facing some working on the continent&#x27;s vast AI infrastructure developments.",
        "evidenceText": "Electrocutions, ‘mental terror’ and withheld wages: the exploited workers building Europe’s data centres | Investigate Europe Technology / Blackout Electrocutions, ‘mental terror’ and withheld wages: The exploited workers building Europe’s data centres 30 October 2026 Mei-Ling McNamara Jan Daalder Credit: Spoovio Technology / Blackout Electrocutions, ‘mental terror’ and withheld wages: The exploited workers building Europe’s data centres Credit: Spoovio Mei-Ling McNamara Jan Daalder 30 October 2026 About Blackout Big Tech is selling a vision for a future fuelled by ubiquitous artificial intelligence, and Europe’s leaders have bought in wholesale. This investigation lifts the veil of secrecy hanging over Europe's booming data centre industry. EN A five-month investigation by Investigate Europe and media partners in nine countries reveals the harsh realities facing some working on the continent's vast AI infrastructure developments. About Blackout Sandu Dumitrescu* checked his watch. He was winding down the last 15 minutes of his shift at the Google data centre site in Saint Ghislain, Belgium in March, when the call came in. He had just finished performing electrical checks on the site’s hall fans when his manager communicated a final request: could he run two quick tests right before he left? The 34-year-old electrical tester had to make a decision: he had already worked almost 10 hours. Yet in the highly pressurised environment of GBL-6, Google’s sixth data centre in the vast €5 billion campus, such last-minute requests were normal. “I had no choice,” Sandu says. “People have been fired after saying no.” He believed that supervisors on the site, where Irish company Dornan Engineering oversaw his contractor, would not take a refusal well. Relying on his week-by-week contract and with hopes to call his wife and young son back in Romania that night, he resigned himself to finish the job. The task was one he had done many times before and involved a series of tests measuring the safety and integrity of the electrical wiring. It should have taken about an hour. Sandu says the request came to him verbally, via an intermediary. A protective suit was locked away in an equipment room. The trip would have added another 15 minutes to the job, he says, and people were waiting. In the rush, Sandu decided not to retrieve the suit. Once in the voltage room, Sandu says the managers confirmed the lines had been de-energised. In the first round, everything went as planned. Then, on the second test, while holding insulated tongs, he linked the cables. But this time, the lines were live. The blast was immediate and catastrophic. In milliseconds, 400 volts and 12,000 amps hit Sandu’s face and across the right side of his body. A colleague who was down below the platform and witnessed the incident told Investigate Europe that he “heard a loud bang, saw a lot of smoke, and saw [Sandu] screaming in pain.” “I went completely blind,” Sandu recalls. “I told the managers three times that I could not see.” But no managers answered, he claims. According to the witness who called for help, untangling Sandu from his harness in a mass of blackened flesh, the managers had run off, while Sandu writhed in pain on the ground. Workers rushed to carry Sandu to an ambulance. When doctors received him in the Grand Hôpital de Charleroi, he was placed in an induced coma for five days. Hospital records seen by Investigate Europe show that the arc flash electrocution had burned 12 per cent of Sandu’s body, with second-degree burns to the face and neck as well as third-degree burns across his right shoulder and fingers. Photographs show scorched ears and swollen raw lips, with his arm a mess of hematoma and clotted skin. When Sandu woke in the intensive care unit a week later after surgery, his wife was at his bedside. He says his entire body felt like a “white-hot blister…. It was so much pain. The human heart cannot comprehend it,” he recalls. Sandu photographed in October 2026, months after the arc flash electrocution burned 12 per cent of his body. Credit: Kevin Faingnaert/Investigate Europe Two days after waking from his coma, representatives from Google, Dornan and his Dutch recruitment agency, Wanted, came to visit. Sandu says that while he lay bandaged in the hospital bed, Google and Dornan each took a statement from him about the accident. Sandu claims the Google employee told him he was lucky to be alive. Sandu’s electrocution is both brutal and harrowing. Yet to date, no fines or sanctions are known to have been issued. Belgium’s federal labour inspectorate closed its investigation into the accident at the end of October and found no wrongdoing. Sandu says, however, that he was never interviewed directly by the labour authorities. Despite making formal requests for his accident report from Wanted and Google, he is yet to receive it. A month after his accident, the recruiter messaged Sandu to see how was. They then asked: “[The company] is recruiting more testers (perhaps you know people?).” A Google spokesperson told Investigate Europe: “Our supplier code of conduct and contracts require all suppliers to comply with local labour laws and uphold safe, healthy working conditions. We maintain active dialogue with union leaders and trade groups, and are committed to providing transparent information about our workplace.” Dornan said it supported Belgian authorities with their investigation. A spokesperson added: “We were deeply concerned by this incident and have supported the individual and his family throughout his recovery. We are committed to maintaining safe working conditions, complying with applicable labour laws, and continually learning to help prevent incidents like this from happening again.” A Wanted spokesperson said: “As an employer we can, will and should be held accountable by the proper authorities. Although we agree that freedom of speech and press is a great asset we cannot comment on the content besides the fact that your statements or claims are not correct.” Google's €6 billion data centre development in Saint Ghislain, Belgium. Credit: Shutterstock A five-month investigation led by Investigate Europe with media partners in nine countries into working conditions on data centre sites across Sweden, Norway, Belgium and the Netherlands reveals that Sandu’s case is far from unique. In extensive interviews with migrant workers at flagship sites where large US tech companies are operating, reporters found evidence of debilitating injuries, squalid living conditions, an unreported infectious outbreak, unpaid wages and exploitative employment practices. Workers at one major Microsoft project described a culture of fear and intimidation where construction managers “rule on mental terror”. The testimonies are supported by dozens of health records, pay slips, phone messages, photographs and videos shared with reporters. The investigation found a recurring pattern where tech companies and their many Irish construction partners embed US-style working practices, often in defiance of national working norms. In Italy, Denmark, Finland, Sweden, Belgium, the Netherlands and Norway unions told reporters they had difficulty accessing certain sites to speak with workers and document conditions. One union representative in Denmark said some sites are “more closed than a state prison”. The European data centre industry is experiencing an infrastructure surge the continent has not seen in decades, dispatching tens of thousands of migrant workers to make its vision of tech sovereignty a reality. “ I wake up at two in the morning, and I can hear rats eating, moving in the walls. This house is full of rats. ” — Adrian*, a Romanian worker at a Microsoft data centre project in Sweden A data centre construction site can contain up to 2,000 workers and dozens of contractors, with up to a quarter of a million people working on sites across Europe every day. The industry is one of big budgets and even bigger secrecy. Construction firms are often required to keep their tech client secret, while contractors and intermediaries are also typically hidden from view. At the same time, construction continues to rank as the most dangerous economic sector in the European Union. According to the latest Eurostat data , the sector recorded 776 deaths in 2024, representing nearly a quarter of all fatal workplace accidents in Europe. By 2030, the industry is forecasting €100 billion of investments into European data centres. With over 2,300 facilities rapidly altering the physical and energy landscape of Europe, and hundreds more planned or in construction , the industry’s priorities appear abundantly clear: where to build, how fast to build, and which companies are prepared to build under budget. ‘Thank you for working safely’ “I wake up at two in the morning, and I can hear rats eating, moving in the walls. This house is full of rats. When I put pizza in the oven, they are running in the walls near me,” says Adrian*, a 45-year-old construction worker on a Microsoft data centre site near Gävle in Sweden. A video Adrian shared with Investigate Europe shows a large rat scurrying across a hallway and into an electrical storage room. In a string of text messages sent in August, Adrian begs the housing manager to get rid of them. The reply comes back: “Brother, I understand. But I have to wait for these other companies to come in and remove them, I can’t come in alone and just pull them out of the walls…” Text messages between a construction worker and Benbau manager about rats in their work-issued housing. Credit: Spoovio If you head northwest out of Gävle, a town two hours from Stockholm, past timber-framed cottages and dense pine forests, a sign points you to a smooth road rolled out specifically for Microsoft. The remote clearing is part of a 130-hectare 33.7 billion Swedish Krona (€3 billion) expansion plan started in June 2024 to scale up Microsoft’s AI data centre infrastructure in the country. Signs for Collen Construction line the drive, their banners draped across the large metal perimeter. Collen is among many Irish contractors overseeing data centre build-outs across Europe. Hired by big tech clients to deliver projects on a strict timeline, these years-long projects manage a syndicate of other firms. In the distance, beyond the security fencing, an airport-sized building looms, reflectionless and inscrutable. In the foreground, a bright red turnstile welcomes workers in white lettering: ‘Thank you for working safely’. One of Collen’s subcontractors, the Irish-owned construction company Benbau, employs hundreds of workers across Europe, including in Sweden, to work in areas such as site management, concrete and carpentry. The workers are housed 20 minutes off-site in modular housing managed by Benbau. For months, there has been a growing sense of outrage among Adrian and his colleagues working for Benbau on the site. They have reported vermin infestations, broken ventilation systems, and lack of heating during the sub-zero Swedish winters, but they claim the problems remain unresolved. One 50-year-old Romanian worker, who is diabetic, shared images of his toes that had lost circulation after wearing what he claims were poor work boots issued by Benbau. He had to have his toe amputated several months ago. Adrian says it has been months since he first reported the rat infestation, and the rodents have multiplied inside the 40-room housing unit. Another video appears to take the viewer down a narrow hallway into a shared kitchen and lounge, where security cameras are mounted in the corners. Inside the crowded housing, workers say the stress runs high: 24 people to a kitchen, 14 men to a single bathroom, with a boarded up hole where the rats crawled through and a bathroom window that is sealed shut. Photographs from inside the modular housing supplied to construction workers for Bebaue at the Microsfot development in Gävle. Credit: Spoovio Such crowded confines can make the transmission of disease more likely. In February this year, one Romanian worker on the site became extremely ill. Following several trips to the hospital, it was confirmed he had active tuberculosis, Investigate Europe was shown the individual’s medical record confirming the positive diagnosis. Shortly after, others showed similar symptoms. After the first worker who contracted the disease had left for hospital, workers in the accommodation became worried about possibly also getting the disease. One worker who had recently moved into a new room said: “I bought bedding myself, washed the mattress with chlorine, scrubbed the walls, left the window open for a long time - it was very cold.” Benbau initially informed only a few men who shared close quarters with the first infected worker and then told the men testing was optional, according to seven workers who spoke with Investigate Europe. Freedom of information requests to Sweden’s national work authorities and Public Health Agency revealed no outbreak had been reported. The county medical officer in Gävle said that only one case was reported in March and shared with the national health authority, who also confirmed the case. In Sweden, tuberculosis is a notifiable disease that is subject to mandatory contact tracing, which workers claim did not occur. Eight Benbau workers living in the same housing and union representatives told reporters that they believed at least 10 to 12 men also had the infectious disease. Mihai*, a 60-year-old worker, also tested positive for latent tuberculosis, hospital records shared with Investigate Europe reveal. He says that Benbau offered to transfer him to another Microsoft site in Denmark, along with others he claims were also infected. Considering his age, Mihai says he decided against travelling. Then, in May 2026, witnesses who lived in the housing where some were confirmed positive with latent tuberculosis say a group of vans arrived to relocate workers to the Denmark site. Two witnesses say that many of those who left that day had previously tested positive for tuberculosis. Some men were diagnosed with latent tuberculosis, yet told reporters they experienced symptoms weeks before. Others who left that day, including an entire crew who worked in concrete for Benbau, had tuberculosis symptoms, the witnesses claim. In total approximately 40 men left for Denmark that day, according to witnesses. The Danish construction union 3F Byggegruppen says their records show around 45 posted workers arriving at the Microsoft site in Høje Taastrup around this time. No further record of what happened to any of the men is available, despite efforts from 3F to reach Benbau employees. Signs for the vast Microsoft data centre development in Gävle. Credit: Mei-Ling McNamara Irish firm Collen Construction is the general contractor on the site. Credit: Mei-Ling McNamara ‘They rule on mental terror’ Workers say for the first few years, the working environment at Collen and Benbau was not too difficult. But in the last two years, with the accelerated build-up of AI infrastructure, they claim the mood has shifted. “It now feels like we are in the army service,” one Romanian worker told Investigate Europe in a video call with other colleagues. “They rule on terror, mental terror.” Workers allege they are regularly subject to harassment and shifting rules. Many say the language barrier creates ongoing problems. To monitor workplace safety, the general contractor, Collen, introduced a football-style violation system. These orders, implemented by Benbau, consist of ‘yellow cards’ and ‘red cards’ for perceived infractions. ‘Carded’ workers describe being interrogated by managers for hours, then made to write letters admitting wrongdoing to workplace incidents even if they feel they have not done anything wrong. “We are forced to sit in a room and confess to things we had not done,” one worker claims. Following a violation, they are then sent to attend a ‘reinduction’ training in English, and not their native Romanian. Under another programme, called ‘Good Save’, workers are required to report site or worker issues twice a week. Failure to do so results in threats of further pay deductions and extra training, texts seen by Investigate Europe show. “These are control mechanisms,” another worker claims. “On Friday there’s the first message: ‘You have by 12 o’clock to finish your [reports]’. Then after 12 the threats begin: ‘You need to do your [report], do you want to work on Saturday?’ Then the last threat is: ‘We are going to cut the [missing report] out of your wages.’” Investigate Europe has seen no evidence that Collen was aware of the alleged practice of deducting pay from workers. A former manager from Romania who worked for Benbau, who had decent housing and a wife and young child on site, told Investigate Europe he “refused to participate in the interrogation sessions” as a translator. He believes the written confessions were “designed to humiliate” other colleagues. “In that atmosphere, I couldn’t continue seeing people working like this,“ he says. “I did not want to be afraid of someone who is forcing men to work 13 hours a day. I talked with my wife and I said: ‘This is the moment we need to go back to our country.’” Text messages received by Romanian workers in Gävle about the 'Good Saves' programme implemented by management. Credit: Spoovio European unionisation encounters US Big Tech The living conditions and working climate forms only part of the story. Over 30 workers shared hundreds of pay slips with Investigate Europe, many show them working up to 60 hour weeks with no overtime pay, in an apparent violation of Swedish labour law. Some workers claim they have received no paid holiday as required under Swedish law for five years. Solidariska byggare (Workers in Solidarity), a union group, has tried to recover years of backpay for migrant workers at the Gävle site, who the union claims have been paid up to 30 per cent less than their Swedish counterparts. Historically, Sweden has operated under a self-regulated labour market system where trade unions and employer organisations set wages and working conditions through collective agreements rather than state-mandated laws. Unions now say over the years this model is beginning to not only erode, but crack wide open. As data centre sites expand, the ever-lengthening chain of subcontractors involved becomes more challenging to track. Documents obtained from a freedom of information request to Sweden’s Work Environment Authority reveal 148 firms working under Collen at the Gävle site, including 28 direct subcontractors. One subcontractor can have more than a dozen firms beneath them. In Sweden, it is customary, though not legally required, for clients to demand a collective agreement clause in their public procurement calls for tender. Yet when Collen broke ground in 2019 for the Microsoft Gävle site, no such agreement was in place. Soon after, many of its subcontractors, including Benbau, also passed over including collective agreements with unions. Construction workers in Gävle listen to a union representative in Romanian and English. Credit: Mei-Ling McNamara Leaflets at a meeting held by Solidariska byggare for workers on the Gävle site. Credit: Mei-Ling McNamara With the accelerated influx of US multinationals building in Europe, many companies working under them are willing to forgo collective agreements entirely, according to unions. With a foreign workers unfamiliar with the laws, and the contractors setting the terms, inequalities can arise quickly. “It is a very clear picture,” says Pelle Sunvisson, a union leader at Solidariksa byggare. He describes what he sees when distributing leaflets outside the Microsoft site in Gävle. “At 4 o’clock there is a wave of Swedish workers. They are typically electricians working with Swedish companies that have Swedish collective agreements. If y",
