        "pageDescription": "Listen to Columbia Journalism Review",
        "evidenceText": "The Kicker - Podcast - Apple Podcasts The Kicker Columbia Journalism Review 4.5 (79) News Semimonthly The Kicker is a podcast on the media and the world today. It comes out twice a month, hosted by Megan Greenwell and produced by Amanda Darrach for the Columbia Journalism Review. It is available wherever you get your podcasts, including Apple Podcasts, Spotify, and YouTube. Latest Episode Newest to Oldest Oldest to Newest Episodes Oct 29 Magazines Are Back, and They’re Newsletters In early 2024, scores of journalists fled Substack over the company’s refusal to ban Nazi newsletters. Smaller newsletter companies saw an opening. Beehiiv, which was founded in 2021 by former Morning Brew employees, became one of the trendy upstarts, luring journalists such as Oliver Darcy and Ryan Broderick. Since then, Preeya Goenka—who has spent her career in email and joined Beehiiv in 2022—has expanded the company’s offerings for journalists. Those accepted into Beehiiv’s media collective pay no monthly fee for its service and receive legal support, a healthcare stipend, and audience-engagement help for two years. (It’s worth noting that journalists who aren’t part of that program have expressed frustration with Beehiiv’s rising monthly cost.) Most media collective members are still solo operators, but a growing number are running full newsrooms: Darcy now employs three journalists, while L.A. Material has a ten-person staff covering news across Los Angeles. I interviewed Goenka about how journalists fit into Beehiiv’s business strategy, who should consider starting a newsletter, and why Substack is definitely not the competition. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes:  Beehiiv Media Collective L.A. Material Status  Garbage Day Credits: Host: Megan Greenwell Producer: Amanda Darrach 45m Oct 15 California’s Big Bet on Government-Funded News Gavin Newsom, California’s governor, had Matt Pearce sweating. Until the day before the signing deadline, Pearce didn’t know if the biggest piece of legislation he had ever worked on—one that creates a new standard for content-neutral government funding of local news—would become law or not.  And then, on September 30, Newsom signed the bill, establishing a groundbreaking precedent for state support of local outlets. Soon, newsrooms will get a twenty-thousand-dollar tax credit every year for each of their first five journalists, plus fifteen thousand more for each journalist after that. If a newsroom opens a new position, that will bring in another fifteen thousand on top of the base credit. Everyone from a solo newsletter writer to Alden Global Capital will be eligible for the money as long as they meet some basic criteria about covering news responsibly for a California audience.  I interviewed Pearce, a longtime Los Angeles Times reporter who switched to government advocacy after taking a buyout, about the long, slow road toward (partial) government solutions to the local news crisis. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes: “California Roadmap,” Matt Pearce. “AB-2222 Personal Income Tax Law and Corporation Tax Law: credits: local news outlets: business expense deduction: excessive employee remuneration,” California Legislature. “Explainer: California’s Community NEWS Act (AB 2222),” Rebuild Local News. “Local Journalist Employment Credit,” Rebuild Local News. “Supporting Local Reporting in Illinois: Early Lessons and Opportunities,” Rebuild Local News. Credits: Host: Megan Greenwell Producer: Amanda Darrach 57m Oct 1 A Victim of the ‘Elon Musk Playbook’ For me, and for most of my fellow millennials in the journalism industry, the idea of spending more than two decades in the same job seems unfathomable. It’s not that I wouldn’t like to, but I’ve never made it more than four years without being laid off or experiencing circumstances so unbearable that I have no choice but to quit.  Alison Gerber arrived at the Chattanooga Times Free Press in 2003 as an assistant metro editor. (I first met her the following year, when I landed in Chattanooga for my first real newspaper internship, though she wasn’t my editor.) In 2011, she was named the top editor.  She didn’t leave that job until this summer, when the newspaper was sold and the new owners cleaned house, firing her and more than fifty employees. Several others quit soon after, followed by a smaller second round of layoffs. The new CEO, a local freight executive and luxury magazine publisher named Craig Fuller (who bought the paper with his father, Max), has said his goal is to “follow the Elon Musk playbook” by cutting out “massive levels of bureaucracy” (a characterization of her newsroom Gerber strongly disputes) in order to make the paper profitable. In CJR this week, Riddhi Setty wrote about Fuller’s arrival in the newsroom, where he has prohibited reporters from disclosing their use of AI in articles and has offered hundred-dollar monthly reimbursement for employees who buy GLP-1s through one of his other companies. I talked to Gerber about her time leading the TFP, which led to three Pulitzer nominations and, for the past two years, recognition as the best large newspaper in Tennessee. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes: Alison Gerber, “Goodbye to Mark Kennedy—and to an era,” Chattanooga Times Free Press.  Alison Gerber, “A Times Free Press reporter traveled to Mexico for a story. Here’s why,” Chattanooga Times Free Press. Craig Fuller, “Plans for the Times Free Press put Chattanoogans, families first,” Chattanooga Times Free Press.  “Report: Local Group Buying Chattanooga Times Free Press from Arkansas Owners,” Chattanoogan.com. Riddhi Setty, “The New Boss at The Chattanooga Times Free Press,” Columbia Journalism Review. Credits: Host, Megan Greenwell Producer, Amanda Darrach 49m Sep 17 Ruth Ann Harnisch on life as the fairy godmother of newsletters, podcasts, and blogs. If you had lots of money to give away, what kind of journalism would you fund? City hall reporting? Big-swing investigative projects? Detailed coverage of your favorite sports team? The magazine of every narrative writer’s dreams?  Over nearly three decades as a professional philanthropist, Ruth Ann Harnisch has funded many kinds of journalism, several of which fit none of those buckets. Deez Links, Delia Cai’s reliably hilarious “culture and online fixation report.” Oldster Magazine, Sari Botton’s excellent newsletter about aging. The Rumpus, the storied literary magazine recently relaunched by Roxane Gay and Debbie Millman. One estimate hassaid Harnisch ashas been a founding supporter of more than a hundred newsletters—the vast majority of them as no-strings-attached gifts, not investments. The common thread? Publications the world would be worse off without, from the serious to the delightfully silly. I interviewed Harnisch about her road to philanthropy, how she makes her donation decisions, and why more people don’t follow her philosophy of giving. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Debbie Millman, “Design Matters: Ruth Ann Harnisch,” PRINT. “Ruth Ann Harnisch,” IMDb. “About the Harnisch Foundation.”  Tracy Gary, Inspired Philanthropy. Host, Megan Greenwell Producer, Amanda Darrach 51m Sep 3 So Are Nonfiction Books Dead or Not? Anna Sproul-Latimer, a literary agent, on what a shrinking market means for journalists. After a boom time for nonfiction books during the pandemic, sales have been flat or down for multiple years running. Even veteran nonfiction authors are having trouble landing book deals, and those who do are getting less money. For ambitious journalists, that’s bad news; writing a book has historically been one of the best ways to level up one’s career and supplement low newsroom salaries. Anna Sproul-Latimer—a veteran agent who represents me, among many other journalists, and writes a terrific newsletter about the publishing industry—doesn’t dispute that things are rough out there. But nor does she buy the narrative, popularized by a viral Wall Street Journal article in May, that the industry is in an irreversible death spiral. There’s still an appetite for reported nonfiction books, she says; the problem is that too many people misunderstand who’s buying them and what that means for what writers should pitch.  I interviewed Sproul-Latimer about the reality of the publishing industry, how she’s seen things change over the course of her nearly twenty years as an agent, and how journalists should think about making book-writing part of their careers. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes: Anna Sproul-Latimer, “Is Nonfiction Dead?” How to Glow in the Dark.  Anna Sproul-Latimer, “How I Evaluate Submissions,” How to Glow in the Dark. Anna Sproul-Latimer, “A Note on Our Daddy Issues,” How to Glow in the Dark. James B. Stewart, Follow the Story. OK Go, “This Too Shall Pass.” Host, Megan Greenwell Producer, Amanda Darrach 54m Aug 20 Kenya Hunter took a terrifying leap out of journalism. She couldn’t be happier. One of my favorite dive-bar conversations with fellow journalists is “What would you do if you couldn’t do this anymore?” We’ve all thought about it; the tough economics of our industry haven’t left us any choice. It’s such an interesting window into people’s psyches: friends have told me about secret dreams of going to medical or law school, opening a restaurant, running a bed-and-breakfast, teaching kindergartners, and more. (Interestingly, no one has ever told me they would go into PR, which I assume is the most common destination for former journalists by a mile.) For years, I counted myself a fan of Kenya Hunter’s work covering health access for Capital B and the Associated Press. Now I admire her for another reason: she took the leap to find a career she loved more than journalism. In 2025, after her contract at the AP wasn’t renewed, she did get one of those PR jobs. But she also trained as a doula. Today she provides postpartum care and nutrition counseling to new parents and offers support around abortions to those who need it, with a particular focus on Black people in Atlanta, where she lives. I talked to Kenya about the transition from writing about healthcare to providing it, what she learned from reporting on race in jobs that weren’t “race reporting,” and how she thinks about her future after so many years of focusing on journalism. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes: Examining the Lack of Diversity at the Maggie L. Walker Governor’s School: A Three-Part Series, Kenya Hunter, Richmond Times-Dispatch. Why Black Doulas Say They Can’t Fix Georgia’s Maternal Mortality Crisis Alone, Kenya Hunter, Capital B.  More Wellstar Closures Bring Dwindling Health Care Options, Kenya Hunter, Capital B.  Hundreds of places in the US said racism was a public health crisis. What’s changed? Kenya Hunter, Associated Press. Finally I Can Talk, Kenya Hunter. Credits: Host: Megan Greenwell Producer: Amanda Darrach 46m Aug 6 Twenty Years of Food Blogs: Smitten Kitchen’s Deb Perelman has never needed AI to develop a recipe. A friend recently posted a photo on Instagram of a giant zucchini harvested from her backyard in Chicago, with a caption that she needed recipes that use a ton of zucchini. I knew exactly where to send her. The best food blog on the internet, Smitten Kitchen, has thirty-seven zucchini recipes in its archives, ranging from quesadillas to latkes to chocolate bread. I sent my friend the link to a zucchini butter spaghetti recipe from five years ago—an internet classic. This summer, Smitten Kitchen celebrates its twentieth anniversary. Since Deb Perelman started the site, in 2006, she’s weathered wave after wave of changes to how food writing on the internet works. A former art therapist and (briefly) tech reporter, she started writing online before Instagram or TikTok existed, and before Twitter and YouTube were part of the job. She was part of an ecosystem of home-cooking blogs, all of which would link to one another, and most of which faded away years ago. SEO boomed, then crashed as a result of Google algorithm changes. Now many people get their recipes from ChatGPT. Yet Smitten Kitchen keeps going, with Perelman still doing almost everything herself. I learned to cook from Smitten Kitchen, so I was thrilled to talk to Perelman about what’s changed and what hasn’t during her twenty years in food blogging. (Her passionate monologue about the rise of AI in internet writing is an all-timer.) Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes: About Smitten Kitchen, Deb Perelman, Smitten Kitchen. Frequently asked questions, Deb Perelman, Smitten Kitchen.  Spring panzanella, Deb Perelman, Smitten Kitchen.  Zucchini butter spaghetti, Deb Perelman, Smitten Kitchen.  Credits: Host: Megan Greenwell Producer: Amanda Darrach 54m Jul 23 What Went Wrong: A first-time editor in chief reflects on a startup that didn’t work When The Barbed Wire debuted, in 2024, it promised “something different: a site that combines the best of savvy, new-model online journalism with Texas’s deep—and very old—association with progressive ideas.” Its goal, Olivia Messer, the editor in chief, wrote at the time, was to reach the millions of Texans who weren’t reading their local newspaper or any statewide news outlet, and to do it in a way that created a new hybrid for-profit/nonprofit revenue model for journalism.  The Barbed Wire lasted for a little under two years, publishing 725 stories. This spring, it ran out of money. All five journalists on staff lost their jobs. (Two occasional newsletters remain, written by freelancers.) The hybrid business model, it turned out, had made it more difficult to bring in philanthropy money; the roster of paid subscribers never grew big enough to keep the operation afloat. It’s easier, and more fun, to focus on what’s working in journalism: new startups, growing newsrooms, initiatives at major outlets. But I also think it’s important to dissect what hasn’t worked. In our conversation, Messer was admirably candid about what factors brought down The Barbed Wire. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes: Goodbye for Now, Olivia Messer, Bluesky. Our Independent Newsroom Was Built on a Ferocious, Delusional Sense of Hope. Help Us Keep It Going, Olivia Messer, The Barbed Wire.  Are You There, Texas? It’s Me, Olivia, Olivia Messer, The Barbed Wire. ‘Gods in the Building’: How the Texas Senate Buries Sexual Harassment Complaints and Enables Bad Actors, Olivia Messer and Cara Kelly, Texas Monthly.  ‘This Is Not Hazing. This Is Rape’: Inside a Texas Town’s Football Nightmare, Olivia Messer, Daily Beast.  Credits: Host: Megan Greenwell Producer: Amanda Darrach 57m See All (329) Trailer Trailer Coming Soon: The Unraveling of Ozy Media Starting on December 9, the Columbia Journalism Review presents a special three-part series of The Kicker: “The Unraveling of Ozy Media,” on the dramatic rise and fall of Carlos Watson, the cofounder of the digital media company Ozy. In 2023, Watson was charged with fraud after it was revealed that one of his partners had masqueraded as a YouTube executive, during a call with potential investors.  But Ozy’s failure is about more than one man. It’s a story about an era of profligate growth and overpromises in digital media that have left many wondering whether there’s any business model that will work for journalism today. Join hosts Josh Hersh and Susie Banikarim as they follow Watson’s trial and ask how much of digital media was built on a lie. 2m Ratings & Reviews 4.5 out of 5 79 Ratings Economic Voice of Reason 04/12/2025 JTI O1 I’ve never heard of this podcast before but I really enjoyed the most recent episode, “Kai Ryssdal Was America’s Economic Voice of Reason This Week.” Straight talk about an important subject from educated, thoughtful people. Who could ask for more. I’ll be listening to more episodes in the future. Superb 12/27/2024 EmRoo7 This is a Peabody-worthy podcast - a phantasmagorical tale - that’s real - told well with humor and sympathy, to a point. The format suits the listener with a wonderful Q&A format with mind boggling detail. Susie Banikarim is a superstar and Josh Hersh is a fantastic foil. I great listen without the usual tedious podcast repetition. Engrossing 12/27/2024 arp83 Great listen - very well done. Ozy Media 12/13/2024 kbm422 Really enjoying the episodes about the downfall of Ozy Media - thank you! About The Kicker is a podcast on the media and the world today. It comes out twice a month, hosted by Megan Greenwell and produced by Amanda Darrach for the Columbia Journalism Review. It is available wherever you get your podcasts, including Apple Podcasts, Spotify, and YouTube. Information Creator Columbia Journalism Review Years Active 2016 - 2026 Episodes 329 Rating Explicit Copyright © 2026 Columbia Journalism Review Show Website The Kicker You Might Also Like Politics Politics Updated Semiweekly News Commentary News Commentary Updated Weekly Politics Politics Updated Semiweekly Politics Politics Updated Semiweekly Society & Culture Society & Culture Updated Semiweekly Daily News Daily News Updated Daily Politics Politics Updated 1d ago To play explicit episodes, sign in. Sign In Stay up to date with this show Sign in or sign up to follow shows, save episodes, and get the latest updates. Sign In Embed Episode Copy Embed Code Select a country or region Africa, Middle East, and India See All Algeria Angola Armenia Azerbaijan Bahrain Benin Botswana Burkina Faso Cameroun Cape Verde Chad Côte d’Ivoire Congo, The Democratic Republic Of The Egypt Eswatini Gabon Gambia Ghana Guinea-Bissau India Iraq Israel Jordan Kenya Kuwait Lebanon Liberia Libya Madagascar Malawi Mali Mauritania Mauritius Morocco Mozambique Namibia Niger (English) Nigeria Oman Qatar Congo, Republic of Rwanda São Tomé and Príncipe Saudi Arabia Senegal Seychelles Sierra Leone South Africa 