 ͏ ‌ ͏ ‌ ͏ ‌ ͏ ‌ ͏ ‌ ͏ ‌ ͏ ‌ ͏ ‌ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ ­ View this email in your browser THIS MONTH’S TL;DR : Announcing our new Creator + Newsroom Studio ; Our Teen Creator Network explores trending topics; The MediaWise Weekly has educators set for lesson plans; Parents talk online gaming safety for kids ; support MediaWise . Your monthly MediaWise check-in Hello 👋 We can't believe it's almost the end of the year! But things aren't slowing down at MediaWise. From a new global initiative to fresh videos tackling big headlines, we've got plenty to share this November. First up, we're bringing newsrooms and independent creators together through our new Creator + Newsroom Studio initiative. With teams spanning five countries, we're exploring what happens when traditional journalism meets the creativity and reach of online creators. Meanwhile, our Teen Creator Network is tackling some big headlines and helping make sense of the stories behind them. Our G.A.M.E. crew is also back with a new conversation about keeping young gamers safe online without taking away the fun. And for educators, The MediaWise Weekly is keeping the lessons coming, with fresh ways to get students thinking critically about the information they encounter every day. Thanks for being part of the MediaWise community. We hope you enjoy this month's edition! ~Brittani & Alex New from MediaWise: The Creator + Newsroom Studio More and more people are turning to content creators for news and information. So what happens when those creators join forces with traditional newsrooms? We're about to find out! MediaWise is bringing the two worlds together through the Creator + Newsroom Studio , a new global initiative supported by Microsoft. The project pairs five news organizations across the U.S., Argentina, Kenya, Australia and Germany with independent creators to produce social-first journalism, explore AI literacy and find fresh ways to connect with audiences. And it's not just about making videos together. Newsrooms bring reporting expertise and editorial standards, while creators know a thing or two about grabbing attention and building connections online. The goal? See what they can learn from each other. Led by MediaWise deputy director Brittani Kollar and MediaWise ambassador Chris Vazquez, the initiative will explore what clicks, what gets tricky and what it takes to make these partnerships work. All those lessons will help shape a practical toolkit, coming in spring 2027, so more newsrooms and creators can team up and put what they've learned into action. Learn more Big headlines, big questions: The latest from our teen creators From college campuses in the U.S. to protests on the streets of France, there's a lot happening in the news. And our Teen Creator Network is helping break it all down. In one of their latest videos, our teen creators took a closer look at Title IX , the federal law that prohibits sex discrimination in education. Using recent questions surrounding events at Cornell University as a starting point, they explain how Title IX works, what protections it provides and where its limits lie. Meanwhile, across the Atlantic, another video takes viewers through a timeline of protests in France , unpacking how demonstrations over school conditions and the cost of living unfolded and why tensions escalated. Two very different stories, but both show how a little context can make a big difference when you're trying to understand what's happening in the world. WATCH WATCH Need a fresh lesson idea? We've got you covered Educators: The holidays are right around the corner, the end of the year is creeping up and your lesson-planning to-do list isn't getting any shorter. Sound familiar? If you're looking to shake things up in the classroom, The MediaWise Weekly has your back. Hundreds of educators across the U.S. are already using our free, ready-to-teach lessons to get students thinking critically about the news and information they encounter every day. And there's no shortage of interesting topics to explore. Recently, students have tackled questions like who decides which ads appear on social media and why a political ad might be approved on one platform but rejected on another. They've also taken a closer look at how to tell if a poll is trustworthy , digging into who's behind the numbers, how people were surveyed and why those little details matter. Each lesson gives students a chance to practice real-world media literacy skills, without adding another big project to your plate. 👉 Sign up to get free weekly lessons delivered straight to your inbox, or explore past lessons on our website. Helping young gamers play it safe online We're back with another livestream from our G.A.M.E. (Gamers Against Manipulation Efforts) initiative! This time, we brought together a panel of parents, gaming creators and online safety experts to tackle a big question: How can we help young gamers stay safe online without taking away the fun? Joining Gamertag Radio host Danny Peña and MediaWise Ambassador Riana Manuel-Peña were @airiesummer , a clinician-scientist and Twitch creator; Lonnetta Cunningham , business entrepreneur, president and co-founder of Team Wrk ; and Sean Marcus, MediaWise's interactive learning designer. The big takeaway? Conversation beats restriction. Our panelists discussed why parents don't need to be gaming experts to support their kids, how open communication can help young people recognize online risks and why gaming can actually be a great way to build friendships, empathy and critical thinking skills. The message was clear: Helping young people make informed decisions online starts with listening, learning and building trust. And we're not done yet! MediaWise is heading to TwitchCon in San Diego on Nov. 14 , where we'll keep the conversation going with a live panel on online safety, misinformation and building healthier gaming communities. Watch the full stream MediaWise tidbits A tip worth sharing: Did you catch our Global Media Literacy Tip of the Year during Global Media and Information Literacy Week? If you missed it, we've got you covered! Check it out on Instagram . Young voices, big ideas: MediaWise headed to Nashville for the second annual UnMuted Amplify Youth Summit, joining young leaders from around the world to tackle online challenges and explore real solutions. Want a peek at what went down? See the recap here ! Support MediaWise Like what you’re reading? Every day, MediaWise helps people of all ages understand and make sense of their digital environment. Your support helps to empower communities worldwide with media literacy skills and digital literacy tools so that we can all have a more positive online experience. Make a gift Follow MediaWise Want to change how you receive these emails? You can update your preferences or unsubscribe",
