        "pageTitle": "How much are candidates spending for the 2026 midterm? | USAFacts",
        "pageDescription": "Based on FEC data downloaded October 28, 2026, Senate and House candidates have spent around $3.2 billion on races for the 2026 November midterm election. That&#x27;s about 90% of campaign funds raised this election cycle.",
        "evidenceText": "How much are candidates spending for the 2026 midterm? | USAFacts How much are candidates spending for the 2026 midterm? Updated Oct. 31, 2026 Refreshed monthly Based on FEC data downloaded October 28, 2026, Senate and House candidates have spent around $3.2 billion on races for the 2026 November midterm election. That's about 90% of campaign funds raised this election cycle. $2.02 B spent by House candidates for federal campaigns since January 2025 $1.18 B spent by Senate candidates on federal campaigns since January 2025 Source: Federal Election Commission The Federal Election Commission ( FEC ) tracks federal campaign contributions and spending in two-year periods. Each period begins in January and goes through December of the following year. For example, the funding cycle for the November 2026 midterm election began in January 2025, and will end in December 2026. Candidates are required to disclose disbursements quarterly throughout the campaign cycle. The next presidential election will be in 2028. Funding for that cycle will begin in January 2027. Which candidates have spent the most money? Based on FEC data downloaded October 28, 2026, the 10 candidates to spend the most money are: James Talarico (Democrat, Texas Senate race): $136,427,055 Jon Ossoff (Democrat, Georgia Senate race): $105,760,717 Sherrod Brown (Democrat, Ohio Senate race): $63,956,397 Abdul El-Sayed (Democrat, Michigan Senate race): $51,096,843 Roy Cooper (Democrat, North Carolina Senate race): $40,496,482 Mary Peltola (Democrat, Alaska Senate race): $32,785,383 Raja Krishnamoorthi (Democrat, Illinois Senate race): $31,528,045 Troy Dale Jackson (Democrat, Maine Senate race): $25,276,003 David Trone (Democrat, Maryland House race): $25,004,528 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez (Democrat, New York House race): $24,188,385 James Talarico has spent the most money so far this election cycle: $136.4 million. Spending by candidate, by party affiliation, since January 2025, based on FEC data downloaded October 28, 2026 Source: Federal Election Commission Of the 10 candidates to spend the most money, all are Democrats. Susan Collins, a Republican candidate running for a Maine Senate seat, has spent the most of Republican candidates: $22.4 million. These spending totals include all candidates who have raised money for the 2025–2026 election cycle, including those who have since lost primaries or caucuses, or have otherwise withdrawn their candidacy. The previous top Republican spender, Lindsey Graham, passed away on July 11, 2026. His sister, Darline Graham Nordone, was appointed to fill his vacancy in the current Senate term, which ends in January 2027. She has since launched her own Senate campaign in South Carolina for the 2026 midterm to serve a full six-year term. Democrats, accounting for 1,369 of 2,643 congressional candidates, have spent $1.9 billion. Republicans, accounting for 1,052 candidates, have spent a total of $1.2 billion. Get weekly insights Subscribe for data-driven insights. No spin, just the facts. Some candidates have spent more than they have raised Of the top 400 highest-spending candidates, 66 have spent more than they have raised. For example, Josh Gottheimer, an incumbent candidate running for a House seat in New Jersey, reported raising $5,788,812, and spending $12,970,258. In other words, that's about 2.2 times more than they raised. 66 of the 400 top-spending congressional candidates have spent more than they've raised. Funds raised and spent by the top 400 spending candidates, since January 2025, based on FEC data downloaded October 28, 2026 Source: Federal Election Commission A campaign can spend more than it's raised by spending money it raised earlier (even in a previous election cycle), rather than new contributions, or if it has taken out loans. Candidates can take out personal loans , bank loans , and draw advances on a brokerage account, credit card, or home-equity line of credit, provided the loan is made under commercially reasonable terms in the ordinary course of the lender's business. Does candidate spending include contributions from PACs? Candidate campaign spending is reported separately from spending by political parties (like the DNC and RNC) and political action committees (PACs); each entity files its own totals with the FEC. Parties and PACs can contribute to candidates ( within limits ), and those contributions do count toward a candidate's total receipts. But parties and PACs also raise and spend significant funds for their own purposes, independent of any candidate, so their overall spending totals are not the same as candidate spending totals. Super PACs can raise and spend unlimited funds to support or oppose candidates, but they cannot donate directly to campaigns or coordinate spending with them. The FEC reports Super PAC spending separately from candidate spending. As a result, candidate campaign fundraising excludes Super PAC spending. Which states have the highest spending by Senate candidates? Candidates have spent a total of $1.18 billion on 2026 Senate races. The Texas senate campaigns have spent more so far than those in any other state: $182 million. Of that, Democratic Senate seat challenger James Talarico has spent the most of Texas's 14 candidates: $136.4 million, and Republican seat challenger Warren Kenneth Paxton Jr. spent $17.5 million. Why are there some states where no money has been spent? Not every state has a Senate election in 2026. Senators serve six-year terms and are divided into three election classes (Class I, Class II, and Class III), with staggered elections. That way, about one-third of Senate seats are up for election every two years. Each state's senators belong to different classes, no state has more than one regularly scheduled Senate election in the same year. Class II seats are up for election in 2026. Senate candidates in Texas have spent the most on campaigns. Total Senate campaign disbursements by state, since January 2025, based on FEC data downloaded October 28, 2026 Source: Federal Election Commission Candidates in Georgia have spent the second-most of all states, totaling $126.8 million. In Georgia, the top spender is Jon Ossoff, an incumbent Democrat who has spent $105.8 million, or around 83.4% of all Senate race money spent in Georgia. The next highest Georgia spender is Buddy Carter, a Republican seat challenger who spent $10.3 million. In June 2026, Carter lost the Republican primary runoff to Michael Collins, who has spent $10.2 million. Which House races have the highest candidate spending this midterm? Maryland's 6th Congressional District is the highest spending House race of this midterm so far, with candidates spending a combined $39 million. Most of that spending came from Democratic challenger David Trone ($25 million) and Democratic incumbent April McClain Delaney ($12.4 million). Trone represented Maryland's 6th district in the House from 2019 to 2025 and did not seek re-election for his seat in 2024. Delaney beat Trone in the June 2026 Maryland Democratic primary. Candidates for Maryland's 6th District have spent a combined $39 million. Total disbursements for federal congressional races by district, since January 2025, based on FEC data downloaded October 28, 2026 Source: Federal Election Commission Candidates in New York's 17th Congressional District have spent the second-most among House districts, at $33.7 million. Peter Chatzky, the challenger Democrat in New York's 17th Congressional District, has spent $11.7 million — more than 34.7% of the district's total. Democratic challenger Cait Conley has spent about $9.8 million, and the district's remaining candidates account for the rest. What happens to unspent campaign funds? If a candidate has withdrawn or been eliminated from their race, they have a few options for unspent funds under the Federal Election Campaign Act (and later amendments), and FEC regulations . Remaining funds can cover both campaign and non-campaign expenses. Campaign-related expenses include using extra funds for a future election. Candidates are required to have a principal committee registered with the FEC to raise and spend campaign funds. Under this type of expense, a candidate can: Keep funds in their existing committee by redesignating it for their next race Establish a new committee and, as long as the old committee doesn't have higher debts than cash on hand, transfer the funds over Under non-campaign expenses, funds can cover: Charitable donations as specified by 26 USC 170 (c) and 113.2(b) Transfers to political party committees Donations to state and local candidates (as state law allows) Any other lawful purpose that isn't personal use If the candidate was an officeholder, a candidate can spend money on \" ordinary and necessary \" officeholder expenses, including: Travel expenses for the officeholder, spouse, and children, when directly connected to the officeholder's official responsibilities \"Winding down\" costs for up to six months after leaving office Winding down costs can include related expenses like moving costs (moving office furnishings from a federal office in Washington, DC, back to a candidate's home state), paying committee staff, and gifts of nominal value (the FEC hasn't established a dollar amount) to non-family members. The FEC prohibits spending campaign funds for personal use. Top 200 spending candidates Top 200 spending candidates Candidate name Total spent James Talarico (Democrat, TX) TX Senate $136,427,055 Jon Ossoff (Democrat, GA) GA Senate $105,760,717 Sherrod Brown (Democrat, OH) OH Senate $63,956,397 Abdul El-Sayed (Democrat, MI) MI Senate $51,096,843 Roy Cooper (Democrat, NC) NC Senate $40,496,482 Mary Peltola (Democrat, AK) AK Senate $32,785,383 Raja Krishnamoorthi (Democrat, IL) IL Senate $31,528,045 Troy Dale Jackson (Democrat, ME) ME Senate $25,276,003 David Trone (Democrat, MD) MD District 06 $25,004,528 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez (Democrat, NY) NY District 14 $24,188,385 Source: Federal Election Commission See all (200) Keep exploring Methodology USAFacts standardizes data, in areas such as time and demographics, to make it easier to understand and compare. Our approach to AI for this analysis Page sources USAFacts endeavors to share the most up-to-date information available. We sourced the data on this page directly from government agencies; however, the intervals at which agencies publish updated data vary. Federal Election Commission Campaign finance data self.__next_f.push([1,\"5:[null,[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L36\\\",null,{\\\"model\\\":\\\"answer-page\\\",\\\"apiKey\\\":\\\"0b9e002c2ba04655a10a2d191e448df9\\\",\\\"content\\\":{\\\"createdDate\\\":1790637125883,\\\"data\\\":{\\\"answerCard\\\":{\\\"chart\\\":{\\\"base_visualisation_id\\\":\\\"21834980\\\",\\\"bindings\\\":{\\\"data\\\":{\\\"labels\\\":\\\"label\\\",\\\"values\\\":[\\\"value\\\"]}},\\\"data\\\":{\\\"data\\\":[{\\\"label\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"3.2\\\"}]},\\\"metadata\\\":{},\\\"state\\\":{\\\"chart_type\\\":\\\"big_number\\\",\\\"color\\\":{\\\"categorical_palette\\\":[\\\"#40B528\\\",\\\"#00A68E\\\",\\\"#024CE5\\\",\\\"#8726EB\\\",\\\"#DB1281\\\",\\\"#E02D04\\\",\\\"#E87500\\\"],\\\"palette\\\":[\\\"#40B528\\\",\\\"#00A68E\\\",\\\"#024CE5\\\",\\\"#8726EB\\\",\\\"#DB1281\\\",\\\"#E02D04\\\",\\\"#E87500\\\"],\\\"sequential_custom_max\\\":\\\"#012775\\\",\\\"sequential_custom_min\\\":\\\"#bfc9ff\\\",\\\"sequential_palette\\\":\\\"Custom\\\"},\\\"colors\\\":{\\\"categorical_palette\\\":[\\\"#40B528\\\",\\\"#00A68E\\\",\\\"#024CE5\\\",\\\"#8726EB\\\",\\\"#DB1281\\\",\\\"#E02D04\\\",\\\"#E87500\\\"],\\\"palette\\\":[\\\"#40B528\\\",\\\"#00A68E\\\",\\\"#024CE5\\\",\\\"#8726EB\\\",\\\"#DB1281\\\",\\\"#E02D04\\\",\\\"#E87500\\\"]},\\\"digits_to_round\\\":1,\\\"highlight_value\\\":\\\"highlight\\\",\\\"layout\\\":{\\\"body_font\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Source Sans3\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://public.flourish.studio/fonts/0159ba3d-6557-49f5-8017-072c