        "finalUrl": "https://www.occrp.org/en/news/raq-jails-ex-oil-boss-for-eight-years-over-119m-kickback-scheme",
        "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
        "pageTitle": "Iraq Jails Ex-Oil Boss for Eight Years Over $119M Kickback Scheme | OCCRP",
        "pageDescription": "Hussein Talib Abboud was ordered to repay $36 million in stolen state funds, delivering a stark warning to an energy sector long plagued by political patronage.",
        "evidenceText": "Iraq Jails Ex-Oil Boss for Eight Years Over $119M Kickback Scheme | OCCRP Corruption • Iraq Iraq Jails Ex-Oil Boss for Eight Years Over $119M Kickback Scheme Hussein Talib Abboud was ordered to repay $36 million in stolen state funds, delivering a stark warning to an energy sector long plagued by political patronage. Banner: Daniel Fogg | Former director general of the State Company for Oil Products Distribution sentenced to eight years in prison for corruption. Reported by Mujab Khalaf OCCRP November 4, 2026 | 2 min read Share Article &times; Aa Text Settings &times; Text Size Small Medium Large Line Spacing Compact Normal Spacious A high-profile court ruling against a senior former oil official has delivered another warning to Iraq’s political establishment that high office no longer guarantees impunity, an influential lawmaker told OCCRP on Wednesday, as authorities widen an aggressive anti-corruption campaign across the country’s lucrative energy sector. The verdict sends “a clear message that positions and responsibilities do not grant immunity from accountability,” said Qaisar al-Jorani, a member of parliament’s committee on oil, gas, and natural resources, calling the misappropriation of state wealth a “red line that must not be crossed.” The ruling, handed down by Iraq’s Criminal Court for Combating Corruption, sentenced Hussein Talib Abboud, the former director general of the State Company for Oil Products Distribution, to eight years in prison and ordered him to repay 55 billion Iraqi dinars (nearly $36.2 million). According to Iraq’s Federal Integrity Commission , Talib abused his authority over refinery financing to steer 40 contracts and 29 purchase orders—worth 181 billion dinars ($119 million)—at the North Refineries Company to affiliated firms. Investigators revealed that Talib personally pocketed 30 billion dinars ($19.7 million) in illicit commissions, while another 35 billion dinars ($23 million) enriched associate parties. Talib, a member of the Hikma National Movement who secured roughly 6,000 votes in the 2025 parliamentary elections, had been poised to enter parliament to fill a vacant seat left by Baghdad Mayor Ammar Musa Kadhim. Iraqi media reported that security forces arrested him in Baghdad in July 2026, shortly before he was scheduled to take the constitutional oath. Al-Jorani urged authorities not to stop with a single conviction, calling for expanded investigations into anyone who facilitated or turned a blind eye to procedural violations that damaged state finances. He also pressed for a comprehensive overhaul of contract-awarding, evaluation, and auditing mechanisms across the state energy sector to close avenues for abuse. The conviction is the latest major breakthrough in an aggressive anti-graft drive launched in April, which previously saw the June arrest of Ziad al-Janabi, the former chairman of parliament’s Integrity Committee. Spurred by systemic budget hemorrhaging and public outrage over entrenched patronage networks, the crackdown has reached unprecedented levels of the country’s political and bureaucratic elite, clawing back tens of millions of dollars from figures once deemed untouchable. While government officials point to the proceedings as proof that official titles no longer confer protection, political observers remain cautious over whether the campaign signals lasting structural reform or a selective rebalancing of power among competing ruling factions. November 4, 2026 Share Article: Reported by Mujab Khalaf OCCRP Related Articles 1 week ago Iraqi MP Gets Six Years, $20M Fine for Oil Graft Corruption Iraq 1 month ago Iraqi Lawmaker Sentenced to 7 Years, Fined $21 Million for Illicit Enrichment Corruption Iraq 1 month ago Former Anti-Corruption Official Among Two Iraqi Lawmakers Jailed for Illicit Enrichment Corruption Iraq 1 week ago Iraqi MP Gets Six Years, $20M Fine for Oil Graft Corruption Iraq 1 month ago Iraqi Lawmaker Sentenced to 7 Years, Fined $21 Million for Illicit Enrichment Corruption Iraq 1 month ago Former Anti-Corruption Official Among Two Iraqi Lawmakers Jailed for Illicit Enrichment Corruption Iraq Help us improve the website! Click below to give us input. It’ll only take 5 minutes. 👉 Help OCCRP Don't show this anymore",
