        "evidenceText": "Justice Department investigating television networks over Trump pool coverage - BBC News Image source, EPA Image caption, The row over the White House pool affected live TV coverage of Trump events last month By Madeline Halpert Published 11 October 2026 The US Department of Justice (DoJ) has said it is investigating several TV networks over whether they broke federal competition laws when they recently suspended pool coverage of Donald Trump. Last month, five networks paused their presidential coverage for several days in response to the Trump administration withdrawing the credentials of reporters from CNN, MS Now and Politico. The DoJ asked ABC, CBS, CNN, NBC and Fox News to hand over communications related to the pool and said the boycott could violate the Sherman Act, referring to a law that bars companies from engaging in monopolistic business practices. Pool members usually share their material with the wider media. A DoJ spokesperson said: \"The Antitrust Division is simply investigating to determine whether these ​news organisations have violated the antitrust laws.\" The television networks have not yet commented. Trump blocked CNN, MS Now and Politico from the White House last month after accusing the organisations of writing \"fiction or lies\" about his team. The White House later reinstated their access following a judge's ruling. The three outlets had sued the Trump administration, arguing the restricted access violated their First Amendment rights. The First Amendment forbids the government from \"abridging\" the freedom of the press - language that courts have traditionally interpreted as preventing the government from discriminating against the media based on the content of their coverage. The Trump administration has taken a number of legal actions against news publications in the US, including lawsuits over coverage of the president. In February, the White House announced it was taking control of the press pool, which has access to Trump and had for more than a century been managed by the White House Correspondents' Association. The same month, the Trump administration moved to bar Associated Press (AP) reporters and photographers from spaces where access is limited, such as the Oval Office or Air Force One, because of the outlet's usage of the term \"Gulf of Mexico\" instead of \"Gulf of America\". AP filed a lawsuit and the case remains ongoing. Related topics Media Press freedom Donald Trump More on this story CNN, MS NOW and Politico file lawsuit against Trump's White House ban Published 22 September Major US outlets suspend shared Trump coverage in protest at media ban Published 22 September The video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC Previous Next 0:33 Skims offers refunds after £110 advent calendar backlash. 00:00:33, play video Skims offers refunds after £110 advent calendar backlash 0:52 Why have at least 10 US Air Force planes suddenly left UK? 00:00:52, play video Why have at least 10 US Air Force planes suddenly left UK? 0:53 Man smashes Chinese government building with digger. 00:00:53, play video Man smashes Chinese government building with digger 1:25 My life as the UK's youngest lighthouse keeper. 00:01:25, play video My life as the UK's youngest lighthouse keeper 0:48 Stricter school phone ban rules for England revealed. 00:00:48, play video Stricter school phone ban rules for England revealed 1:16 Will smoky bacon crisps really be banned? 00:01:16, play video Will smoky bacon crisps really be banned? 2:02 How model agencies helped Epstein access victims. 00:02:02, play video How model agencies helped Epstein access victims 1:17 Russian newspaper forced to close after eviction. 00:01:17, play video Russian newspaper forced to close after eviction 0:20 Drone drops food to chimpanzee in reserve cut off by floods. 00:00:20, play video Drone drops food to chimpanzee in reserve cut off by floods 1:17 M John Harrison on his Booker Prize-winning