        "evidenceText": "‘60 Minutes’ Legend Lesley Stahl Reveals Why She Stayed at Trumpy Network Skip to main content By Laura Esposito Published Nov 05, 2026 6:53PM EST The legendary journalist Lesley Stahl has revealed why she chose to remain a 60 Minutes correspondent after the storied news program gained new, right-leaning, owners. In an emotional address at The Daily Beast’s Power 100 luncheon on Thursday, Stahl said she considered quitting as new management purged its award-winning staff. But, she said, she feared the show would have “vanished” without her and its other veteran correspondents. ADVERTISEMENT “It was not an easy decision,” Stahl, 84, admitted, as she accepted the inaugural Daily Beast Power 100 Award at the luncheon, which is sponsored by Uber. Stahl took the opportunity to explain her decision to remain at CBS. Alycia Kravitz Photography/Alycia Kravitz Photography Stahl, 84, was honored by The Daily Beast Power 100 luncheon, sponsored by Uber, at The Modern in midtown Manhattan. Alycia Kravitz Photography/Alycia Kravitz Photography Daily Beast Chief Creative and Content Officer Joanna Coles presented Stahl with the award at the even. Kara Swisher, the podcaster and trailblazing tech journalist, introduced Stahl, calling her an “inspiration.” ADVERTISEMENT “And I don’t have that many inspirations, to be honest with you,” Swisher quipped. “Right now, pretty much just the pope—I’m like the worst Catholic, too.” Get the Best of the Beast Sign Up for The Swamp Newsletter SUBSCRIBE By clicking “ SUBSCRIBE ” you agree to our Terms of Use and Privacy Policy . Stahl has remained at 60 Minutes , the most treasured—and highest-rated—news show on television, in a time of turmoil. In 2025, CBS was purchased by David Ellison, the son of the Trumpy Oracle billionaire Larry Ellison. Ellison then handed control of CBS News to the MAGA-curious former opinion writer Bari Weiss, setting off chaos at the network. Kara Swisher praied Stahl before presenting her with the award. Alycia Kravitz Photography/Alycia Kravitz Photography The turmoil came to engulf 60 Minutes , as first Anderson Cooper quit, then Weiss, who had no prior broadcast experience, fired half a dozen journalists and producers from the legendary news program—including Tanya Simon, its executive producer, who had just overseen record ratings for its latest season, and three correspondents: Scott Pelley, Cecilia Vega, and Sharyn Alfonsi. Play Video As the program entered its 59th season last month, 14-time Emmy winner Stahl, Bill Whitaker and Jon Wertheim, were the only correspondents to keep their jobs. On Thursday, Stahl spoke candidly about what happened. “There was an urge to walk out in protest and in solidarity with our colleagues who had been abruptly and cruelly fired,” Stahl said. “But we decided to stay out of a fear, a fear that if we didn’t, 60 Minutes would vanish, and all our colleagues who were still there, producers, editors, so forth—that they would be let go.” Joanna Coles presented Stahl with the award at her latest Power 100 luncheon, which brings trailblazing women across industries together. Alycia Kravitz Photography/Alycia Kravitz Photography During her acceptance speech, Stahl made a point of recognizing the guests she brought to share the honor, including Simon, who had spent a quarter of a century as a 60 Minutes producer. Weiss replaced her with 50-year-old TV news virgin Nick Bilton, whose season so far has included some of the lowest-rated shows of the century. “She is very much missed as my boss,” Stahl said, gesturing toward Simon as the room erupted in applause. Stahl pointedly brought Tanya Simon, the fired 60 Minuted executive producer (right) as her guest, and praised her as she accepted the award. Alycia Kravitz Photography/Alycia Kravitz Photography Simon (right) was fired by Weiss in a day of bloodletting, along with Alfonsi (second left) and Vega (second right). CBS Photo Archive/CBS via Getty Images Gayle King, the co-host of CBS Mornings, was also at the luncheon, along with stars including Cynthia Nixon, Melora Hardin, and Candace Bushnell. Stahl, who has been a 60 Minutes correspondent since 1991, having joined CBS News in 1971, acknowledged that ratings have been hit by the show’s turmoil—but said that preserving the DNA of an institution founded by Don Hewitt, and whose correspondents have included Mike Wallace, Morley Safer, and Charles Osgood. “Many of our loyal viewers over the last 58 years have tuned us out, presumably in protest, and I am devastated by this, because Bill, John, and I stay to preserve our DNA and to stand up for tough, honest journalism, the kind Mike Wallace would salute—and we are doing our best to fend off interference,” Stahl said. Gayle King, the CBS Mornings co-host was at the luncheon to honor Stahl, and greeted her during the event. Alycia Kravitz Photography/Alycia Kravitz Photography In a legendary CBS News career, which saw her join 60 Minutes in 1991,Stahl has reported from war zones—such as outside Gaza in 2025—and interviewed successive presidents, including Donald Trump. CBS News While last Sunday’s broadcast drew 10.4 million viewers, a relatively large number, 28.1 million people had been watching football on CBS immediately before it began. And the previous Sunday’s broadcast drew just 5.64 million viewers, according to Nielsen data, one of the smallest audiences the legacy program has attracted in years—continuing its downward ratings trend . Editor-in-chief of CBS News Bari Weiss took the helm without prior broadcast experience. Kevin Dietsch/Getty Images So far this season, Stahl’s reporting has included two hard-hitting segments on Israeli settler violence in the West Bank and the Trump administration’s alleged voter suppression efforts. Stahl reportedly clashed with Weiss, who is staunchly pro-Israel, over her report from the West Bank, with tensions escalating to the point that she privately discussed quitting, Status reported last month . But Stahl implored former viewers to return to the program, because of its higher purpose. “We are the watchdogs,” she said. “We are the cleansers of corruption, and we are the ones who ring the bell when the system goes out of whack. “All we can do is hold our position and keep on making.” See more of our coverage in your search results. ADD THE DAILY BEAST ON GOOGLE Laura Esposito Breaking News Reporter Got a tip? Send it to The Daily Beast here . Unlock a year of full access to The Daily Beast for $35. Monthly $1 First month then $5.99/month SELECT What's Included Unlimited access (web + app) Exclusive ad-free newsletters Commenting access Daily crossword Sneak peek of launches & events Auto-renews at $5.99 monthly. Cancel anytime Annual $35 First year then $59.99/year SELECT What's Included Discounted annual rate - Save 47% Unlimited access (web + app) Exclusive ad-free newsletters Commenting access Daily crossword Sneak peek of launches & events Auto-renews at $59.99 yearly. Cancel anytime Best Value Premium $79 First year then $119.99/year SELECT What's Included Discounted annual rate Full access to the Daily Beast Substack ($60 value)* Premium ad-free reading Unlimited access (web + app) Exclusive ad-free newsletters Commenting access Daily crossword Sneak peek of launches & events Auto-renews at $119.99 yearly. Cancel anytime *Substack access provided by the next business day, using your subscription email. Choosing the Premium plan constitutes your permission to share your subscription email with Substack and your agreement to Substack’s Privacy Policy . Looks like you already have a subscription! Account (completed) Payment (completed) Confirmation (completed) You're all set! Thanks for subscribing. Back 1 Account 2 Payment 3 Confirmation Sign in or create an account Email Continue OR Continue with Google Continue with Facebook Loading subscription options Register below to read this article for free or subscribe to unlock unlimited access to The