        "pageTitle": "RAG for decision models is here! Parallel just launched the Decisions API, which combines live web search with bounded decision-making in a single call. Decision models like Jev are useful when the… | Sumanth P | 89 comments",
        "pageDescription": "RAG for decision models is here!\n\nParallel just launched the Decisions API, which combines live web search with bounded decision-making in a single call.\n\nDecision models like Jev are useful when the output is small and structured: yes/no, one option from a list, a category, or a routing decision.\n\nThe problem is that those decisions are only as good as the context you give them.\n\nIf the answer depends on something that changed recently, or information that only exists on the web, the normal flow becomes retrieval first and decision second.\n\nParallel brings those two steps together.\n\nYou provide the state and the questions you want answered. The API searches the web, grounds the decision in what it finds, and returns the typed answer along with probability, confidence, and sources.\n\nThat m",
        "evidenceText": "RAG for decision models is here! Parallel just launched the Decisions API, which combines live web search with bounded decision-making in a single call. Decision models like Jev are useful when the… | Sumanth P | 89 comments Agree & Join LinkedIn By clicking Continue to join or sign in, you agree to LinkedIn’s User Agreement , Privacy Policy , and Cookie Policy . Skip to main content Sumanth P’s Post Sumanth P 1w Report this post RAG for decision models is here! Parallel just launched the Decisions API, which combines live web search with bounded decision-making in a single call. Decision models like Jev are useful when the output is small and structured: yes/no, one option from a list, a category, or a routing decision. The problem is that those decisions are only as good as the context you give them. If the answer depends on something that changed recently, or information that only exists on the web, the normal flow becomes retrieval first and decision second. Parallel brings those two steps together. You provide the state and the questions you want answered. The API searches the web, grounds the decision in what it finds, and returns the typed answer along with probability, confidence, and sources. That makes it much more useful for agent workflows where a decision depends on current external information, research tasks where the model needs to verify something before choosing an outcome, or eval pipelines where the judgment needs grounding from the web. It can also answer multiple questions against the same retrieved context in one request, instead of repeating the search for every decision. So instead of: Search → retrieve context → decision model → structured answer you can make the grounded decision directly through one API. I think this is a pretty natural extension of where decision models are going: bounded outputs are useful, but giving that decision layer access to the live web makes the set of problems it can handle much larger. 1,327 89 Comments Like Comment Share Copy LinkedIn Facebook X Sai Charan 1w Report this comment This makes decision models a lot more practical for anything that changes over time. Like Reply 2 Reactions 3 Reactions Refat Ametov 5d Report this comment A cited answer depends on which pages the search ranked first. If an outdated pricing page outranks the changelog that replaced it, the decision follows the old price. A separate retrieval step gives teams a place to filter by date and log what was read before deciding. One call trades some of that control for speed. The returned sources still make a later audit possible, if someone samples them. Like Reply 1 Reaction Manoj Kumar 1d Report this comment This is a game-changer for agentic workflows! Combining live web retrieval with bounded decision-making in a single API call significantly reduces latency and complexity. Excited to see how this improves reliability in dynamic environments. Like Reply 1 Reaction Ahmed N. 4d Report this comment Sumanth P - Nice extension of the decision-model pattern. The citations are the sleeper feature here — a structured decision you can’t audit is a liability in any regulated flow, and “Yes, 75%, here’s the three sources” is the difference between a decision you can sign off on and one you have to manually re-check. One caution: grounding is only as trustworthy as the retrieval, so a confidently-wrong decision now inherits the SEO/freshness noise of live web search. The confidence score needs to reflect source quality, not just model certainty. Like Reply 1 Reaction Syed Muhammad Zubair Alam 5d Report this comment Bringing retrieval and decision-making together is an interesting step. The bigger challenge will be validating those decisions when sources are outdated or conflicting. Having citations helps, but the quality of the underlying evidence still matters. Like Reply 1 Reaction Sabri MAHMOUD 5d Report this comment Trying to wrap my head around this so I give it a question and a set of possible answers, and it searches the web to pick one? How is that different from asking an AI with web search the same question? Like Reply 1 Reaction Fahad Hussain 6d Report this comment Combining live retrieval with structured decisions makes these models much more useful for real-world workflows. The confidence scores and source citations are especially helpful when a decision needs to be checked rather than blindly accepted. Like Reply 1 Reaction Vijay Athuluri 1w Report this comment The refusal type in the example caught my eye. Nice that it can say \"no decision\" instead of guessing. The thing I'd want with live web grounding is a way to replay a decision later. If the page changes tomorrow, the same question might flip, and debugging that without the original sources would be painful. Do the citations come back with a snapshot of the page, or just the URL? Like Reply 1 Reaction Botuka Vinay Yadav 5d Report this comment That batching detail stood out to me. Answering several questions against the same retrieved context in one request is exactly the kind of thing that keeps costs and latency down in production. Curious how it handles conflicting signals when the web sources disagree, since that is where a bounded decision gets tricky. Like Reply 1 Reaction Sagar Gupta 1d Report this comment Returning typed answers together with confidence and sources could make a decision API easier to inspect than a bare label, especially when web context shifts. How do you handle contradictory or stale sources before a high-stakes yes/no, and does the caller get enough provenance to apply its own threshold? Like Reply 1 Reaction See more comments To view or add a comment, sign in 93,851 followers 607 Posts View Profile Follow Explore content categories Career Productivity Finance Soft Skills & Emotional Intelligence Project Management Education Technology Leadership Ecommerce User Experience Show more Show less Sign in to view more content Create your free account or sign in to continue your search Email or phone Password Show Forgot password? Sign in Sign in with Email or New to LinkedIn? Join now By clicking Continue to join or sign in, you agree to LinkedIn’s User Agreement , Privacy Policy , and Cookie Policy .",
