        "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt The president had appeared to accept the Democrats’ sweeping victories. His latest comments drew exasperated responses, even from some allies. Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION Before we get started, a quick note: Today, Nov. 10, is Poynter Day , marking 51 years since the Institute was founded to uphold independent, ethical journalism. If The Poynter Report has helped you make sense of the media industry this year, please consider supporting our nonprofit newsroom with a birthday gift today. Happy birthday, Poynter! Surprise, surprise: President Trump suggests the midterms were rigged President Donald Trump speaks with reporters before boarding Air Force One in Florida on Sunday. (AP Photo/Jacquelyn Martin) Well, you knew this was coming. Two weeks after suggesting he would accept the results of the 2026 midterm elections, even if the Democrats had a good night, Trump woke up on Monday morning and reverted to his old playbook. As he has done repeatedly without evidence about the 2020 presidential election, Trump questioned the legitimacy of this year&rsquo;s midterm elections. Trump wrote in a Truth Social post on Monday: &ldquo;Does anybody really believe that this &lsquo;Election&rsquo; was honest. How could the Polls be so wrong??? Some early voting &lsquo;dumps&rsquo; were so large that it was impossible to win, no matter how many people went to vote. It&rsquo;s all about the machines, which are far more important than the voter!&rdquo; First, the polls were not wrong. Most polls predicted that the Democrats would have a big night, even in places such as Texas, where polls showed Democratic Senate candidate James Talarico knocking off Republican Ken Paxton. In fact, noted pollster Nate Silver gave the Democrats a 96% chance of winning the House and an 80% chance of taking the Senate. Trump&rsquo;s post also came after days of him seemingly accepting the blue wave that swept the nation. In interviews, including one with &ldquo;60 Minutes&rdquo; on Sunday night, Trump pointed to various reasons why Republicans had such an awful election, including the war in Iran, high gas prices and poor GOP candidates. But he hadn&rsquo;t expressed doubt in the results, as he has in the past. Not that he had any reason to express doubt. The New York Times&rsquo; Luke Broadwater and Zolan Kanno-Youngs wrote , &ldquo;Election officials have said the vote went reasonably smoothly, with few major issues reported and no evidence of widespread fraud.&rdquo; But, of course, that didn&rsquo;t stop Trump. Politico&rsquo;s Kyle Cheney wrote , &ldquo;Trump has for 10 years made false claims of fraud and irregularities in elections in which he or his allies were defeated, including in the aftermath of the 2020 election when he whipped supporters into a frenzy and began mounting an attempt to overturn his defeat to Joe Biden. But this time, there appear to be fewer allies willing to take up his calls.&rdquo; Social media users swiftly responded to Trump with equal parts antipathy, weariness and sarcasm. &ldquo;The View&rdquo; co-host Alyssa Farah Griffin, who worked in the Trump White House during his first term, tweeted , &ldquo;Lather, rinse, repeat.&rdquo; Susan Glasser of The New Yorker tweeted , &ldquo;This was so inevitable.&rdquo; On and on it went like that. Even Megyn Kelly didn&rsquo;t have the stomach for more unproven claims by Trump. She took a screengrab of his Truth Social post and wrote on X , &ldquo;Just stop.&rdquo; Douthat&rsquo;s good performance Speaking of Trump&rsquo;s &ldquo;60 Minutes&rdquo; interview, Ross Douthat, the show&rsquo;s newest correspondent, is getting good reviews for his tough interview with the president. In Monday&rsquo;s newsletter , I praised Douthat by writing, &ldquo;Douthat did a solid job, asking good, tough and fair (and a couple of offbeat) questions, and jumping in when necessary to keep Trump from rambling and going off focus. The result was a revealing interview, more illuminating than most interviews with Trump.&rdquo; Independent journalist and former CNN commentator Chris Cillizza tweeted , &ldquo;To all the people who questioned CBS hiring Ross Douthat because he wasn&rsquo;t a &lsquo;tv reporter,&rsquo; I would suggest you go watch his interview with President Trump on &lsquo;60 Minutes.&rsquo; A master class.&rdquo; &ldquo;The View&rdquo; co-host Alyssa Farah Griffin also called it &ldquo;a master class.&rdquo; Fox News&rsquo; Brit Hume tweeted , &ldquo;Ross Douthat&rsquo;s questioning of Trump on this was polite, persistent and revealing. A model for how to interrogate a president: non-hostile, respectful but penetrating. Good for him.&rdquo; Biden&rsquo;s book Donald Trump, left, and Joe Biden meet in the White House here on Nov. 13, 2024 &mdash; eight days after the 2024 presidential election. (AP Photo/Evan Vucci) Former President Joe Biden has a new memoir &mdash; &ldquo;Promise Me, America&rdquo; &mdash; coming out next week. The Atlantic has an excerpt: &ldquo;What Trump Did on January 6 &lsquo;Should Follow Him to His Grave.&rsquo;&rdquo; In the excerpt, Biden writes about how Trump pardoned those who participated in the Jan. 6 insurrection: &ldquo;And we know the truth. Donald Trump led an insurrection. His goal was to remain in power after the American people had voted him out of office. That&rsquo;s the truth. It was a violent assault. The rioters weren&rsquo;t &lsquo;peaceful protesters.&rsquo; They came armed to stop the official certification of a legitimate election. The criminals who attacked law-enforcement officers that day didn&rsquo;t deserve pardons. They deserved prison terms. To this day, Trump and his supporters continue to preen and posture like they&rsquo;re supporters of law enforcement. Here&rsquo;s my view: You don&rsquo;t get to turn a blind eye to cops being punched, speared, sprayed, and brutalized and call yourself a friend of law enforcement. What Trump did on January 6, 2021, should never be forgotten. It should follow him to his grave.&rdquo; In the excerpt, Biden also writes that when he welcomed Trump to the White House eight days after the 2024 election in which Trump defeated Kamala Harris, Trump told him, &ldquo;You know, Joe, if it weren&rsquo;t for politics, I believe we&rsquo;d be good friends.&rdquo; As for his upcoming book tour, Biden said his battle with prostate cancer will limit his ability to promote the book. He wrote on social media , &ldquo;Some days are better than others. It hasn&rsquo;t stopped me. But sometimes it slows me down. What that means is, unfortunately, I won&rsquo;t be able to do as many conversations about my book as I&rsquo;d hoped. However, I&rsquo;m hopeful the words on the page speak for themselves.&rdquo; Russini returns Dianna Russini is officially back. The NFL writer who parted ways with The Athletic after allegations of an inappropriate relationship with New England Patriots coach Mike Vrabel will host a weekly one-hour show about the NFL that will stream on BetUS TV&rsquo;s website and X account beginning this Wednesday. BetUS is a gambling site. Russini will also write a weekly column and appear regularly on the &ldquo;Nobody&rsquo;s Safe&rdquo; podcast with Fox Sports radio host Jon &ldquo;Stugotz&rdquo; Weiner. The announcement comes seven months after a controversy led to her departure from The Athletic. Page Six published photos of Russini at an Arizona resort with Vrabel, an NFL head coach she covered. The photos showed Vrabel and Russini &mdash; both married to other people &mdash; hugging, interlocking hands and hanging out poolside and in a hot tub. Russini and Vrabel denied they were involved romantically. The Athletic launched an investigation into Russini, who then resigned before the results of that investigation were announced. Later, The Athletic said its investigation found that Russini&rsquo;s conduct was a &ldquo;clear violation&rdquo; of newsroom standards, adding, &ldquo;Regardless of whether the relationship was romantic, it was a breach and should have been disclosed.&rdquo; In a story by People&rsquo;s Janelle Griffith published on Monday, Russini said, &ldquo;The best way to describe what the last six months were like for me is heartbreaking. At the beginning of this, it was a nightmare.&rdquo; Russini said she returned to sports media with the support of her husband and loved ones, as well as those in the NFL community. The story in People wasn&rsquo;t hard-hitting, and it didn&rsquo;t press Russini on the nature of her relationship with Vrabel and whether her job had been compromised. She did say, &ldquo;The lies became so out of control. This was a runaway train, and there was nothing I could do to stop it. Once we realized nothing was going to stop this, it became unhealthy for us to read it because it wasn&rsquo;t helping us heal. It was piling on and adding to what we were already dealing with.&rdquo; Russini added, &ldquo;I would not wish that experience on any person ever. But through it, I did get an opportunity to not just learn about myself, but my family and our priorities, and maybe some decisions and things I was doing in my career, and putting it before what I really wanted most, which was to be a mom. And while it was so hard at first, feeling their love and support helped me believe that, &lsquo;Wait, I know I can go back to doing this. I know that they&rsquo;re settled. I know that we&rsquo;re a unit, and no one is going to come between us. Nothing&rsquo;s going to break us.&rsquo;&rdquo; Media tidbits Trump says he was &ldquo;only kidding&rdquo; when he offered UFC CEO Dana White the White House press secretary job in a Truth Social post. He told reporters late Sunday that he wasn&rsquo;t being serious. Speaking of which, here&rsquo;s Mediaite&rsquo;s Sarah Rumpf with &ldquo;Trump Brings UFC Boss Dana White Into Briefing Room in Utterly Surreal Scene.&rdquo; CNN&rsquo;s Brian Stelter with &ldquo;Joe Rogan helped Trump win the White House. Now his show is exposing divisions inside it.&rdquo; Starting next season, the College Football Playoff will expand to 24 teams. ESPN will remain the exclusive rights holder through 2031-32. The Athletic&rsquo;s Stewart Mandel and Ralph D. Russo write , &ldquo;According to a source briefed on the new contract but not authorized to speak publicly about the details, the deal is worth $1.7 billion over five years, with an average annual value of $345 million per year. However, ESPN will also be repaid around $140 million per year for the canceled ACC, SEC and Big 12 championship games it owns rights to through separate agreements. The new deal will replace the existing six-year, $1.3 billion contract ESPN and the CFP agreed to in 2024 when the Playoff expanded to 12 teams.&rdquo; My Poynter colleague Kelly McBride with &ldquo;In a West Virginia county of 6,540, accountability journalism gets personal.&rdquo; Writing about former Department of Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem, it&rsquo;s The Daily Beast&rsquo;s Martha McHardy with &ldquo;ICE Barbie Humiliated on Fox News Over Bizarre Boast.&rdquo; Semafor&rsquo;s Max Tani with &ldquo;Anti-Waymo campaign relied on op-eds written with AI.&rdquo; The Associated Press&rsquo; Jake Coyle with &ldquo;In another shake-up at DC Studios, Gunn and Safran are out as Skydance appoints Marvel vet Latchman.&rdquo; Hot type If you thought President Trump might be humbled and more apt to work with the other side of the aisle following the Democrats&rsquo; massive midterm election results, he seemed to snuff out that idea by naming Stephen Miller his new chief of staff. Check out this piece from The Atlantic&rsquo;s Jonathan Lemire, Michael Scherer, Ashley Parker, Sarah Fitzpatrick and Vivian Salama: &ldquo;A Chief of Staff Who &lsquo;Will Crack Skulls.&rsquo;&rdquo; And from the weekend, The Wall Street Journal&rsquo;s Michelle Hackman, Josh Dawsey, Alex Leary and Sadie Gurman with &ldquo;Trump Signals Combative Next Two Years With Elevation of Stephen Miller.&rdquo; More resources for journalists Protect your newsroom&rsquo;s digital journalism with Today&rsquo;s News for Tomorrow , a free program offering expert training, professional archiving tools and ongoing support to preserve local news for future generations . Apply by Dec. 11. Take the next step in your leadership career with Leadership Academy for Women , an advanced program for women in journalism with at least three years of leadership or management experience. Strengthen your leadership skills, expand your professional network and gain strategies for navigating challenges and advancing your career. Apply by Feb. 8, 2027. Build practical AI skills while learning to use the technology responsibly. Explore AI tools, ethics, misinformation and bias, learn how to apply AI to your everyday work, and earn a Poynter certificate. Complete the core course in just 4-5 hours, anytime and at your own pace. Have feedback or a tip? Email Poynter senior media writer Tom Jones at tjones@poynter.org . The Poynter Report is your daily dive into the world of media, packed with the latest news and insights. Get it delivered to your inbox Monday through Friday by signing up here . And don&rsquo;t forget to tune into our biweekly podcast for even more. Support the journalism that keeps you informed. GIVE NOW ADVERTISE // DONATE