    {
      "id": "ebc86b4c43522f54643b19334b971c183e9009b887851e81fd7745976ca546ec",
      "title": "682: quantum of sollazzo",
      "url": "https://marcoshuerta.com/posts/full-moon-myth",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "providers": [
        "Gmail Radar PLOT newsletter"
      ],
      "firstSeen": "2026-11-10T20:44:59.238Z",
      "lastSeen": "2026-11-10T20:44:59.238Z",
      "status": "pending",
      "reviewPriority": "normal",
      "audit": {
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T22:19:16.265Z",
        "httpStatus": 200,
        "finalUrl": "https://marcoshuerta.com/posts/full-moon-myth/",
        "contentType": "text/html",
        "pageTitle": "The Full Moon Myth Takes the Field – Marcos Huerta",
        "pageDescription": "Google’s ad on the full moon effect in baseball is a case study in small number statistics",
        "evidenceText": "The Full Moon Myth Takes the Field – Marcos Huerta Introduction People love a full moon. Supermoon invaded the lexicon (despite being only marginally larger than any other full moon) over the last few decades, along with many other moon superlatives. Blue moon had been around for a while, as well as harvest moon but lots of other moons have shown up in the media. (Can we please go back to just saying lunar eclipse and not blood moon ?) Among lunar related topics, one belief has long persisted - that certain events or trends are more likely during a full moon. Perhaps, the full moon changes behavior or there are more accidents, ER visits, crime, etc. during the full moon. This is called the Full Moon Myth and, as you might guess from the myth in the title, there is no actual data or evidence behind it at all. There’s no “lunar lunacy” ( Rotton and Kelly 1985 ) , and there’s no increase in ER visits ( VanMeter et al. 2024 ) . This 2009 Scientific American article is a good reference for other non-existent full moon correlations. Note TLDR Google ran a “Gemini with Google Cloud” commercial during the World Series that made 3 dubious claims about stats changing during the full moon. All are examples of small number statistics with large error bars, and analyzing more than one year of data (I used 1900-2025) shows any effect of the full moon to be spurious. Important Corrections My initial version of this post used the wrong UTC for the moon phase, probably shifting it by a bit less than a day. Midnight UTC July 2 is the evening of July 1, and I’m joining on date. I have updated it to use 21:00 UTC which is 5PM EDT. That splits the difference between night games and day games, though there were fewer night games in the early 20th century (and no daylight saving time) but I’m not going to try to account for that. I have thought more about what it means to be a “full moon” and added a discussion of what is the appropriate definition. Google’s commercial : “Can a full moon bring out a game’s wild side?” So, with the full moon myth in mind, you can imagine my reaction when I saw this Google Gemini / Cloud ad while watching Game 1 of the World Series: The ad opens with the question: “Can a full moon bring out a game’s wild side?” Well, “Gemini on Google Cloud” looked into it! 1 The commercial makes the following claims for the 2025 MLB season: Multi-run late-inning comebacks 2 rose 34% under a full moon. Seven-plus run blowout games jumped 25%. The rate for “homers that howled over 450 feet more than doubled.” I posted on Blue Sky almost immediately upon seeing the commercial: Did a Google Cloud ad just claim that the full moon changes baseball stats? That's … nuts and now I'm going to have to join lunar phase data with baseball data to show this is nonsense. I know one study once showed accident/moon correlation but only because the moon coincided with weekends. — Marcos Huerta (@marcoshuerta.com) 2026-10-24T00:51:14.775Z While I posted some preliminary results in the Bluesky thread itself, I will summarize and expand upon my findings in this post. 3 Analysis Baseball Data Retrosheet 4 has two files I can use for my analysis, The file gameinfo.csv has final scores and stats for each game, and teamstats.csv has 2 lines (one for each team, sometimes more) for each game that includes runs per inning (which I’ll need to look at the late inning comeback claim.) The “450+” home run claim requires home run data, so I turned to Statcast data from Baseball Savant . Unlike Retrosheet, which goes back to the 19th century, the Statcast data only goes back to 2008 5 . Note Most of the code blocks below are calling custom functions I wrote, see the code section at the end for the all of those function definitions. Moon Data For the lunar phase data, I created a daily ephemeris at JPL Horizons Systems for the moon. These aren’t CSVs, but more like fixed width data files with non-standard headers and footers so I manually removed these extra lines with BBEdit and read in using pandas read_table . What is a full moon? In the initial version of this post, I just used > 99% illumination as my definition of full moon. Any date when phase > .99 counted as a full moon game. But, it’s a sinusoidal curve - it’s not uncommon for the moon to be > 99% for 2 days in row. A strict definition of full moon, where it’s 1 day per peak (using the local maximum) would mean only 83 games played under full moons for 2025 on the following 7 dates: April 12 May 12 June 10 July 10 August 8 September 7 October 6 Using the > 99% illuminated criteria, that adds 6 more ‘full moons’ listed below, almost doubling the 2025 “full moon game” count to 161. April 13 May 11 June 11 August 9 September 6 October 7 I think the “strict” definition is most likely the data that Google used, so I have redone my calculations since the initial post to only use “local max” full moons, now joined on 21:00 UTC. Claim: Seven-plus run blowout games jumped 25% This is the second claim in the ad, but it’s the easiest to analyze so I’ll walk through it first. I actually found a slightly different than 25% increase for 2025. 2025 Analysis As mentioned above, there were 83 games played under a full moon in 2025, and 2395 games played without a full moon. The total number of 7+ run blowouts during a full moon is 15 . Show the code from baseball import ( make_one_df_range, make_fake_phase, get_merged_data, make_phase_bin_data, clean_name, make_fake_vs_real_full_moons, find_col, make_moon, make_table, ) import pandas as pd import polars as pl import plotly.express as px merged = get_merged_data() make_one_df_range( merged. filter (pl.col( 'season' ).eq( 2025 )), stat = 'large_victory' , range_size = 1 , max_full_moon = True , ).pipe(make_table, 'large_victory' ) Large Victory Full Moon Statistics Moon Status Large Victory Rate Large Victory Rate Error Game Count Large Victory Game Count False 14.15% 1.40% 2395 339 True 18.07% 8.28% 83 15 h_{\\bullet} = \\frac{339}{2395} = 0.14 h_{\\circ} = \\frac{15}{83} = 0.18 Due to the small number of games played under a full moon in 2025, and the rarity of blowouts, the 27% increase has an error bar of … ± 59%. It’s also cherry picked because there’s a negative effect at 5+ and 9+ runs. Show the code blowouts_2025 = pd.concat([ make_phase_bin_data( merged. filter (pl.col( 'season' ).eq( 2025 )), phase_range = [ 99 ], range_size = 2 , stat = 'large_victory' , threshold = threshold, )[[ 'ratio' , 'ratio_error' ]].assign(pct_increase = pd.col( 'ratio' ) - 1 , threshold = threshold) for threshold in range ( 5 , 11 ) ]) fig = px.bar( pl.from_pandas(blowouts_2025).with_columns(pl.col( 'threshold' ).cast(pl.String) + '+ Runs' ), y = 'threshold' , x = 'pct_increase' , error_x = 'ratio_error' , title = 'Percent Increase of Blowouts during Full Moon 2025' , labels = {x: clean_name(x) for x in [ 'threshold' , 'pct_increase' ]}, ) fig.update_xaxes(tickformat = '.1%' ) So, regardless of how one defines ‘blowouts’, the 2025 data has no signal outside the error bars. I can’t even call this p-hacking because none of these are statistically significant. Google cherry-picked data, and ignored the small sample size and error bars. We need more data to shrink those error bars, so next I’ll look at every season since 1900! Every game since 1900 Show the code blowouts_since_1900 = pd.concat([ make_phase_bin_data( merged, phase_range = [ 99 ], range_size = 1 , stat = 'large_victory' , threshold = threshold, )[[ 'ratio' , 'ratio_error' ]].assign(pct_increase = pd.col( 'ratio' ) - 1 , threshold = threshold) for threshold in range ( 5 , 11 ) ]) fig = px.bar( pl.from_pandas(blowouts_since_1900).with_columns( pl.col( 'threshold' ).cast(pl.String) + '+ Runs' ), y = 'threshold' , x = 'pct_increase' , error_x = 'ratio_error' , title = 'Percent Increase of Blowouts during Full Moon 1900-2025' , labels = {x: clean_name(x) for x in [ 'threshold' , 'pct_increase' ]}, ) fig.update_xaxes(tickformat = '.1%' ) I doubt Google would make an ad about a 2.1% increase during a full moon. Perhaps you don’t love the error bars from the Wald method. I simulated 1000 fake moons with random periods. With these fake moons, I can compute the same “% increase” on all the fake full moons, and look at the distribution. Show the code fake_moons_all_seasons = make_fake_vs_real_full_moons(merged, n = 1000 ) px.histogram( fake_moons_all_seasons.with_columns( (pl.col( 'full_moon_ratio' ) - 1 ).alias( 'Full Moon % Increase of 7+ Run wins' ) ), x = 'Full Moon % Increase of 7+ Run wins' , color = 'fake moon' , title = '1900-2025, 7+ run Margin of Victory Fake Moon phase Histogram' , ) Plenty of the fake full moon cycles reach the same or higher “full moon % increase” as the real moon. There’s not much signal here. I’m declaring Claim 2 debunked . Claim: Multi-run late-inning comebacks rose 34% under a full moon. This was a bit harder to compute. Thankfully the teamstats.csv from Retrosheet has 2 rows per team and runs by innings. With a little finesse, I can make a “comeback lookup” dataframe to merge into gameinfo.csv data. To make things simpler, I only look at the score/margin after the end of a full inning. I’m going to assume “multi-run” comeback just means 2 or more runs, but “late inning”- how did they define it? Once again I’ll just try several run/inning combinations for 2025, but despite trying 2+ and 3+ leads after multiple innings, I haven’t been able to reproduce the exact 34% increase. I can show you giant error bars, once again. Show the code import itertools def make_comeback_expr(inning = 6 , lead = 2 ): return ( pl.col( f'comeback_after_ { inning } ' ).eq( - 1 ) & (pl.col( f'score_after_ { inning } _h' ) - pl.col( f'score_after_ { inning } _v' )) . abs () .ge(lead) ).alias( f'comeback_of_ { lead } _after_ { inning } ' ) teamstats = pl.read_csv( 'teamstats.csv' , infer_schema_length = 100000 , ignore_errors = True ) gid_comeback_lookup = ( teamstats . filter (pl.col( 'stattype' ).eq( 'value' )) .with_columns(score_after_5 = pl.sum_horizontal([ f'inn { n } ' for n in range ( 1 , 6 )])) .with_columns(score_after_6 = pl.sum_horizontal([ f'inn { n } ' for n in range ( 1 , 7 )])) .with_columns(score_after_7 = pl.sum_horizontal([ f'inn { n } ' for n in range ( 1 , 8 )])) .with_columns(score_after_8 = pl.sum_horizontal([ f'inn { n } ' for n in range ( 1 , 9 )])) .select([ 'gid' , 'vishome' , 'win' , 'score_after_5' , 'score_after_6' , 'score_after_7' , 'score_after_8' , ]) .pivot( on = 'vishome' , values = [ 'score_after_5' , 'score_after_6' , 'score_after_7' , 'score_after_8' , 'win' ], index = 'gid' , ) .with_columns( * [ ( (pl.col( f'score_after_ { n } _h' ) - pl.col( f'score_after_ { n } _v' )).sign() * (pl.col( 'win_h' ) - pl.col( 'win_v' )).sign() ).alias( f'comeback_after_ { n } ' ) for n in range ( 5 , 9 ) ]) .with_columns( * [ make_comeback_expr(i, l) for i, l in itertools.product([ 5 , 6 , 7 , 8 ], [ 2 , 3 ]) ]) ) 2025 Comeback Analysis Show the code merged_comebacks = ( merged.lazy().join(gid_comeback_lookup.lazy(), on = 'gid' , how = 'left' ).collect() ) make_one_df_range( merged_comebacks. filter (pl.col( 'season' ).eq( 2025 )), stat = 'comeback_of_2_after_6' , phase = 99 , range_size = 1 , max_full_moon = True , ).pipe(make_table, 'comeback_of_2_after_6' ) Comeback Of 2 After 6 Full Moon Statistics Moon Status Comeback Of 2 After 6 Rate Comeback Of 2 After 6 Rate Error Game Count Comeback Of 2 After 6 Game Count False 4.09% 0.79% 2395 98 True 8.43% 5.98% 83 7 Of the 83 games during a full moon in 2025, there were 7 comebacks of 2+ runs after 6 full innings for a rate of 8.43%. (98 in 2395 non-full-moon games.) Show the code comebacks_2025 = pd.concat([ make_phase_bin_data( merged_comebacks. filter (pl.col( 'season' ).eq( 2025 )), phase_range = [ 99 ], stat = stat, )[[ 'ratio' , 'ratio_error' , 'count_true' ]].assign( stat_name = stat, pct_increase = pd.col( 'ratio' ) - 1 ) for stat in find_col(merged_comebacks, 'comeback_of' ) ]) fig = px.bar( pl.from_pandas(comebacks_2025).with_columns( pl .col( 'stat_name' ) . str .replace_all( '_' , ' ' , literal = True ) . str .to_titlecase() . str .replace_all( r'Of ( \\d ) ' , r'Of $ 1 + ' ) ), y = 'stat_name' , x = 'pct_increase' , error_x = 'ratio_error' , hover_data = [ 'count_true' ], title = 'Percent Increase of Late Multi-Run comebacks during Full Moon 2025' , ) fig.update_xaxes(tickformat = '.1%' ) Comebacks of 2+ runs after 5 full innings is 24% ± 98%… that’s the closest I could get to the 34% increase. The strongest effect looks like 3 or more runs after 8 full innings, which is 2(!) comebacks out of 83 games and a whopping 861% increase! But with an even bigger error bar. These are some big percent increases, but at this sample size still very much small number statistics, with non-meaningful results and large error bars. 1900-2025 Comeback Analysis Since 1900, sometimes the trend seems to be fewer comebacks during the full moon, sometimes more, but again - never outside the large error bars. The strongest “signal” is probably 2+ runs after 8 which is up 11%. I ran some fake moon simulations for this scenario. Show the code sim_data = make_fake_vs_real_full_moons(merged_comebacks, n = 1000 , stat = 'comeback_of_2_after_8' ) px.histogram( sim_data.with_columns( (pl.col( 'full_moon_ratio' ) - 1 ).alias( 'Full moon % Increase of Late Inning, Multi Run comebacks' ) ), x = 'Full moon % Increase of Late Inning, Multi Run comebacks' , color = 'fake moon' , title = '1900-2025, Real Moon vs Simulation Histogram of Late Inning, Multi-run Comebacks' , subtitle = 'Comebacks of 2+ run after 8' , ) I mean… it’s not completely nothing? It’s about 1 sigma. But we’re clearly hunting desperately for signal by looking at a bunch of different definitions - pure p-hacking . I find it notable that the broadest criteria with the most sample is 2+ rallies after 5 full innings and there’s not much there: a 0.8% decrease . I’m declaring Claim 1 debunked . Claim: Rate for homers that howled over 450 feet more than doubled. Warning Correction My initial take here, using the broader definition of a full moon (> 99% illuminated), and slightly wrong lunar phase data showed no strong effect (+20%). Using the better lunar data with only 1 full moon per month, I found some very large (but still in the noise) increases. Since the first version posted, I also restricted the analysis to regular season games. I was not 100% sure I know what this one means. I’m pretty sure it means \\frac{h_{450}}{h} , i.e. the rate of the 450+ homers with all home runs as the denominator. It could possibly mean “rate of games that have 450+ home runs”? The data is from Statcast and that link should show you the results I used, with a save button to save the CSV 6 . There were 95 homers with Statcast estimated distances of 450 feet or higher in 2025. There were 5646 total homers. During a full moon, \\frac{7}{179} homers were 450+, or 3.9%. During all other times, it was \\frac{88}{5467} , or 1.61%. That’s a 142.9% \\pm 183% higher rate. A big increase! Yet, an increase with a massive error bar on a very small absolute count - we’re talking 7 home runs. I made a graph with error bars with rates for home runs of difference distances, there’s negative or no signal at 400+ feet, the strongest signal might be at the 410 feet threshold which is almost 2 sigma. Show the code homer_data = pl.read_csv( 'savant_data.csv' ).with_columns( date = pl.col( 'game_date' ). str .to_date( '%Y-%m- %d ' ) ) moon_data = make_moon() merged = ( homer_data .join( moon_data, on = 'date' , ) .with_columns( * [ pl.col( 'hit_distance_sc' ).ge(d).alias( f'homer_over_ { d } ' ) for d in range ( 380 , 470 , 10 ) ]) .with_row_index( 'gid' ) ) Show the code homers_by_distance = pd.concat([ make_phase_bin_data(merged, phase_range = [ 99 ], stat = stat)[[ 'ratio' , 'ratio_error' ]].assign( stat_name = stat, pct_increase = pd.col( 'ratio' ) - 1 ) for stat in [ 'homer_over_380' , 'homer_over_390' , 'homer_over_400' , 'homer_over_410' , 'homer_over_420' , 'homer_over_430' , 'homer_over_440' , 'homer_over_450' , 'homer_over_460' , # 'homer_over_470', ] ]) fig = px.bar( pl.from_pandas(homers_by_distance).with_columns( pl .col( 'stat_name' ) . str .replace_all( '_' , ' ' , literal = True ) . str .replace_all( 'homer' , 'Homers' ) . str .to_titlecase() . str .replace_all( r'Of ( \\d ) ' , r'Of $ 1 + ' ) ), y = 'stat_name' , x = 'pct_increase' , error_x = 'ratio_error' , labels = { 'pct_increase' : 'Percent Increase during Full Moon' , 'stat_name' : 'Home run distance' , }, title = 'Percent Increase of Homers during the Full Moon, 2025' , ) fig.update_xaxes(tickformat = '.1%' ) These numbers are based on tiny totals. There are 0 home runs during a full moon over 470 feet, and 2 over 460 feet during the full moon. These are the definition of small number stats. 2024 Data Let’s look at 2024. I struggled to get Baseball Savant to generate the right CSV for 2024 - the data looked right on my screen, but the download button kept giving me other, unrelated data. I had to save the HTML, and use pandas read_html to to the data I could see in my browser into a CSV. Show the code homer_data = pl.read_csv( 'homers_2024_from_web.csv' ).with_columns( date = pl.col( 'game_date' ). str .to_date( '%Y-%m- %d ' )) merged = (homer_data.join( moon_data, on = 'date' , ).with_columns( * [ pl.col( 'hit_distance_sc' ).ge(d).alias( f'homer_over_ { d } ' ) for d in range ( 380 , 470 , 10 ) ]).with_row_index( 'gid' )) homers_by_distance = pd.concat([ make_phase_bin_data(merged, phase_range = [ 99 ], stat = stat)[[ 'ratio' , 'ratio_error' ]].assign(stat_name = stat, pct_increase = pd.col( 'ratio' ) - 1 ) for stat in [ 'homer_over_380' , 'homer_over_390' , 'homer_over_400' , 'homer_over_410' , 'homer_over_420' , 'homer_over_430' , 'homer_over_440' , 'homer_over_450' , 'homer_over_460' , # 'homer_over_470', ] ]) fig = px.bar( pl.from_pandas(homers_by_distance).with_columns( pl.col( 'stat_name' ). str .replace_all( '_' , ' ' , literal = True ). str .replace_all( 'homer' , 'Homers' ). str .to_titlecase(). str .replace_all( r'Of ( \\d ) ' , r'Of $ 1 + ' )), y = 'stat_name' , x = 'pct_increase' , error_x = 'ratio_error' , labels = { 'pct_increase' : 'Percent Increase during Full Moon' , 'stat_name' : 'Home run distance' , }, title = 'Percent Increase of Homers during the Full Moon, 2024' , ) fig.update_xaxes(tickformat = '.1%' ) I guess 430+ homer rates look somewhat strong again? Yet, 440+ is essentially zero. This is definitely p-hacking, looking at a bunch of metrics trying to find anything that might look significant. I’m declaring Claim 3: Debunked . 7 Follow up, Home Runs, 2015-2026 Statcast data for computed distance is available since 2015. I had various issues getting the CSV downloads at Baseball Savant to work, something about the url was misconstructed. I copied out code from baseballr package to make my own CSV urls, and that worked. Show the code homersall_parquet = pl.read_parquet( 'statcast_homers_2015_to_2026.parquet' ) merged_all = homersall_parquet.join(moon_data, on = 'date' ).with_columns( * [ pl.col( 'hit_distance_sc' ).ge(d).alias( f\"homer_over_ { d } \" ) for d in range ( 380 , 500 , 10 ) ]).with_row_index( 'gid' ) homers_by_distance = pd.concat([ make_phase_bin_data(merged_all. filter (pl.col( 'date' ).dt.year().ge( 2015 )), phase_range = [ 99 ], range_size = 2 , max_full_moon = True , stat = stat)[[ 'ratio' , 'ratio_error' ]].assign(stat_name = stat, pct_increase = pd.col( 'ratio' ) - 1 ) for stat in [ 'homer_over_380' , 'homer_over_390' , 'homer_over_400' , 'homer_over_410' , 'homer_over_420' , 'homer_over_430' , 'homer_over_440' , 'homer_over_450' , 'homer_over_460' , 'homer_over_470' , ] ]) fig = px.bar( pl.from_pandas(homers_by_distance).with_columns( pl.col( 'stat_name' ). str .replace_all( '_' , ' ' , literal = True ). str .replace_all( 'homer' , 'Homers' ). str .to_titlecase(). str .replace_all( r'Of ( \\d ) ' , r'Of $ 1 + ' )), y = 'stat_name' , x = 'pct",
