        "pageTitle": "Is It Crazy to Go to Journalism School? - Columbia Journalism Review",
        "pageDescription": "A student reflects on leaving the law to pursue his writing dreams.",
        "evidenceText": "Is It Crazy to Go to Journalism School? - Columbia Journalism Review b   The Kicker b Sign up for the daily CJR newsletter . Jonny Peña lived several lives before going back to school in pursuit of two master’s degrees, one in international affairs and the other in investigative journalism. Born in Colombia, he moved to Atlanta when he was four and was undocumented throughout his childhood. After earning permanent-resident status, he joined the Marines, working in Arizona, Japan, Afghanistan, and California. Then he enrolled in law school, became a prosecutor for the Manhattan district attorney’s office, took a break to work on financial crimes at a bank in Mexico City, investigated police shootings for the New York State attorney general, and went back to the district attorney’s office to work on financial fraud.  He loved working as a prosecutor. He made a salary in the mid-hundred-thousand-dollar range. But he gave it all up in hopes of becoming a journalist. He knows some people probably think he’s out of his mind, but he’s committed (even if learning to write for a general-interest audience has been tougher than he expected). I interviewed Peña about the transition, covering immigration in New York for class, and how he’s thinking about life after graduation. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes : Jonny Peña , Substack. “What I Learned Reporting on New York City’s Immigration Courts (Part 2),” Jonny Peña, Substack. “ As DACA Renewals Stall, Dreamers Lose Work Permits—and Paychecks ,” Jonny Peña, Labor New York .   Credits : Host: Megan Greenwell Producer: Amanda Darrach Has America ever needed a media defender more than now? Help us by joining CJR today . Megan Greenwell is a freelance journalist based in Brooklyn and the author of Bad Company: Private Equity and the Death of the American Dream . More from CJR How We Watched the Midterms Steve Kornacki and Hasan Piker went head-to-head, and Donald Trump called in. By Ivan L. Nagy   Reporting the Backlash The midterms pushed data centers center stage, as media, activists, and politicians questioned AI’s ever-expanding infrastructure. By Lindsay Muscato   Novaya Gazeta ’s Journalism Will Live On The paper is shutting down. But a new project grants it electronic immortality. By Joel Simon   The Susan Collins Effect “It’s an image that’s cultivated by her PR people and by the Maine media to give the impression that she’s something she’s not.” By Rob Wolfe   The voice of journalism, since 1961 About Mission Masthead Privacy Policy Contact Support CJR Become a Member Donate Advertise Contact Us Copyright 2026, Columbia Journalism Review",
