        "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt 😢 Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . I watched the Online News Association&rsquo;s annual conference from afar for years before I got to go to my first one. As a projects reporter at the St. Louis Beacon, it looked techy and cool and way beyond my skill set at first. But eventually, as a media reporter for Poynter, I got the chance to attend, and it blew me away.  In 2014, the conference was in Chicago, and then-New York Times staffer Amy O&rsquo;Leary took the stage in a keynote about the leaked Times innovation report. I remember the whole thing feeling electric, like we were getting to peek inside change as it was unfolding.   The next year, Poynter and ONA announced a joint leadership program for women , and again, it felt like something huge was happening .  For me, the moments catching up with people in the hallways became the most meaningful experiences. But over time, there were more hallways, and a lot of conferences, where that could happen.  So when ONA CEO Niketa Patel shared a note yesterday about a &ldquo;strategic shift,&rdquo; it certainly felt like something big was taking place. You can read the entire note here , but the highlights (lowlights) are that the conference will pause this year and that ONA&rsquo;s staff is getting cut.  I&rsquo;m not sure if it&rsquo;s the end of an era, the era was already over and we&rsquo;re just now calling it, or this is really just a pause. I checked in with my colleague Tony Elkins (who I think I met for the first time at ONA in D.C.), about what those conferences meant to him.  &ldquo;To say ONA was important to me is an understatement for two big reasons,&rdquo; he told me in Slack. &ldquo;First, the people I met at the annual conference have been instrumental to the rather odd path my career has taken. There have been so many hallway sessions that ultimately led me to where I am now. The second reason is that it helped me hone my skills as a speaker and a facilitator. This is foundational to the teaching I do now, and I don&rsquo;t know if I would have the opportunity to speak in front of so many people, in rooms of all shapes and sizes. I really do hope the organization can find its footing because, with all credit to my favorite session by Robert Hernandez, the future of journalism feels [blank] without it.&rdquo; My colleague Alex Mahadevan also found community at ONA. &ldquo;ONA was particularly helpful for lonely-coder introverts like myself,&rdquo; he told me in Slack. &ldquo;I was pretty much the only person doing AI (internally and teaching it) at Poynter, and it was cool to meet people in the same position. To vent, of course, but also to come up with cool ideas we could use at our own organizations. I always saw NICAR as more hands-on skill-building conference, but ONA was a social one. It was super valuable to bond with a cohort.\" Did you go to ONA? What were your experiences? Reply to this email; I&rsquo;d love to hear from you. That&rsquo;s it for me. I&rsquo;ve been on a tiny book tour and having the best time getting to have adventures with local journalists. 😍 Kristen Kristen Hare Faculty The Poynter Institute @kristenhare ADVERTISE // DONATE // LEARN // JOBS Did someone forward you this email? Sign up here. © All rights reserved Poynter Institute 2026 801 Third Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701 If you don't want to receive email updates from Poynter, we understand. You can change your subscription preferences or unsubscribe from all Poynter emails .",
