        "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
        "pageTitle": "Dianna Russini&#039;s comeback over before it started as livestream plagued by tech issues",
        "pageDescription": "Dianna Russini&#039;s highly anticipated comeback was over before it started as her new show couldn&#039;t even begin due to tech issues.",
        "evidenceText": "Dianna Russini's comeback over before it started as livestream plagued by tech issues Skip to content Credit: The Russini Report By Drew Lerner on 11/11/2026 11/11/2026 Less AI and more AA in your search results. Believe it or not, the offshore sportsbook nobody had heard of until it announced a weekly show starring Dianna Russini earlier this week wasn’t ready for its big moment. The aforementioned sportsbook, BetUS, was unable to get its livestream of The Russini Report off the ground at noon ET on Wednesday, when the show was set to make its debut. The page on its website featured an embedded video with elevator music and rotating pictures of Russini with a caption reading “show starts soon” for more than 30 minutes past 12 p.m. ET. And funnily enough, that video wasn’t even an embedded livestream, but just a 90-second clip that would play on repeat. Those who looked for The Russini Report on other platforms also struck out. The X page for the show posted a link that said “WATCH HERE” that was completely broken and didn’t even lead to a webpage. The Russini Report YouTube channel did not feature any sort of livestream. And the BetUS Vimeo account, which embedded the 90-second clip on its website, was set to private. 🚨🔴THE MOMENT IS HERE. WE ARE LIVE! 🔥 🎥WATCH HERE: https://t.co/6M35HOJrG6 🎙️🏈 THE RUSSINI REPORT with @DMRussini officially kicks off! 🔥 Breaking NFL news, insider scoops &amp; unfiltered takes! 🏆 Bill Parcells 🎙️ @DamonAmendo 🔥 @SpikeEskin 📺 WATCH LIVE ON BETUS TV! https://t.co/pLfXt1I4x4 — The Russini Report (@RussiniReport) November 11, 2026 If there was a worst-case scenario that Russini and BetUS imagined for the show’s debut, this probably exceeded those expectations in the worst way. At 12:24 p.m. ET, nearly a half-hour after the show was scheduled to start, the show’s X handle claimed it was the overwhelming number of people trying to watch the stream that “broke it.” Well… you broke it. 😅 The premiere of The Russini Report drew a record-breaking audience today! We’re adding more seats so everyone gets in. Stay with us! We’ll be back shortly. 🙏 — The Russini Report (@RussiniReport) November 11, 2026 “The premiere of The Russini Report drew a record-breaking audience today!” the post embarrassingly went on to claim. “We’re adding more seats so everyone gets in.” How can a show that never even happened draw a “record-breaking audience,” you may ask? We’re still trying to figure that out ourselves. And for starters, blaming the people tuning in for the show for the technical difficulties, and not whoever is producing it, won’t exactly put Russini and BetUS in the good graces of the fans she’s looking to win back. It’s impossible to take this venture seriously . The X handle doesn’t even have a profile picture. This is amateur hour all around. And as this post goes live around 12:40 p.m. ET on Wednesday, there is still no show to speak of. If there’s a definition of Dead On Arrival, this could very well be it. UPDATE [12:53 p.m. ET]: The first episode of The Russini Report was posted on Vimeo about 50 minutes after it was scheduled to debut. The show appears to be prerecorded, though the original post advertising the show at noon ET seemed to suggest it’d be live. Russini also opens the show by letting viewers know she will keep an eye on emails being sent in, with the implication she’d address them live on the air. It’s unclear if the original plan was to do the show live or prerecord it. Either way, there was some mixed messaging. Russini largely stuck to football throughout the episode, though at the very end she thanks her “new family at BetUS” before thanking her “own family.” About Drew Lerner Drew Lerner is a staff writer for Awful Announcing and an aspiring cable subscriber. He previously covered sports media for Sports Media Watch. Future beat writer for the Oasis reunion tour. Drew writes the Awful Announcing 'The A Block' newsletter every Friday Subscribe here . View all posts by Drew Lerner Related Content The SEC’s TV ratings dominance is getting scary for the Big Ten Jordon Hudson reportedly sues Airbnb owners over leak of topless Bill Belichick footage The 76ers are either building a superteam or creating a superproblem The Dave Portnoy ‘Big Noon Kickoff’ breakup was always inevitable Recent Posts The league says live match viewers per week increased 93%. The beloved commentator died from an \"acute illness.\" Tomlin stepped away from the Pittsburgh Steelers in January and quickly became a hot media prospect before NBC scooped him up. Credit: Kirby Lee-Imagn Images \"It is so much easier to get an audience and make money [by] appealing to the lowest common denominator of the MAGA-sphere than it is to do it in sports.\" \"Maybe, just maybe, the apology can be as loud as y'all leading y'all shows with this topic.\" Sidebar Popular Posts Five NFL teams play three consecutive primetime games this season; zero did so last year. Mike Florio thinks he knows why Where have you gone, Lee Corso? College football fans falling out of love with Saturday pregame shows Recent Posts The league says live match viewers per week increased 93%. The beloved commentator died from an \"acute illness.\" Tomlin stepped away from the Pittsburgh Steelers in January and quickly became a hot media prospect before NBC scooped him up. Credit: Kirby Lee-Imagn Images \"It is so much easier to get an audience and make money [by] appealing to the lowest common denominator of the MAGA-sphere than it is to do it in sports.\" \"Maybe, just maybe, the apology can be as loud as y'all leading y'all shows with this topic.\"",
