        "evidenceText": "Husted addresses HB6 ties, rising prices in Ohio Senate push Skip to content Share Add Us On Google Add as a preferred source on Google COLUMBUS, Ohio (WOIO) - The race in the special election for Ohio’s open senate seat is its final stages. Current Sen. Jon Husted, R-Ohio, was selected by Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine to serve the final two years of former Sen. JD Vance’s term after Vance was elected vice president. Husted is trying to stay in Washington but is being challenged by former Sen. Sherrod Brown, a Democrat, who lost two years ago to Republican Sen. Bernie Moreno. The polls have indicated this is a very close race. Some polls indicate Brown has a slight lead, others say it’s Husted. Today, I was given 15 minutes with Husted while on the campaign trail in Columbus. In our one-on-one interview, I asked about the closeness of the race and what he will try to do to win in the closing weeks. I also asked about the two areas Brown and the Democrats are using to attack Husted. First, his connection to House Bill 6 while he was lieutenant governor in Ohio, which ended up being the largest corruption scandal in state history. Husted had to testify in the trail against two former FirstEnergy executives accused of bribing lawmakers to get HB6 passed. The second area Husted is being attached to is the issues of data centers. While lieutenant governor, a number of data centers opened in the state. Husted was quick to point out that whenever a new data center would open, then-Sen. Brown was quick to celebrate the opening and send out media releases. In the above interview, Husted addresses both issues and topics on the economy and rising prices for families. Copyright 2026 WOIO. All rights reserved. Most Read Three killed in wrong-way crash on I-90 after attempted traffic stop Woman found dead on conveyor belt at Summit County recycling facility identified Husband, wife found murdered in North Royalton home, ex son-in-law also found dead Memory card w