        "pageTitle": "Gary Click lashes out after weakest win of his Statehouse career — TiffinOhio.net",
        "pageDescription": "The Republican incumbent received nearly $93,000 in campaign support from the Ohio House GOP",
        "evidenceText": "Gary Click lashes out after weakest win of his Statehouse career — TiffinOhio.net Skip to main content Type at least 2 characters to search. local State Rep. Gary Click, a Republican from Vickery, represents Ohio's 88th House District. (Screenshot via The Rooster / Facebook) State Rep. Gary Click, R-Vickery, won reelection Tuesday with 58.57% of the vote over Tiffin City Councilman Aaron Jones, his weakest showing since 2020, after Ohio House Republicans supplied $92,765.80 in advertising and printing. Click then called Seneca County Democrats disgraceful and TiffinOhio.net a fake news blog. Key points How Click compared with other Republicans on the same ballot in both counties Click's unsupported claim that dark money from Chicago was used against him Why Jones's concession call went unanswered and how Jones described his campaign's goals By R.L. Porter State Rep. Gary Click (R-Vickery) won reelection Tuesday with the weakest performance of his Statehouse career after receiving nearly $93,000 in advertising and printing support from the Ohio House Republican establishment. By Wednesday, he was lashing out at Democrats and the press, declaring an exception to his “usual practice” of being gracious in victory. Click defeated Democrat Aaron Jones, a Tiffin city councilman, by 23,930 votes to 16,927, according to unofficial returns from the Seneca County and Sandusky County boards of elections. Click received 58.57% to Jones’s 41.43%, an eight-point decline from his 2024 vote share and more than nine points below his 2022 performance. Campaign-finance records reviewed by TiffinOhio.net show that the Ohio House Republican Organizational Committee, known as OHROC, reported $92,765.80 in in-kind contributions to Click’s campaign in its 2026 pre-general filings. Seven transactions included $91,124.33 in advertising and $1,641.47 in printing. Click’s own pre-general report separately listed more than $18,000 in cash expenditures by his campaign committee. That substantial financial backing accompanied Click’s weakest general-election result since he first won the House seat in 2020. He received 62.90% that year, 67.80% in 2022 and 66.57% in 2024. His 2026 performance was also the weakest Republican showing in any contested District 88 midterm election since the district began encompassing Seneca and Sandusky counties in 2012. Republican Bill Reineke received 59.01% in 2014 and 65.87% in 2018. Jones’s 41.43% was the strongest Democratic share in a District 88 midterm during that period. Click also ran behind other Republicans on Tuesday’s ballot. Across the same two counties, state auditor candidate Frank LaRose received 62.27% of the two-party vote, Attorney General Keith Faber received 62.02%, and state treasurer candidate Jay Edwards received 61.44%. Click lashes out as Jones congratulates him Following the election, Click used his campaign’s Facebook account to acknowledge that he ordinarily believes winners should be gracious before launching into attacks against his political opponents. “It has been my custom to be gracious in victory. I think that is normally the right thing to do,” Click wrote. He singled out Seneca County Democratic Party leader Gina Grandillo and the county’s Democrats, calling their conduct “disgraceful and disgusting.” He also attacked this outlet, TiffinOhio.net, which he described as a “fake news blog.” On his personal Facebook account, Click separately accused opponents of spreading personal smears and alleged that “dark money from Chicago” had been used against him. His posts provided no documentation identifying the alleged funding or substantiating the accusations. Jones took a different approach. In his public concession statement Tuesday night, he congratulated Click, thanked campaign volunteers and expressed hope that the incumbent would listen to residents concerned about property taxes, public schools and working families. A source within the Jones campaign told TiffinOhio.net that Jones personally called Click at approximately 9:30 p.m. Tuesday to congratulate him and concede the race. Click did not answer, and Jones left a voicemail. According to the source, Click had not returned the call as of Wednesday. Nearly $93,000 in GOP support after a primary scare The substantial OHROC investment followed a Republican primary in which Click nearly lost his nomination to Eric Watson, a first-time candidate from Tiffin. Watson defeated Click in Seneca County and finished just 600 votes behind him districtwide, receiving 47.72% of the Republican primary vote. Watson mounted that challenge despite substantially weaker fundraising and a series of extraordinary claims about data centers. In February, The Toledo Blade reported on a recording supplied by Click in which Watson claimed artificial intelligence data centers could harvest people’s conscious and unconscious thoughts, including while they slept. Click’s campaign had already benefited from OHROC support during the primary. Campaign-finance filings reviewed by TiffinOhio.net showed Click’s committee transferred $45,000 to the Republican caucus organization, which subsequently reported providing $38,611.62 in campaign support. Click reported $69,461.37 in contributions through primary day, nearly four times Watson’s $17,488.05. The incumbent ultimately survived both elections with the backing of Republican leaders and the party’s campaign apparatus. Jones, who said his campaign was outspent by a wide margin, nevertheless secured the strongest Democratic midterm performance in the district in more than a decade. Click’s renewed attacks on Seneca County Democrats also followed his own documented false allegations against local political opponents. In August, campaign-finance records disproved his assertion that Jones, Grandillo and Fremont City Councilwoman Angie Ruiz had contributed to Watson’s Republican primary campaign. None had done so. Click deleted the accusation and acknowledged his mistake. Three months later, Click celebrated his reelection by accusing opponents of dishonesty and alleging political funding from Chicago. His victory posts offered no evidence supporting those accusations, even as his own campaign-finance disclosures documented tens of thousands of dollars in advertising supplied by the Republican establishment. See more of our trusted coverage in your search results. Add TiffinOhio.net on Google Never miss another breaking news story. Join our Facebook Messenger channel today. Add TiffinOhio.net on Messenger By R.L. Porter R.L. Porter is a political and investigative journalist for TiffinOhio.net. rporter@northstarcivic.com More from TiffinOhio.net For You › Sign in or create a free account for personalized recommendations, saved stories, and more. Loading recommendations… Gary Click keeps whining about news coverage after his weakest election win Term-limited Click offered no evidence for his dark money claims, as a source says he plans a 2028 run for the 26th District Ohio Senate seat. November 9, 2026 Gary Click keeps whining about news coverage after his weakest election win Term-limited Click offered no evidence for his dark money claims, as a source says he plans a 2028 run for the 26th District Ohio Senate seat. By R.L. Porter Gary Click says he might have voted for Democrat Aaron Jones, calls Republican primary challenger ‘a nut’ In a recorded conversation obtained by TiffinOhio.net, the three-term Republican lawmaker disparaged Eric Watson, a first-time candidate who nearly defeated him in May despite a substantial fundraising disadvantage. October 15, 2026 Gary Click says he might have voted for Democrat Aaron Jones, calls Republican primary challenger ‘a nut’ In a recorded conversation obtained by TiffinOhio.net, the three-term Republican lawmaker disparaged Eric Watson, a first-time candidate who nearly defeated him in May despite a substantial fundraising disadvantage. By Dave Miller Why did Gary Click quietly leave his church? Months later, voters still don't have a clear answer Rep. Gary Click called the transition \"very public\" when challenged but has not identified any announcement from March 2025, when he says he stepped down. September 15, 2026 Why did Gary Click quietly leave his church? Months later, voters still don't have a clear answer Rep. Gary Click called the transition \"very public\" when challenged but has not identified any announcement from March 2025, when he says he stepped down. By Dave Miller After GOP revolt and false claims, Gary Click faces Aaron Jones on Election Day Click, who won two-thirds of the vote in 2024, survived his May primary by about 600 votes and seeks his final term against the Tiffin councilman. November 2, 2026 After GOP revolt and false claims, Gary Click faces Aaron Jones on Election Day Click, who won two-thirds of the vote in 2024, survived his May primary by about 600 votes and seeks his final term against the Tiffin councilman. By R.L. Porter Gary Click is showing off a campaign-wrapped Challenger after racking up 10 traffic citations Click ranked among Ohio's top three most-cited lawmakers with 10 tickets, a record he called less important than other issues facing the Statehouse. August 26, 2026 Gary Click is showing off a campaign-wrapped Challenger after racking up 10 traffic citations Click ranked among Ohio's top three most-cited lawmakers with 10 tickets, a record he called less important than other issues facing the Statehouse. By R.L. Porter Gary Click barely survived his GOP primary. Now Washington and Columbus power brokers are coming to Tiffin to fundraise for him. The Sept. 21 golf fundraiser follows a primary Click won by just 599 votes and lost outright in Seneca County, where the event will be held. August 17, 2026 Gary Click barely survived his GOP primary. Now Washington and Columbus power brokers are coming to Tiffin to fundraise for him. The Sept. 21 golf fundraiser follows a primary Click won by just 599 votes and lost outright in Seneca County, where the event will be held. By R.L. Porter Tap to finish signing up & listen Gary Click lashes out after weakest election performance of his Statehouse career 7 min Gary Click lashes out after weakest election performance of his Statehouse career by R.L. Porter Newsletter required Subscribe free to listen to this story This article includes a 7 min AI narration. Sign up for our free newsletter to unlock listening and the full story — no spam, unsubscribe anytime. Subscribe free to unlock Close 0:00 7 min Share Your Feedback We want to hear from you. Whether you've spotted an error, have a story idea, or just want to share your thoughts—we're listening. Article Title Feedback Type * Select feedback type… Report an Error or Correction Suggest Additional Context Share a Related Story Idea Positive Feedback Display or Technical Issue AI Summary or Overview Audio Transcription Feedback Broken Link General Feedback Other Your feedback * Your Email (optional) We read every submission. A response is not guaranteed, but we appreciate all feedback. I have read and agree to the Terms of Service and Privacy Policy * Submit Feedback Republish this article Gary Click lashes out after weakest election performance of his Statehouse career by R.L. Porter, TiffinOhio.net November 4, 2026 HTML Plain Text Copy to Clipboard Canonical Tag: Copy Tag Article Content: Copy Content Attribution line (HTML): Copy Line 📰 Before you go... Stay connected with local news that matters. Get our free newsletter delivered to your inbox. Email address Get free updates By subscribing, you agree to our Terms and Privacy Policy . ✓ Thank you! You're now subscribed. Pro tip: Add newsbriefing@tiffinohio.net to your contacts to ensure our newsletters reach your inbox. You may also like... Gary Click keeps whining about news coverage after his weakest election win Term-limited Click offered no evidence for his dark money claims, as a source says he plans a 2028 ru