        "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt The departures of two veteran editorial leaders give Bari Weiss an opening to reshape the influential Sunday political news program Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION Face the changes: Two top leaders are leaving CBS News&rsquo; &lsquo;Face the Nation&rsquo; Margaret Brennan on the set of CBS News&rsquo; &ldquo;Face the Nation&rdquo; in 2020. (Courtesy of CBS News) There&rsquo;s a big shakeup at CBS News as Mary Hager, senior executive producer of &ldquo;Face the Nation,&rdquo; prepares to leave the network after 35 years. Status&rsquo; Oliver Darcy broke the news, writing , &ldquo;Hager&rsquo;s departure represents a significant loss for CBS News. While her official title is senior executive producer of &lsquo;Face the Nation,&rsquo; she played an outsized role in CBS News&rsquo; Washington bureau. A 35-year veteran of the network, she possesses a wealth of institutional knowledge, having helped steer its political journalism through multiple presidential elections and various political cycles.&rdquo; Hager was first named executive producer of &ldquo;Face the Nation&rdquo; in 2011, and became CBS News&rsquo; executive editor of politics in 2022. As Darcy noted, Hager is senior executive producer of &ldquo;Face the Nation.&rdquo; It turns out that Anne Hsu, the program&rsquo;s executive producer, is also leaving. Darcy writes that Hsu is moving to the network&rsquo;s bureau in Hong Kong. Darcy writes, &ldquo;The loss of both Hager and Hsu will leave the program without two of its most senior editorial leaders at a particularly precarious moment for CBS News, which has been navigating an incredibly turbulent year under new Editor-In-Chief Bari Weiss, particularly with the upheaval at &lsquo;60 Minutes.&rsquo; The exits of Hager and Hsu will present Weiss with an opportunity to effectively reset &lsquo;Face the Nation&rsquo; and steer the influential program more to her liking. While the Sunday show has not been a primary focus of Weiss&rsquo; since she took the reins at CBS News, she has privately mulled its future.&rdquo; Several reports indicate that Hager&rsquo;s retirement had been planned and was not a sudden decision. She reportedly called the decision difficult. Nevertheless, Hager&rsquo;s retirement and Hsu&rsquo;s departure are likely rattling those at the program. The Guardian&rsquo;s Jeremy Barr tweeted that CBS News president Tom Cibrowski met with the &ldquo;Face the Nation&rdquo; staff on Wednesday to not only celebrate Hager&rsquo;s legacy on the show, but to &ldquo;ease concerns.&rdquo; Hager does leave the show in good shape. Variety&rsquo;s Brian Steinberg wrote , &ldquo;She will leave &lsquo;Nation&rsquo; as the show is faring well in the ratings. Season to date as of November 8, &lsquo;Face The Nation&rsquo; has won more viewers than either NBC&rsquo;s &lsquo;Meet The Press&rsquo; or ABC&rsquo;s &lsquo;This Week.&rsquo; And while it has lost some viewership compared to last season, the show has seen a surge among viewers between 26 and 54, the demographic coveted most by advertisers &mdash; and leads its rivals in that category as well.&rdquo; One more thing to keep in mind: Darcy reported Wednesday that earlier this year, Weiss quietly explored the idea of replacing Margaret Brennan as moderator of &ldquo;Face the Nation,&rdquo; but, Darcy wrote, &ldquo;that potential move never seemed to materialize.&rdquo; Darcy added, &ldquo;Now, with two of the show&rsquo;s most influential editorial figures on their way out, Weiss will have an opportunity to &mdash; at the very least &mdash; install her preferred leadership and move the program in a different direction.&rdquo; Biden looks back Former President Joe Biden has been in the news a lot lately. That&rsquo;s because he has a memoir coming out next week: &ldquo;Promise Me, America&rdquo; On Wednesday, People magazine published a Q&A he did with Nick Maslow: &ldquo;Joe Biden, in His Only New Interview, Goes on Camera with PEOPLE to Talk About Everything.&rdquo; Well, not quite everything. But they did hit on plenty of topics: Biden&rsquo;s prostate cancer, his marriage and family, his faith, and of course, his thoughts on the presidency and Donald Trump&rsquo;s redecorating of the White House. Maslow asked, &ldquo;What&rsquo;s your reaction to the Trump ballroom and the East Wing being destroyed?&rdquo; Biden said, &ldquo;Almost as disturbing as the gold bars on the walls of the White House and the Oval Office. I mean, what the hell are we talking about? I find it disturbing. &hellip; I&rsquo;ve viewed the president&rsquo;s judgment in tearing down the East Wing and a number of other things as nothing more than to try to create monuments to himself: &lsquo;I built that.&rsquo; I find it beneath the country.&rdquo; Biden was also asked if he would have done anything differently in 2024. Biden said, &ldquo;What I can&rsquo;t say, because there&rsquo;s no evidence of it &mdash; if I had known that I was feeling lousy because I had cancer, I would have resigned. I would&rsquo;ve not gone forward, because I don&rsquo;t think an 80-year-old man with cancer should be running for (president). But that wasn&rsquo;t the case. &hellip; I believe that on an objective basis, I was the best-equipped person to do the job.&rdquo; Caught on video U.S. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth, right, and his wife Jennifer Rauchet attend a ceremony at the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier at Arlington National Cemetery in Arlington, Va. on Wednesday. (AP Photo/Mark Schiefelbein) One day after House Republicans blocked an effort to impeach him, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and his entourage were verbally reprimanded by a soldier guarding the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier after someone in the group laughed loudly. Following a Veterans Day ceremony, Hegseth went to the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier at Arlington National Cemetery with his wife, Jennifer Hegseth, chief of staff Ricky Buria, deputy chief of staff Patrick Weaver and senior adviser Tami Radabaugh. As the group dispersed after posing for photos near the steps, someone laughed. A U.S. Army sentinel guarding the tomb turned toward the group and yelled, &ldquo;All visitors will maintain an atmosphere of silence and respect at all times!&rdquo; CNN ran a video of the incident. Chief Pentagon spokesman Sean Parnell said in a statement, &ldquo;The Secretary and the entire Department have always honored &mdash; and will always honor &mdash; the sacrifices our military men and women have made to keep America safe. While commemorating this solemn day, a soldier correctly and respectfully reminded everyone to maintain decorum.&rdquo; Bad news at ONA The Online News Association has canceled its annual conference for 2027 and is parting ways with its longtime staff. ONA executive director and CEO Niketa Patel said in a note that it was a difficult decision, writing, &ldquo;Before the pandemic, the annual conference was ONA&rsquo;s biggest source of revenue, and together with grants, it sustained the organization. This is no longer true. The costs of large convenings have climbed sharply while professional development and sponsorship budgets have shrunk. We worked to diversify our revenue, but the Board and I have reached the point where we must decide what ONA can continue and what it must pause.&rdquo; My Poynter colleague Kristen Hare wrote in her Local Edition newsletter , &ldquo;I&rsquo;m not sure if it&rsquo;s the end of an era, the era was already over and we&rsquo;re just now calling it, or this is really just a pause.&rdquo; Tony Elkins, a faculty member at Poynter, told Hare, &ldquo;To say ONA was important to me is an understatement for two big reasons. First, the people I met at the annual conference have been instrumental to the rather odd path my career has taken. There have been so many hallway sessions that ultimately led me to where I am now. The second reason is that it helped me hone my skills as a speaker and a facilitator. This is foundational to the teaching I do now, and I don&rsquo;t know if I would have the opportunity to speak in front of so many people, in rooms of all shapes and sizes. I really do hope the organization can find its footing because, with all credit to my favorite session by Robert Hernandez, the future of journalism feels (blank) without it.&rdquo; Poynter faculty member Alex Mahadevan, the director of MediaWise, told Hare, &ldquo;ONA was particularly helpful for lonely-coder introverts like myself. I was pretty much the only person doing AI (internally and teaching it) at Poynter, and it was cool to meet people in the same position. To vent, of course, but also to come up with cool ideas we could use at our own organizations. I always saw NICAR as a more hands-on skill-building conference, but ONA was a social one. It was super valuable to bond with a cohort.&rdquo; Watch your feet What is it with Rex Ryan and feet? Ryan, the former NFL coach who is now an analyst on ESPN, turned himself in to Nashville police on Wednesday after allegedly running over the feet of two pedestrians while driving his truck downtown last Thursday night and then fleeing the scene. He faces six misdemeanor charges &mdash; two counts each of ​​leaving the scene of an accident, failure to provide information or render aid and disobeying a traffic signal. Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Matt Yoder wrote , &ldquo;Of course, anyone in the know will find that to be incredibly ironic given Ryan&rsquo;s well-documented love of that particular body part.&rdquo; Reports are that the pedestrians had the right of way in a crosswalk as they attempted to cross Broadway in the heart of Nashville&rsquo;s bar and music district. After allegedly hitting the pedestrians while making a turn, Ryan kept on driving. The pedestrians were able to catch up with Ryan when he stopped at a nearby traffic light. Ryan reportedly got out of the truck and argued with them before getting back in and driving off again. Photos were taken of his license plate and reported to police. The pedestrians allegedly suffered minor injuries. In a statement, ESPN said, &ldquo;We are aware and are in touch with Rex.&rdquo; Ryan, 63, is the former head coach of the New York Jets and Buffalo Bills, and joined ESPN in 2017. He is a regular on shows such as &ldquo;Sunday NFL Countdown&rdquo; and &ldquo;Get Up,&rdquo; as well as &ldquo;SportsCenter.&rdquo; TMZ first reported the news of Ryan&rsquo;s arrest. Media tidbits My colleague Loreben Tuquero of Poynter&rsquo;s PolitiFact with &ldquo;Political campaigns spent $187 million promoting AI-generated ads in the 2026 midterms. Did they work?&rdquo; The Wall Street Journal&rsquo;s Noah Higgins-Dunn and Amrith Ramkumar with &ldquo;Three States Where the AI Boom Could Slow After the Midterms.&rdquo; For The Atlantic, Kyle Berlin with &ldquo;The Worst Thing About AI Writing.&rdquo; Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Drew Lerner with &ldquo;Dianna Russini&rsquo;s comeback over before it started as livestream plagued by tech issues.&rdquo; Hot type The Atlantic&rsquo;s Ali Breland with &ldquo;The Christian Nationalists Have a Plan.&rdquo; More resources for journalists Strengthen your leadership skills with Poynter Leadership Academy , designed for experienced newsroom leaders with at least three years of management experience. Develop your strategic thinking, decision-making and ability to lead teams through change alongside a cohort of journalism professionals. Apply by Jan. 18, 2027 . Strengthen your editing skills and earn an industry-recognized certificate from Poynter and ACES . Choose from Introductory, Intermediate or Advanced pathways covering current editing standards, tools and best practices. You can start at the level that&rsquo;s right for you and learn at your own pace. Don't have a researcher on staff? Poynter Research can help you uncover hard-to-find facts, verify claims and track down public records, so you can save time and report with confidence.  Have feedback or a tip? Email Poynter senior media writer Tom Jones at tjones@poynter.org . The Poynter Report is your daily dive into the world of media, packed with the latest news and insights. Get it delivered to your inbox Monday through Friday by signing up here . And don&rsquo;t forget to tune into our biweekly podcast for even more. Support the journalism that keeps you informed. GIVE NOW ADVERTISE // DONATE // LEARN // JOBS Did someone forward you this email? Sign up here. © All rights reserved Poynter Institute 2026 801 Third Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701 If you don't want to receive email updates from Poynter, we understand. You can change your subscription preferences or unsubscribe from all Poynter emails .",
