        "pageDescription": "These days, ESPN NFL analystand former Jets coach Rex Ryan is unapologetic about his infamous foot fetish and what people think of it.",
        "evidenceText": "Rex Ryan unapologetic about foot fetish: 'If a chick's got great feet, I'm looking' Skip to content Credit: HLN By Sean Keeley on 10/22/2024 10/23/2024 Less AI and more AA in your search results. While the era didn’t end well, the current incarnation of the New York Jets has a lot of fans wistful for the days of Rex Ryan. The former Jets head coach took the franchise to back-to-back AFC Championship games and they haven’t even been back to the playoffs in the 13 seasons since. While Aaron Rodgers has helped keep the Jets in the New York tabloid headlines all season long, Ryan had a few controversies of his own while in town. None more memorable than the 2010 revelation that he and his wife had made a foot fetish video that appeared on the internet. While he and the Jets initially tried to deflect attention from the viral video, he would eventually become more playful about it and his interest in feet. These days, it’s not uncommon to see one of his ESPN colleagues or other NFL folks make a foot joke at Rex’s expense while he laughs along with them . Ryan appeared on The Pivot this week where the topic of his foot fetish was broached. The ESPN NFL analyst adamantly defended his appreciation of his wife’s toes. My Birkin, type shit pic.twitter.com/Tfqax1kRoz — Chairman of #FeetFridays and #SolesSaturday (@FlyoutChase) October 23, 2024 “I love my wife’s feet,” said Ryan. “I don’t know what it is. But if a chick’s got jacked-up feet, I’m out. I don’t care how gorgeous. I’m out. I don’t know what the hell it is. Some people are boob guys, leg guys. Of course, I notice a pretty gorgeous face, whatever… but I’m checking the feet out.” Ryan added that he thought his wife was “gonna frickin’ kill me” when the video went viral but was shocked to find out that many of his Jets players admitted they had a similar fetish. “Now I have fun with it and it’s like, yeah, that’s me. That’s who I am. I own that shit,” he added. “And if a chick’s got great feet, I’m looking.” [ The Pivot , Image Credit: HLN ] About Sean Keeley Along with writing for Awful Announcing and The Comeback, Sean is the Managing Editor for Comeback Media. Previously, he created the Syracuse blog Troy Nunes Is An Absolute Magician and wrote 'How To Grow An Orange: The Right Way to Brainwash Your Child Into Rooting for Syracuse.' He has also written non-Syracuse-related things for SB Nation, Curbed, and other outlets. He currently lives in Seattle where he is complaining about bagels. Send tips/comments/complaints to sean@awfulannouncing.com Sean writes the Awful Announcing 'The A Block' newsletter every Thursday. Subscribe here . View all posts by Sean Keeley Follow on Twitter Related Content The SEC’s TV ratings dominance is getting scary for the Big Ten Jordon Hudson reportedly sues Airbnb owners over leak of topless Bill Belichick footage The 76ers are either building a superteam or creating a superproblem The Dave Portnoy ‘Big Noon Kickoff’ breakup was always inevitable Recent Posts The league says live match viewers per week increased 93%. The beloved commentator died from an \"acute illness.\" Tomlin stepped away from the Pittsburgh Steelers in January and quickly became a hot media prospect before NBC scooped him up. Credit: Kirby Lee-Imagn Images \"It is so much easier to get an audience and make money [by] appealing to the lowest common denominator of the MAGA-sphere than it is to do it in sports.\" \"Maybe, just maybe, the apology can be as loud as y'all leading y'all shows with this topic.\" Sidebar Popular Posts Five NFL teams play three consecutive primetime games this season; zero did so last year. Mike Florio thinks he knows why Where have you gone, Lee Corso? College football fans falling out of love with Saturday pregame shows Recent Posts The league says live match viewers per week increased 93%. The beloved commentator died from an \"acute illness.\" Tomlin stepped away from the Pittsburgh Steelers in January and quickly became a hot media prospect before NBC scooped him up. Credit: Kirby Lee-Imagn Images \"It is so much easier to get an audience and make money [by] appealing to the lowest common denominator of the MAGA-sphere than it is to do it in sports.\" \"Maybe, just maybe, the apology can be as loud as y'all leading y'all shows with this topic.\"",
