        "evidenceText": "Husted rents D.C. home from Meta lobbyist, won't disclose rent — TiffinOhio.net Skip to main content Type at least 2 characters to search. local U.S. Senator Jon Husted (Photo via Facebook) U.S. Sen. Jon Husted, R-Ohio, rents an apartment in a Washington, D.C., house owned by David Lugar, a lobbyist whose clients include Meta. Politico reported that neither Husted nor Lugar would say how much rent he pays. Husted's spokesperson called it strictly a landlord-tenant relationship, and Lugar said he has never lobbied Husted. Key points What Lugar's April 2026 federal filing shows about his lobbying for Meta Husted's years as lieutenant governor promoting Ohio's data-center tax exemptions How the Ohio Democratic Party responded days before Husted faces Sherrod Brown By Dave Miller U.S. Sen. Jon Husted is living in a Washington, D.C., property owned by a corporate lobbyist who represents Meta, one of the technology giants at the center of Ohio’s controversial data-center expansion. Neither the Republican senator nor his landlord would provide Politico with the amount Husted pays in rent. The arrangement was exposed Friday in a Politico investigation identifying Husted’s landlord as David Lugar, a veteran Washington lobbyist whose clients include Meta, Boeing and Wells Fargo. Husted rents a separate apartment inside a multifamily house owned by Lugar, who co-chairs the lobbying firm 535 Group and chairs The Lugar Group. Republican Sen. Todd Young of Indiana also rents an apartment in the building. Lugar told Politico that he does not live or work at the property. Both Husted’s office and Lugar insist the senator pays fair-market rent. But when Politico asked how much Husted actually pays, neither provided an amount. Husted’s spokesperson described the arrangement as “strictly a landlord and tenant relationship,” while Lugar maintained that he has never lobbied Husted or his staff. Those assurances leave a central fact undisclosed: the amount of money a sitting U.S. senator pays to a corporate lobbyist whose clients include one of the companies benefiting from Ohio’s enormous data-center expansion. Federal records confirm Husted’s landlord lobbies for Meta Lugar’s relationship with Meta is documented in federal lobbying disclosures. An April 20, 2026, filing , bearing Lugar’s digital signature, identifies Meta as a client of 535 Group. The firm reported receiving $30,000 in lobbying income from the company during the first three months of 2026. The filing names Lugar as a lobbyist on matters involving online advertising, competition legislation, privacy and encryption. It identifies the U.S. Senate among the institutions covered by those lobbying activities, although it does not identify Husted’s office as a lobbying target. Politico reported that Lugar acknowledged lobbying the office of his other Senate tenant, Todd Young, during Young’s tenancy. Lugar said he had never lobbied Husted or members of Husted’s staff. The financial relationship nevertheless places Husted in a privately owned Washington residence controlled by a lobbyist whose corporate clients have significant business and policy interests before Congress. Husted spent years championing Ohio’s data-center boom The disclosure comes just days before Ohio voters decide whether Husted will retain his Senate seat in a closely watched contest against Democrat Sherrod Brown, who has made Husted’s record of supporting data centers and corporate tax incentives a central campaign issue. Before entering the Senate in 2025, Husted spent six years as Ohio’s lieutenant governor under Republican Gov. Mike DeWine. During that period, he repeatedly promoted data-center development, defended tax incentives for technology companies and helped announce major projects across the state. One of those projects was in northwest Ohio. In September 2023, DeWine and Husted announced approval of a 75%, 15-year data-center tax exemption for Aligned Data Centers in Perkins Township, Erie County. The development was projected to create just 18 full-time jobs. The tax exemption was formally approved by the Ohio Tax Credit Authority, but the DeWine-Husted administration publicly championed the project as part of its economic development agenda. Ohio’s broader data-center tax incentives have since proven vastly more expensive than state officials anticipated. According to the nonpartisan Ohio Legislative Service Commission, data-center sales-tax exemptions cost Ohio approximately $1.57 billion in forgone revenue during 2025 alone. The cost was more than 11 times the state’s earlier forecast of approximately $136 million. In May 2026, DeWine suspended consideration of new data-center tax exemptions while lawmakers examined the industry’s rapidly growing footprint and financial consequences. Existing agreements, however, remained in place. Meta’s expansion reaches northwest Ohio Meta, the company represented by Husted’s landlord, is developing its massive Prometheus artificial intelligence computing complex in New Albany, Ohio, while arranging enormous quantities of electricity to support its growing infrastructure. Its energy interests extend directly into northwest Ohio. In January, Meta announced major nuclear energy agreements , including long-term electricity purchases involving the Davis-Besse nuclear power plant near Oak Harbor in Ottawa County and the Perry nuclear plant in northeast Ohio. Those agreements connect Meta’s expanding artificial intelligence operations to Ohio’s existing energy infrastructure and the broader regional electricity grid. Meta says it pays the full costs of the energy consumed by its data centers and supports investments to expand electricity generation. Meanwhile, Husted has attempted to reposition himself on data centers as growing public opposition to corporate tax breaks, electricity costs and the industry’s expansion has become a major political issue. In July, Husted introduced the Ratepayer Protection Act, legislation intended to address whether technology companies should pay for the additional electricity infrastructure required by their facilities. The measure would require states to consider standards for allocating those costs, rather than directly mandate them nationwide. It failed to advance in the Senate in September. That effort followed years in which Husted had publicly defended incentives intended to bring the same industry into Ohio. Husted’s rent remains undisclosed The Ohio Democratic Party denounced the rental arrangement Saturday, accusing Husted of benefiting from a sweetheart deal with a lobbyist for Meta. Husted rents housing from Lugar, Lugar lobbies for Meta, and neither supplied Politico with the amount of rent being paid. The disclosure comes as Ohioans confront the financial consequences of the data-center industry Husted spent years promoting, including billions of dollars in tax incentives and disputes over who should pay for the electricity infrastructure needed to support massive technology facilities. Husted has spent months arguing that powerful technology companies should pay their own way. When asked what he pays to live in a property owned by a lobbyist representing one of those companies, however, his office provided assurances that the price was fair without revealing what that price actually is. Husted faces Democratic challenger Sherrod Brown in the Tuesday, Nov. 3 general election. See more of our trusted coverage in your search results. Add TiffinOhio.net on Google Never miss another breaking news story. Join our Facebook Messenger channel today. Add TiffinOhio.net on Messenger By Dave Miller Dave Miller is a journalist and political analyst based in Northwest Ohio. His reporting focuses on local and state politics, economic policy and issues affecting working families. dmiller@northstarcivic.com More from TiffinOhio.net For You › Sign in or create a free account for personalized recommendations, saved stories, and more. Loading recommendations… Husted championed Ohio’s data center boom — and defended the billions in tax breaks that fueled it Newly surfaced recordings capture Jon Husted praising data centers as a pipeline of jobs and economic growth. His years of support for the industry and its tax incentives preceded a $1.6 billion annual tax-break bill for Ohio and mounting concerns about electricity prices. October 20, 2026 Husted championed Ohio’s data center boom — and defended the billions in tax breaks that fueled it Newly surfaced recordings capture Jon Husted praising data centers as a pipeline of jobs and economic growth. His years of support for the industry and its tax incentives preceded a $1.6 billion annual tax-break bill for Ohio and mounting concerns about electricity prices. By Dave Miller Husted’s data center bill would help industry, not consumers, critics say Sen. Jon Husted’s Ratepayer Protection Act would require states to consider protections for consumers. September 29, 2026 Husted’s data center bill would help industry, not consumers, critics say Sen. Jon Husted’s Ratepayer Protection Act would require states to consider protections for consumers. By Josh Israel Husted helped fuel Ohio’s data center boom. His consumer bill is built on Big Tech’s voluntary pledge. Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta fought Ohio's binding cost-sharing tariff, then signed the voluntary pledge Husted's federal bill only requires states to consider adopting. September 26, 2026 Husted helped fuel Ohio’s data center boom. His consumer bill is built on Big Tech’s voluntary pledge. Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta fought Ohio's binding cost-sharing tariff, then signed the voluntary pledge Husted's federal bill only requires states to consider adopting. By Dave Miller Husted took $630K from data center interests Husted personally championed a sales tax exemption that helped bring a Google data center to New Albany, and his top aide has taken $94,000 in donations. September 18, 2026 Husted took $630K from data center interests Husted personally championed a sales tax exemption that helped bring a Google data center to New Albany, and his top aide has taken $94,000 in donations. By Jesse Valentine Northwest Ohio rally targets Jon Husted over Ohio data center boom, rising electric costs A GOP memo obtained by Axios warns data center backlash could cost Husted his seat, the same argument protesters made outside his Lima appearance. August 19, 2026 Northwest Ohio rally targets Jon Husted over Ohio data center boom, rising electric costs A GOP memo obtained by Axios warns data center backlash could cost Husted his seat, the same argument protesters made outside his Lima appearance. By Dave Miller Leaked GOP memo: Data centers are ‘anchor hanging around Jon Husted’s neck’ The NRSC memo urges AI companies to fix their own public image, warning that a Husted loss could make politicians nationwide wary of backing data centers. August 19, 2026 Leaked GOP memo: Data centers are ‘anchor hanging around Jon Husted’s neck’ The NRSC memo urges AI companies to fix their own public image, warning that a Husted loss could make politicians nationwide wary of backing data centers. By Dave Miller Tap to finish signing up & listen Jon Husted lives in Meta lobbyist’s D.C. house, won’t say how much he pays in rent 7 min Jon Husted lives in Meta lobbyist’s D.C. house, won’t say how much he pays in rent by Dave Miller Newsletter required Subscribe free to listen to this story This article includes a 7 min AI narration. Sign up for our free newsletter to unlock listening and the full story — no spam, unsubscribe anytime. Subscribe free to unlock Close 0:00 7 min Share Your Feedback We want to hear from you. Whether you've spotted an error, have a story idea, or just want to share your thoughts—we're listening. Article Title Feedback Type * Select feedback type… Report an Error or Correction Suggest Additional Context Share a Related Story Idea Positive Feedback Display or 