        "evidenceText": "The OSINT Newsletter - Issue #130 - The OSINT Newsletter The OSINT Newsletter Subscribe Sign in The OSINT Newsletter - Issue #130 OSINT Tool Tuesday: MW Geofind Nov 10, 2026 16 1 Share 👋 Welcome to OSINT Tool Tuesday. This week we're looking at MW Geofind ; an online OSINT tool that helps you discover YouTube videos based on geographical location. Rather than scrolling endlessly through YouTube hoping to stumble across something useful, you can search for geotagged videos in specific locations and explore the results on an interactive map. 🚨 This tool has been added to the OSINT Resources for Community Discovery page on The OSINT Newsletter for easy reference later. That list serves as a roadmap for new tutorials in the future. If there are any tools you’d like to see added to the list and covered, please reach out to jake@osint.news with details. This Substack is reader-supported. To receive new posts and support my work, consider becoming a free or paid subscriber. Subscribe 🪃 If you missed the last newsletter, here’s a link to catch up. ⚡ Out at Sea: OSINT and Maritime The OSINT Newsletter - Issue #129 Nov 5 Read full story 🎙️ If you prefer to listen, here’s a link to the podcast instead. Episode 25: Disc Golf Apps, Tracking Aircraft, and Searching with FOFA The OSINT Newsletter · Oct 30 Listen now Let’s get started. ⬇️ MW Geofind MW Geofind is a free online search tool that allows you to find YouTube videos that have geographical location information attached to them. You can search using a location name or coordinates, add keywords, specify a search radius, and narrow down results using various filters. The really useful part? Your results are displayed on a map, making it much easier to explore where videos have been geotagged and identify potential material for further investigation. 🎩 H/T: MW Geofind/ Matthew Wright The platform offers three main search options: Location Search: Find geotagged YouTube videos within a specified geographical area. Topic Search: Search YouTube using keywords and identify results that contain geotag information. Channel Search: Investigate the uploads from particular YouTube channels and identify videos with geotags. In this guide, we’ll concentrate on Location Search, explore the available filters, look at how to investigate the results, and consider where MW Geofind might fit into your wider OSINT workflow. Let’s get started! ⬇️ Getting Started Simply head over to MW Geofind in your browser and you’ll be presented with the Location Search interface. (No downloads, complicated installations, or lengthy setup processes required!) The basic idea is simple: choose a location, decide how far around that location you’d like to search, add any keywords or filters that might help, and let MW Geofind do the searching. You don’t even need to know the exact coordinates of a location. You can search using a place name or interact directly with the map. Let’s see what that looks like… Searching Locations The first option is to search for a location. For example, you might enter a well-known landmark, town, city, or address into the location search field. You can also interact directly with the map by dragging and dropping a point, right-clicking to select a location or using the available map controls. This is particularly useful when you’re researching somewhere that doesn’t have an obvious address or when you already have a specific point of interest in mind. Coordinates You don’t have to rely on location names either. MW Geofind allows you to work with geographical coordinates, which is especially handy when you’ve already identified a location during an investigation. This also helps when searching internationally, where place names, spellings and languages might otherwise complicate your research. ❗Beware, there is an odd MW Geofind quirk that identical coordinates can be associated with different geographical descriptions. For example, our Trafalgar Square, London coordinates also correspond with central Cordoba, Spain. Search Radius Once you’ve selected your location, the next step is choosing your search radius. MW Geofind allows you to specify a radius of up to 1,000km. This is where things get interesting! A smaller radius is useful when you’re looking at a specific building, landmark or neighbourhood. A larger radius can help when researching a wider geographical area or when you’re not entirely sure where relevant footage might have been uploaded from. For example: 5km: A relatively focused search around a particular location. 20km: A wider search covering surrounding neighbourhoods and nearby areas. 100km: Useful when expanding your search to include a larger region. 🗒️ Remember, the radius is a search parameter rather than a guarantee that every result will fall neatly within that boundary. MW Geofind notes that some results may appear outside the selected radius. Keywords You can also introduce keywords to make your searches more specific. Think about what you might expect to find in the videos you’re researching. For example, if you’re investigating a particular event, you could try relevant terms such as: Protest Demonstration Flooding Fire Festival Road closure You can use YouTube-style keyword search operators too, which can help refine your searches. For example, we searched ‘protest’ in London with a 20km radius: One thing worth remembering is that keyword searching works best when you give it enough room to find relevant results. The developer recommends using keywords with larger search radii or in more densely populated locations. A useful approach is to begin with a broader search, see what’s being returned, and then introduce additional keywords to narrow things down. Filtering Results This is where you can start getting a little more creative with your searches. MW Geofind includes several filters that allow you to refine the results and focus on the videos most relevant to your investigation as shown below: Depending on what you’re looking for, you can filter by: Timeframe and Date Range You can select a timeframe or specify particular start and end dates. This is particularly useful when investigating an event that occurred on a known date or when looking for footage uploaded during a particular period. For example, if you’re researching an incident that took place between 1 and 15 October, you can use the date fields to focus your search on that period. Duration You can also filter results by video duration. This can help when you’re looking for shorter clips rather than longer recordings, or when a particular type of footage is more likely to appear in one format than another. Language The language filter can help narrow down results when you’re investigating content associated with a particular language, especially useful during international conflicts such as the Russia-Ukraine war. Sort By You can adjust how results are sorted, helping you explore the returned videos in a way that makes sense for your research. Options include: date, rating, relevance, title, and view count. Additional Filters There are also some additional options available, including: Live events only Creative Commons licensed videos High-quality videos 3D videos Paid promotions Not every filter will be relevant to every investigation, but it’s worth experimenting to see which combinations produce the most useful results. 🗒️ A little tip: Don’t apply every filter straight away. Sometimes being too specific can mean missing useful material. Start broad, review what’s available and gradually refine your search. Investigative Use Cases So, where could MW Geofind fit into an actual investigation? Let’s look at a few examples… Event Research One of the most obvious applications is researching events. Imagine you’re investigating a public demonstration, sporting event, or major incident. You know roughly where it happened, but you’re looking for additional publicly available footage. You could enter the location, select an appropriate search radius, and use relevant keywords to identify potentially useful YouTube videos. This might help uncover footage you hadn’t come across through conventional searches. Geolocation Research MW Geofind can also support wider geolocation investigations. If you’re researching a particular location, you can use the tool to identify videos that have been geotagged in the surrounding area. These videos may contain visual material that can help you understand the environment, identify landmarks or generate new research leads. 🗒️Remember that a video’s geotag is not the same thing as independently verifying where the footage was actually recorded. Timeline Development The date filters can also be useful when developing an investigative timeline. For example, you might be researching an incident and want to identify videos uploaded around the same period. By adjusting the date range, you can explore potentially relevant footage and compare the available information against other sources. 🗒️Just remember that the upload date doesn’t necessarily tell you when the footage was recorded. Channel Research Don’t overlook the Channel Search functionality. If you’ve already identified a YouTube channel of interest, you can enter its channel URL or handle and examine its uploads for geotagged videos. This can be useful when researching a channel’s publicly available content and looking for geographical information that might otherwise be missed. Finding Additional Investigative Leads Sometimes the most useful result isn’t necessarily the video you originally set out to find. A search might reveal another video, a different channel, or an interesting geographical connection that gives you somewhere new to look. These findings can provide additional leads to investigate using other OSINT resources. Understanding Results: A Few Important Considerations re Geotags Before getting too carried away with all those map markers, there’s a few things worth noting. YouTube geotags are optional and are added by video creators when uploading their content. They’re not automatically generated from the video’s EXIF data. This means there are several reasons why the location information might not tell the whole story. ❗ Not every video is geotagged. If a creator hasn’t added location information, MW Geofind won’t find that video through its location search. ❗ Geotags can be inaccurate. Creators can select an incorrect location, whether intentionally or accidentally. ❗ Coordinates aren’t always precise. YouTube location metadata can associate the same coordinates with different geographical descriptions, such as a country, city or landmark. ❗ The results aren’t comprehensive. MW Geofind only searches the subset of YouTube videos that have relevant geographical information available. ❗ A geotag doesn’t prove where someone was. A video could be tagged with a location that has no direct connection to where the creator or recording device was physically located. This is why it’s so important to verify any geographical information independently. Platform Pivots MW Geofind becomes even more useful when you start combining it with other OSINT resources. For example, you could use MW Geofind to discover a potentially relevant video, examine its metadata using MW Metadata, and then investigate the visual content using maps, satellite imagery and other geolocation resources. Other useful resources might include: Google Maps and Google Earth for geographical context. Google Street View for comparing visible landmarks and surroundings. OpenStreetMap for exploring geographical features. InVID-WeVerify for video verification and analysis. YouTube itself for exploring additional content and channel information. OPSEC Considerations Like any OSINT tool, MW Geofind has limitations. One important consideration is that it relies on the YouTube API, meaning searches are subject to API rate limits. If the daily quota is exhausted, the tool may become temporarily unresponsive until the quota resets. From an OPSEC and ethical perspective, there are also a few things to remember: Use publicly available information responsibly and in accordance with applicable laws. Avoid assuming that a video creator was physically present at the location attached to their video. Consider the privacy of individuals appearing in publicly uploaded footage. Be mindful of how sensitive location information is recorded, stored and shared. Preserve relevant source URLs and the date of your research. Verify important findings using independent sources before making investigative conclusions. Additional Tips Here’s a few ways to get more out of MW Geofind: Look at the map before opening videos. Take a moment to examine how results are distributed geographically. Clusters of videos in a particular area might help you identify locations worth investigating further. Pay attention to video titles and descriptions. These can provide useful context about the content, particularly when the thumbnail alone doesn’t tell you much. Check the original upload. Whenever you find something potentially relevant, open the video on YouTube and examine the original page rather than relying entirely on the information displayed in MW Geofind. Look for unusual results. A video that seems unrelated to your original search might still contain something useful. Don’t dismiss unexpected results too quickly! Compare thumbnails with the video itself. A thumbnail can give you a quick preview, but always watch the relevant footage before making assumptions about its contents. Be aware of deleted or unavailable videos. Not every result will necessarily remain accessible. If you encounter an unavailable video, make a note of the information still visible and look for independently verifiable references. Make use of the metadata links. When a video catches your attention, explore the additional information available through MW Metadata to see whether it provides anything useful for your research. 🔍 One final tip: If MW Geofind doesn’t return anything useful, don’t automatically assume there are no relevant videos. Try a conventional YouTube search as well. Videos without geotags can still contain valuable information in their titles, descriptions and visual content. 🏁 A new CTF is live: OIL EXPORT A suspicious tanker. A network of opaque shipping companies. A trail of maritime movements. A maritime intelligence analyst has received an alert about a tanker suspected of participating in Russian oil exports through a network of opaque shipping companies. 🕵️ Your mission: Starting with a single IMO number, uncover the vessel’s name, callsign, and the countries it has previously been associated with. 🔎 Can you connect the dots ? Compete now! 🪃 If you missed the last CTF, here’s a link to catch up . Last week’s CTF challenge featured a challenge titled “URL INSPECTION”, in which participants were tasked with identifying the exact date and time a suspect had accessed a URL. Challenge solution WU: To solve this challenge, participants need to use the tool previously presented in the newsletter, called unfurl: https://hindsig.ht/unfurl/ by entering the url, the tool will decode the Facebook fbclid and return the date and time. ✅ That’s it for the free version of The OSINT Newsletter. Consider upgrading to a paid subscription to support this publication and independent research. By upgrading to paid, you’ll get access to the following: 👀 All paid posts in the archive. Go back and see what you’ve missed ! 🚀 If you don’t have a paid subscription already, don’t worry. There’s a 7-day free trial. If you like what you’re reading, upgrade your subscription. If you can’t, I totally understand. Be on the lookout for promotions throughout the year. 🚨 The OSINT Newsletter offers a free premium subscription to all members of law enforcement. To upgrade your subscription, please reach out to LEA@osint.news from your official law enforcement email address. This Substack is reader-supported. To receive new posts and support my work, consider becoming a free or paid subscriber. Subscribe 16 1 Share Previous Discussion about this post Comments Restacks Top Latest Discussions Ready for more? Subscribe © 2026 Jake Creps · Privacy ∙ Terms ∙ Collection notice Start your Substack Get the app Substack is the home for writing, podcasting, and media founders.",
