        "pageTitle": "Opinion | The View From Space the U.S. Government Doesn’t Want You to See - The New York Times",
        "pageDescription": "There are images from space that the U.S. government doesn’t want the public to see. Here’s why.",
        "evidenceText": "Opinion | The View From Space the U.S. Government Doesn’t Want You to See - The New York Times Opinion | The View From Space the U.S. Government Doesn’t Want You to See https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/opinion/satellite-images-us-government.html Share full article 420 Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT You have a preview view of this article while we are checking your access. When we have confirmed access, the full article content will load. The U.S. government is trying to censor your view from space. Ollie Ballinger, an open-source researcher and lecturer, uses satellite imagery to monitor conflicts around the world. But he says pressure from the government has prompted private companies to restrict access to images of places it may not want the public to see. Why would companies listen to these requests? “Any pressure from the U.S. government carries a lot of weight for these companies,” Mr. Ballinger says, “because the American government is their biggest customer.” In the Opinion video above, he reveals how these restrictions are limiting a vital tool for accountability and public safety and warns of a future in which governments have the power to control what the public can see and, ultimately, what it can know. Below is an edited transcript of this video. We recommend watching it in its original form for the full effect. You can do so using the video player above. Ollie Ballinger: Let me show you something the U.S. government doesn’t want you to see. The aftermath of American missile strikes on schools and homes, revealed by satellite imagery. This view from space has been used to uncover destruction in Gaza, mass graves in Ukraine, internment camps in China and the Minab school strike in Iran. Journalists, humanitarian groups and researchers like me rely on satellite imagery to monitor conflicts, natural disasters and atrocities around the world. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. If you are in Reader mode please exit and log into your Times account, or subscribe for all of The Times. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. Already a subscriber? Log in . Want all of The Times? Subscribe . Related Content Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
