        "httpStatus": 200,
        "finalUrl": "https://www.cjr.org/laurels-and-darts/appalachian-williford-midterm-elections-boone-north-carolina-free-press-reingold-weiss-el-sayed-islamophobia-michigan-get-huge-clavicular-looksmaxxing.php",
        "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
        "pageTitle": "Picture Perfect - Columbia Journalism Review",
        "pageDescription": "A photograph that captures the reality of our political moment. Plus, an AI-generated reflection of Islamophobia from The Free Press, and a dystopian documentary on male body image.",
        "evidenceText": "Picture Perfect - Columbia Journalism Review b Ray Russell campaigner Noah Derucher and onlookers view election results at a Democratic watch party. (Photo: Noah Williford) Laurels and Darts b Sign up for the daily CJR newsletter . Sometimes an image captures a moment, and a mood, perfectly. That was the case last week when Noah Williford , a photographer for The Appalachian , Appalachian State University’s independent student paper, took the picture above at FizzEd, a bar in Boone, North Carolina, on the night of the midterm elections. “You could feel this vibe shift,” Williford told me. “It felt like this representation of what was going on nationally in this little bar and restaurant downtown.” Williford told me that The Appalachian , founded in 1934, fills an important local news gap. “A lot of smaller newspapers are owned by larger companies and are letting go of staff or increasing prices. People in places like Appalachia are going to look for news that they both can trust and can afford. And that historically is student media,” he said. “Not just across Appalachia, but also across the state of North Carolina, people are increasingly looking to their local college newspaper, their local college radio station.” Williford’s picture gained national attention when Chris Moody, one of his professors at Appalachian State, posted it on X with the caption: “This photo from Election Night in Boone, North Carolina, is a modern Norman Rockwell painting.” The post now has more than twenty-four thousand likes. Williford said he grabbed the shot just as people in the room learned that the New York Times had flipped its prediction in North Carolina’s Fifth Congressional District race, from Virginia Foxx, a Republican who has held office since 2005, to her Democratic challenger, Chuck Hubbard. (As of press time for this column, the race had yet to be called.) “The thing that makes me grin about it, especially in today’s day and age, is that it’s shockingly earnest. It’s people who decided to go out on a Tuesday night just with the hope of something good, you know? There’s no cynicism,” Williford told me. “You realize this blue wave, as we call it, is thousands of people across the United States, collectively trying to believe in something different.” It was not just the White House and MAGA Republicans who had a no-good, very-bad week last week. The Free Press , Bari Weiss’s erstwhile blog, which Paramount acquired for roughly a hundred and fifty million dollars last year, had a run of editorial mistakes that suggested a stunning lack of basic journalistic standards. On Tuesday night, Olivia Reingold, a Free Press writer who was once described as “Bari Weiss’s most loyal soldier,” broke the “news” on X that she had spotted “Valentino heels, a Chanel bag, a Moncler jacket, Rolex watches, and a YSL bag among other designer items” at what she said was “Abdul El-Sayed’s election night event.” The post included a picture of a woman’s feet in Valentino Rockstud leather heels and a quilted gold Chanel bag. The implication, I think, was that Reingold was exposing hypocrisy among the champagne crowd at El-Sayed’s party. Whatever the aim, within an hour she posted a curious clarification: the woman photographed was not at Senator-elect El-Sayed’s election party, she was at a “party for the entire Michigan Democratic Party, so it’s a data point for the entire state apparatus, not just Abdul El-Sayed’s camp.” What was the data point exactly? Who knows. But given the Islamaphobic bent of Reingold’s previous coverage, the signs point to something racist.  That was not the end of it. Social media sleuths quickly pointed out that the photo was AI-generated. ( Vanity Fair published an impressive forensic fashion analysis on why the items were not authentic.) Two days later, Reingold posted what I suppose qualifies as a second clarification: “Hello my name is Olivia Reingold and I used ChatGPT to brighten and sharpen an image of a woman in Valentino shoes and a Chanel bag. After processing, it still shows these same things: a woman wearing Valentino shoes and a Chanel bag. She is a real (and chic!) woman, really wearing those items. The observation holds.” She still has not told us what, exactly, the observation was supposed to be. The AI image is still on her feed.  If Reingold understands that using AI to “brighten and sharpen an image” is generally not acceptable practice in news, you certainly wouldn’t know it from her note, which gives the impression that she is more affronted by being held to a journalistic standard than concerned about having violated one. A spokesperson for The Free Press did not respond to a question about whether the publication, as an entity of CBS News, has any policy governing the use of AI, particularly when it comes to producing or altering photographs. Reingold, who was last seen filming herself (rather than the scene) at the White House Correspondents’ Association dinner shooting, rose to Free Press fame with popular influencer-style videos. Lately, Maya Sulkin, another Free Press reporter, has joined her ranks. Last Wednesday, Sulkin posted a reaction video to The Free Press ’s X account in which she blankly watched El-Sayed’s victory speech and declared that he had delivered it in Arabic. She also implied that Ilhan Omar, the representative from Minnesota, delivered her speech in Somali, and concluded that Americans had just elected “socialist and Islamist people who don’t share American values and want to completely change America.” After viewers of the video complained, a community note was added to the post: “Apart from his own name spoken idiomatically with Arabic pronunciation, Abdulrahman Mohamed El-Sayed used four Arabic words in his victory speech, which represents 0.27% of the words he spoke.”  The Free Press first added a correction on X, saying the video was inaccurate (but not adding anything where the video appeared on Facebook, Instagram, or TikTok), before finally deleting the post two days later. In a statement, The Free Press said it had taken down the video because it “contained a factual error and its tone fell short of what we believe advances healthy public discourse” and did not meet the standards of the outlet’s “rigorous editorial process.” A spokesperson declined to answer questions about the editorial and ethical standards at The Free Press and CBS News, describe the editorial process referenced, or clarify whether anyone at the publication would face consequences for the incident, including Sulkin. You might reasonably think that that was finally the end of it. You’d be wrong. Anand Giridharadas revealed on social media late last week that he had recorded a podcast interview with Reingold about his new book, Man in the Mirror, that hadn’t been released, and suggested it had been shelved for ideological reasons. “It was about the Jordan Neely/Daniel Penny case, which we both covered,” Giridharadas posted. “It was a powerful conversation because there were real differences.” When I reached out to him, Giridharadas told me that he had done the interview precisely because he hoped “to try to get out of echo chambers.” ( The Free Press did not respond to my questions about this, either.) The Free Press is no longer the small, contrarian digital darling that helped Weiss improbably ascend to the top spot at CBS News. For better or worse, it is now part of a global news operation that is supposed to display the highest editorial integrity. One way Weiss could demonstrate that she is up to the task would be to demand that the publications and journalists she oversees adhere to exacting standards. But, to do that, she’d have to see the value in it. As Giridharadas told me, “I would just say to Bari and the leadership, they right now have a scandal of publishing fake AI-generated content. They should feel proud that the content they generated with me was the opposite of AI. It was two human beings, deeply grappling across difference and having the conversation. And if they are more comfortable publishing fake photographs than real conversations, she has ceased to be the person she promised she would be.” If you’re even moderately aware of online culture, you’ve probably heard of Clavicular, the influencer widely regarded as the world’s number one “looksmaxxer.” For the uninitiated, looksmaxxing is a subculture, popular among young men and adolescent boys, built on obsessive pursuit of physical perfection, a phenomenon that evolved out of incel communities and the manosphere. As The Atlantic ’s Ali Breland told CJR’s Ivan L. Nagy back in February, the guys in this world are “symptomatic of the nihilistic stream of slop that is going viral now.” Get Huge: Looksmaxxing to Death , a fascinating new docuseries released by Hulu on Tuesday, explores the origins of looksmaxxing, tracing its roots to a controversial fitness personality named Anthony Hughes, better known online as Tony Huge. A biohacking guru with cartoonishly large muscles and a devoted online following, Huge lives in Thailand surrounded by a harem of women and alpha males who seek his counsel. His methods are an extreme expression of toxic masculinity, literally fueled by steroids, peptides, amphetamines, meth, ketamine, and other drugs. “Women have been dealing with these unhealthy, toxic beauty and health standards forever. And now it’s happening quickly with men, because you can’t go online without your insecurities being preyed on,” Jennings Brown , an executive producer on the series and the journalist who developed the show, told me. “These men are taking it to such a toxic, extreme level that they’re dying or having heart attacks in their twenties and thirties or other strange pulmonary-health issues, because they want to increase their SMV, their sexual market value.” Huge emerges as a modern-day Dr. Frankenstein, seemingly unable to understand the creatures he has spawned or to confront the destructive consequences of his experimental practices. In one particularly telling scene, he appears genuinely shocked by the extreme surgeries that Androgenic, the second most popular looksmaxxer, is planning to undergo while in Thailand. When some of the influencers in Huge’s orbit begin dying under mysterious circumstances, he seems incapable of processing their deaths or considering his own possible culpability. At times, the project can feel like a lurid dispatch from a world so detached from reality that it’s hard to see its relevance to the broader culture. But the influence of looksmaxxing on young men cannot be overstated. It has become enormously popular among adolescent boys in recent years, bringing with it very real risks to their physical and mental health. “Body dysmorphia is very popular now, and it’s kind of in everything. It’s affecting men in a way that it’s always affected women. We’re seeing it unfold in unusual ways that we aren’t really prepared for as a society,” Jennings told me. “It bleeds into everything.” If you have a suggestion for this column, please send it to laurelsanddarts@cjr.org . We can’t acknowledge all submissions, but we will mention you if we use your idea. For more on Laurels and Darts, please click here . To receive this and other CJR newsletters in your inbox, please click here . Has America ever needed a media defender more than now? Help us by joining CJR today . Susie Banikarim is an Emmy-winning journalist and recovering media executive. She is the director of the 2020 documentary Enemies of the People: Trump and the Political Press and cohosted the podcast In Retrospect . More from CJR Is It Crazy to Go to Journalism School? A student reflects on leaving the law to pursue his writing dreams. By Megan Greenwell   Crooked Con’s Hottest Takes Meeting up with podcasters, creator-journalists, and political operatives against a dominant blue backdrop. By Carolina Abbott Galvão   Misread Mandate Trump never had the support media moguls seemed to think he did. What’s next for the outlets that capitulated? By Jem Bartholomew   Political Journalists in Disarray Midterm missteps, a notable apology, and a sturgeon rescue. By Bill Grueskin   The voice of journalism, since 1961 About Mission Masthead Privacy Policy Contact Support CJR Become a Member Donate Advertise Contact Us Copyright 2026, Columbia Journalism Review",
