        "pageTitle": "A crisis mismanaged: Inside 57 days of tragedy and turmoil at Arizona State - The Athletic",
        "pageDescription": "After a season-opening workout left a teammate on life support, men&#x27;s hockey players lost trust in school leaders.",
        "evidenceText": "A crisis mismanaged: Inside 57 days of tragedy and turmoil at Arizona State - The Athletic Eamonn Dalton / The Athletic; Photos: USA TODAY Network; Zac BonDurant / Getty Images Investigation A crisis mismanaged: Inside 57 days of tragedy and turmoil at Arizona State By Katie Strang , Adam Kilgore and Nathan Fenno Nov. 12, 2026 Updated 1:19 pm EST Share article 214 On Aug. 20, sophomore Matthew Mayich, a 21-year-old from Stoney Creek, Ontario, collapsed at the conclusion of the Arizona State men’s hockey team’s first workout of the preseason. He suffered exertional heat stroke, his family’s lawyer said, and remains on life support with no chance of recovery. Arizona State cut ties with head coach Greg Powers nearly two months later. The days in between represented a crisis of leadership at the university, The Athletic found. Arizona State chose to investigate itself despite calls for external reviews and allowed Powers and his loyalists to remain in charge of the team. With players left to find their own paths, the locker room splintered, with some fearing retaliation if they didn’t support the coaching staff and others concerned they were betraying a fallen teammate if they did. Advertisement This account of how ASU leaders handled the aftermath of Mayich’s hospitalization is based on public documents and interviews with more than 20 people, many of whom spoke on the condition of anonymity because they feared repercussions on their futures, both at the school and in the sport. Arizona State president Michael Crow, athletic director Graham Rossini and senior vice president James Rund declined interview requests for this story; a university spokesman addressed some specific inquiries. In a statement, Powers’ attorney said, “As has been the case throughout this situation, Mr. Powers declines comment until the University has completed its review and the ongoing independent investigation is finalized.” Day 1 Tim Ziesel, the man Powers chose to run the first official workout of the preseason, stood before the ASU players. Powers had bemoaned publicly and privately that last season’s team was “soft.” Ziesel, a former Green Beret, was brought in for what Powers described as a team-building opportunity. No one submitted Ziesel’s workout plan for approval, but ASU had signed off on Ziesel meeting with the hockey team. Ziesel had suffered a traumatic brain injury in 2023, when a drunk driver collided with his motorcycle; in a victim-impact statement submitted in court, he and his wife explained how the incident had changed him. The 2024 statement said he “struggles to gather his thoughts and have organized, complex analysis” and “has emotional regulation difficulties” that cause him to get “angry at small things.” Advertisement Running the workout as coaches looked on, Ziesel told the players his 8-year-old son could perform the drills better. He told the players their generation “had lost what it meant to be masculine.” He repeatedly said he was “dangerous” and bragged about how quickly he could “enact violence.” Ziesel declined to comment through his attorney, who cited an ongoing criminal investigation. Players stumbled and vomited. Mayich struggled, then collapsed during the final activity. When the ambulance carrying Mayich left Sun Angel Stadium, Powers told players they were “closer to making the Frozen Four” because of the workout and Mayich having pushed himself “to the maximum.” Day 2 Two more days of workouts led by Ziesel were canceled, but the team practiced — a 4-on-4 scrimmage with coaches watching from the benches. Mayich was on life support, but Powers told the players he had received “good news”: Mayich’s heart and other organs were in good condition, leading players to believe he would be fine. That night, a group of players went out to a bar, eager to decompress after an emotional week, awash in relief after Powers’ update about Mayich’s health. Players were still talking about Ziesel’s antics, jokingly repeating the line he used during the workout: “I’m dangerous.” Day 3 The school had not acknowledged Mayich’s condition publicly. On its Instagram account, ASU’s hockey program boasted that five Sun Devils had signed NHL contracts since 2025. Advertisement An ASU spokesman later said the school has been following federal privacy laws and respected Mayich’s family’s privacy throughout this time. Meanwhile, Crow emailed the Arizona Board of Regents with the subject line, “Student Athlete Health Alert.” He told the regents who oversee ASU that a hockey player had a medical emergency at 8:30 a.m. two days prior. “He has not recovered and is under intense ICU care. Reviews of the circumstances are underway. I will let you know how things progress.” That night, players gathered at an apartment complex for a meeting called by one of the team’s veterans, who delivered the news that Mayich no longer had brain activity and was not expected to recover. Some players started crying. Powers attended the meeting. He embraced players, kissed some on their foreheads and told them there was no playbook for what the team was experiencing. Among the last things he said: “I don’t want you to worry about me.” After the meeting, Powers texted some players individually to say how much he cared about them. Day 6 In media reports, Mayich was identified publicly for the first time as the player who collapsed. Matthew Mayich was selected by the St. Louis Blues with the 170th overall pick in the 2023 NHL draft. (Bruce Bennett / Getty Images) Day 9 Rossini, a baseball team manager while an ASU student and whose only college administrative experience has been at the school, visited the Arizona Sports 98.7 FM studio for an interview with the “Burns & Gambo” show. In the first public remarks he or any ASU leader would make about the situation involving Mayich, he made a clear distinction about how the university planned to look into the incident: “This is not an investigation. We’re collecting information. If there’s things we need to identify and learn from, we’re going to do that. But this is not an investigation where somebody is suggesting something went wrong.” Advertisement Arizona State tabbed Rund to lead the “review.” Rund previously oversaw ASU’s club sports, hockey among them. When a donor pledged the money to take ASU to Division I in 2014, Powers’ first call was to Rund, who secured a meeting with ASU athletic department officials. An ASU spokesman later said there is no conflict of interest between Rund giving ongoing advice and assistance to Rossini and leading the review. Ziesel’s representative, Buddy Rake, gave an interview to an NBC affiliate in Phoenix. Rake said: “There isn’t a D1 athlete on the planet that would describe this as a hard day of exercising, including the ASU hockey team.” His comments echoed the narrative Powers’ supporters were floating to media members and others in the hockey community: That the workout hadn’t been that difficult, and that players had ample access to water and rest. Day 12 Rob Carey, an attorney for the Mayich family, told The Athletic that ASU “seems to be circling the wagons” at the expense of uncovering what had happened. He requested Powers and his staff be placed on leave. “It’s very clear to me that this is a time when adults have to do the right thing — sometimes the right thing is the hard thing,” Carey said. “And from everything I can tell and the leads we have, ASU is not handling this responsibly.” Carey sent document preservation letters to ASU and Powers, instructing them not to destroy or delete any evidence relating to the Aug. 20 workout. In the locker room, players were divided, with some agreeing with Carey’s position that Powers and his staff needed to be put on leave and others defending the coach. Day 14 The Athletic reported that the ASU Police Department had opened an investigation of the events surrounding the workout. The school characterized this as standard operating procedure for a serious injury incident and declined to answer questions about who initiated the probe or how it would differ from the Rund-led “review.” Advertisement Several players were concerned. If they participated in Rund’s review or talked to police and said something critical about the way the workout was run or the handling of the matter, would what they said get back to Powers, who maintained control of their athletic careers? Rossini met with the players for the first time since the workout. He spoke briefly, telling players he was there for them and had been since the event. Said one player: “He then left almost as quickly as he got there.” An ASU spokesman later said Rossini and members of ASU student services visited Mayich in the hospital, including a visit the night he collapsed and in subsequent days. Day 15 Teammates gathered to support Mayich and his family during a vigil at the hospital. No members of the coaching staff were present. Day 16 Carey wrote a letter to ASU asking the school to refer the ASUPD investigation to the Phoenix Police Department, the Arizona Attorney General’s office or the Arizona Department of Public Safety to ensure independence. Rossini emailed ASU hockey parents, addressing a group that had been feeling largely in the dark. “I’m committed to working together with you through this and learning not only what happened but working to make sure this never happens again,” he wrote. Graham Rossini has served as the athletic director at his alma mater since 2024. (Mark J. Rebilas / Imagn Images) Day 17 Arizona State’s hockey program publicly acknowledged Mayich by name for the first time with an X post on its hockey account. Replies were turned off. Advertisement Day 20 In its nightly media report to the Arizona Board of Regents, ASU noted that interest in the controversy was fading: “Hockey questions still trickling in every day, but at a slower pace.” Powers and his staff appeared to have support from Rossini as they continued to oversee practices. But as Rund and the ASU police department began to contact players, many were conflicted. Some felt increasing pressure — from peers, from members of the coaching staff — to say nothing was improper about the workout despite feeling differently. An ASU spokesman said recently that Powers and his staff remained in place to prepare players for their season, support players’ well-being and allow time to gather more information regarding Mayich’s collapse. Day 25 Crow, the university president, departed for London ahead of the opening of a satellite campus there and the Sun Devils’ football team playing in the Union Jack Classic at Wembley Stadium. He commemorated the moment with a social media photo of his luggage. Day 29 The Athletic published a story detailing the workout that contradicted Powers’ version. The story also described Powers’ treatment of players last season, which included calling them “soft” and “pussies” and disparaging them in text messages and in signs posted in the facility. Day 30 Like Crow, Rossini kept his plans to travel overseas with the football team. Seeking a way to get school leaders’ attention, some players discussed sharing concerns they had about the coaching staff directly with Crow. Advertisement Transfer forward Daniel Shlaine, whose brother previously played for Powers, was quoted in College Hockey News saying the workout “wasn’t physically exhausting, more mentally exhausting.” Another player echoed his support of Powers anonymously in the story. The divide within the team deepened. Day 34 Arizona State released the initial findings of Rund’s review. It cited policy violations that included a failure to submit Ziesel’s workout to the strength and conditioning staff for approval and not following the school’s heat-safety procedures. But the initial report held no one responsible and offered only one actionable promise: the creation of a “blue-ribbon advisory panel” to give “recommendations” for future outdoor activities and the use of outside consultants. The report repeatedly misspelled Ziesel’s name as “Zeisel.” Carey called the review “mind-boggling.” Some players began to discuss among themselves what they had told police or university officials about the workout. They noticed that some of those details — including the lack of water for some teammates, including Mayich — were not included in Rund’s report, which angered and frustrated them. Later that night, Arizona’s Family Sports television network published a video from the workout showing several players who appeared to be in distress and in direct sunlight. Mayich could be seen struggling, at one point crumpling into the grass. Advertisement Day 35 Powers spoke publicly for the first time since the workout. In a virtual news conference as part of the National College Hockey Conference’s media day, he refused to answer several questions about the workout and touted ASU as “still a great place” for prospective players. His session included an exchange with a reporter from The Athletic : “In talking to players that have played for you previously, they said that one of the things that has been a guiding principle and philosophy for you is accountability. How have you modeled accountability for your team over the past month?” “In a lot of ways,” Powers said. “Can you expand on that?” “No. Not today.” Day 36 Powers texted contacts in the hockey community to let them know he had a new phone number. He told ASU that he had been receiving death threats and harassing phone calls. Day 39 At least seven players met with attorney Tim Casey at a local hotel to share concerns about the coaching staff and sign attestations about what took place on Aug. 20. Michael Crow has been president of Arizona State University since 2002. (Patrick T. Fallon / AFP via Getty Images) Day 40 Seven players, who remained anonymous, sent a letter through Casey to Crow asking him to place Powers and his staff on leave. The players disputed several findings from ASU’s initial report on the workout, saying Mayich was not allowed time to drink water and received inadequate medical care after collapsing. They also said they feared retribution from the coaching staff. Advertisement The letter, which portrayed the school’s “review” as a whitewash, prompted two state legislators, including Rep. Matt Gress, to call for an independent investigation. Arizona Board of Regents chair Doug Goodyear forwarded Gress’ letter and the players’ report to regent Lee Stein, a high-profile Phoenix attorney. “I’d like to talk with you about this tomorrow,” Goodyear wrote to Stein. Day 41 Team members, still practicing daily under Powers and his staff, were on edge after the letter became public. A players-only meeting in the locker room grew contentious as some team members demanded to know who signed the letter to Crow, bemoaning the lack of trust it created. One player told the team he believed the Mayich family did not want any adverse action taken against Powers and his staff. In response, the family later released a statement: “(The) coaches who ran that workout on August 20 need to be removed while an independent investigation gets to the truth. The players shouldn’t have to worry about retaliation from the people they report to every day.” Day 42 Arizona State placed Powers on paid administrative leave . Rossini issued a statement that did not mention the letter to Crow. “After thorough and careful deliberation, I believe this is the best move to provide stability for the hockey program,” the statement said. Rossini named assistant Mike Corbett the temporary head coach. After that announcement, The Athletic reported that Corbett had been charged with driving under the influence twice, including a few months after starting at ASU in 2024. He pleaded guilty to a misdemeanor count of driving under the influence but kept his job. Asked by The Athletic when the school became aware of that DUI conviction or another that Corbett had in 2006, the school declined to answer. Alex Hicks — a longtime Powers confidant whom Carey referred to as Powers’ “proxy” — and Jimmy Mullin, Powers’ other assistant, also remained with the program. Advertisement Meanwhile, Arizona’s two U.S. Senators, Ruben Gallego and Mark Kelly, issued statements calling for an independent investigation of the workout. Day 43 Rossini went on Arizona Sports 98.7 FM to explain placing Powers on leave two days before the season opener. He said he had done so to limit “distractions” for players. Rossini told the station he would hire an interim coach to replace Powers, a key distinction. Players can enter the transfer portal midseason only if their program hires a new permanent head coach. If the school instead names an interim coach, they are not permitted to transfer until the offseason. Crow met in closed session with the Arizona Board of Regents as calls mounted for ASU to take action. Day 44 Ceding to mounting pressure, Arizona State retained former Arizona Supreme Court Chief Justice Scott Bales to review what happened to Mayich. Even that announcement sparked confusion. The school communicated the development by releasing a letter from Rund to Crow that described Bales’ duties as reviewing policies and procedures related to outdoor athletic activities and the use of outside consultants. Carey quickly blasted the move as inadequate: “ASU has finally agreed to bring in someone from outside. It took six weeks, two U.S. senators, legislative committee chairs, and the Board of Regents to get there, and ASU has again failed to do what is necessary.” That night, the Sun Devils opened their season with a victory at Lindenwood. Advertisement Day 45 One of the attorneys representing the Mayich family, Leonard Aragon, alleged in a letter to ASU that Powers had replaced his cell phone and “the timing and circumstances raise serious concerns that relevant evidence has been destroyed.” The attorney asked ASU to confirm the phone hadn’t been wiped or discarded, and that its data was preserved. An ASU spokesperson later said Powers never changed phones, only his number, and that content from his phone was downloaded to ASU’s Office of the General Counsel. Day 49 Rossini’s decision to retain Powers’ coaching staff did not alleviate players’ concerns about retaliation. Hicks, the assistant coach and Powers’ confidant, glared at players and stared them down at practice. Multiple people made university officials, including Rossini, aware of Hicks’ behavior. Day 50 Hicks left the program. The school would not say whether he was placed on leave or quit. Hicks did not respond to multiple messages seeking comment. Day 51 The Sun Devils lost their home opener to Holy Cross, 2-1, with Corbett serving as ASU’s temporary coach. “People can say what they want to say about the coaching staff or me personally and dig up things and that’s their prerogative to do that,” Corbett said in the postgame news conference. “We hurt, too. We are human beings, too. We were there, too, and we hurt.” Day 56 Powers’ nearly two-decade run leading ASU’s hockey program came to an end. He signed a separation agreement that will pay him 75 percent of the reported five-year, $2.5 million deal he signed in 2022, as well as two bonuses totaling $85,000. Advertisement The agreement requires Powers to cooperate with the school in any legal matter, including providing records and appearing for depositions or trials. Day 57 Dave Hakstol was announced as the new head coach of the Sun Devils. Corbett was not retained, leaving Jimmy Mullin as Powers’ lone remaining assistant. Though Rossini had told players he’d hire an interim coach, Hakstol was named as ASU’s permanent coach. By then, most players had appeared in at least one game. Players can play for only one school during a given season. Hakstol flew to Massachusetts, where Rossini introduced him to players at a team breakfast. They would beat UMass-Lowell the next night, 5-4. In an interview with the Sun Devi",
